Content: The DIVINE study examined the effects of high-dose therapy with FA (40 mg/day), vitamin B12 (1000 mg/day), and vitamin B6 (2 mg/day) in patients with diabetic nephropathy. It showed that this treatment regimen does not increase survival or slow the progression of ESRD. Instead, it leads to a higher incidence of cardiovascular events and a greater decrease in the eGFR. However, such results may be influenced by its suboptimal study design, such as the lack of division of patients with CKD and [...] In healthy adults, vitamin B12 deficiency is extremely rare. Even if a healthy person consumed insufficient amounts of vitamin B12 for a period of 3 years, he would not have inadequate levels of this vitamin. However, these data do not necessarily apply to patients with CKD because there are no data on the amount of vitamin B12 stored in the body in patients with this disease . Therefore, one can only rely on the suggestion that patients with CKD undergoing hemodialysis respond positively after [...] mortality and a 9% increase in the risk of cardiovascular disease. Hcy levels in these patients appear to have increased from 13 to 31 μmol/L due to supplementation with B vitamins in intervention studies. This was associated with a 27% reduction in the risk of cardiovascular disease, although mortality did not decrease . At the same time, Heinz’s randomized, double-blind, multicenter study showed that the increased intake of folic acid, vitamin B12, and vitamin B6 did not reduce overall [...] The role of folic acid and vitamin B12 supplementation in reducing mortality and preventing progression to ESRD has not yet been determined [8,19]. According to a meta-analysis by Heinz et al., Hcy emerged as a risk factor for cardiovascular disease and mortality in ESRD, particularly in those who do not receive supplemental FA. Prospective studies have shown that in patients with ESRD, an increase in Hcy concentration of 5 μmol/L is associated with a 7% increase in the risk of 