Title: Mainstreaming mental health in developing countries - SciELO México
URL: http://www.scielo.org.mx/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0036-36342018000200024
Content: In most countries, the social response to this huge challenge is dolefully inadequate. Millions of people suffering from mental illness do not seek help due to lack of services and/or stigma. Indeed, the gaps in treatment are appalling. Around 80% of people with severe mental disorders in low- and middle-income countries receive no treatment.11 The corresponding figure for developed nations is around 40%-still an unacceptable proportion. In the face of such gaps, families bear the brunt of [...] The low availability and quality of treatment are a reflection of the lack of resources. As mentioned before, mental disorders concentrate 7.4% of the global burden of disease. Yet, on average, only 2% of national health budgets is devoted to mental health programs.7 In low-income countries, expenditure in mental health is less than 25 cents per person per year, while in high-income countries is US$ 44.8 per capita. [...] This picture is part of a larger epidemiologic transition characterized by the rise of chronic problems. The rapid pace of change has led to the growing importance of non-communicable diseases or NCDs, which are now the main causes of death in most countries, producing 38 million deaths annually, 28 million of which occur in low- and middle-income countries.9 [...] Concerning human resources for mental health, half of the population of the world lives in a country where, on average, there is less than one psychiatrist per 100 000 population (figure 2).6 In contrast, the supply of psychiatrists is 8.6 per 100 000 population in high-income countries.



Source: World Health Organization. Mental Health Atlas 20117

Figure 2 Median rate of psychiatrists in low, lower-middle, upp er-middle, and high income countries, 2011 [...] As these brief epidemiological account illustrates, mental disorders represent a major challenge. In our rapidly changing world, mental disorders demand an immediate and far-reaching global response, which initially means mobilizing additional resources for mental health, but also implementing a different strategy to address them. The new approach should aim at mainstreaming mental disorders, which means dealing with them within the frame of the overall health system.
