tention to [...] countries were inadequate global supply capacity of new vaccines and insufficient domestic financing (to create a viable market for, and fund the purchases of newer more expensive vaccines) which manifested in unaffordable prices. In this regard, the Alliance was primarily set up to address between-country inequities, between the poorest and wealthier countries, and to increase the number of people worldwide benefiting from vaccination [42:690–3."")–44].Footnote 8
