Title: Homocysteine levels: What it means, symptoms, treatment
URL: https://www.medicalnewstoday.com/articles/homocysteine-levels
Content: One of the more common causes of high homocysteine is a B6, B12, or folate deficiency. This is because the body needs these nutrients to break down homocysteine. A lack of them can lead to a buildup of homocysteine in the blood.

Some people are more at risk of deficiencies in these vitamins than others. This includes people with:

Some medications can also cause folate deficiency, including methotrexate, metformin, and oral contraceptive pills. [...] Research associates consistently high homocysteine levels with an increased risk of cardiovascular disease, cancer, and several other conditions. Anyone concerned about their homocysteine levels and what they mean should speak with a doctor.

 

### How we reviewed this article:

Share this article

## Latest news

### Related Coverage

Folate is an essential nutrient. A lack of dietary folate can cause anemia. Learn about the causes, symptoms, treatment, and prevention of folate… [...] A 2019 review also associates hyperhomocysteinemia and homocysteinuria with cancer.

## Diagnosing high homocysteine levels

A doctor may recommend a blood test for homocysteine levels if the person has symptoms of vitamin B6, B12, or folate deficiency. They may also run the test if the person already has risk factors for heart disease.

If a person’s test results show high levels of homocysteine, a doctor may need to run additional tests to determine the underlying cause. [...] ## Symptoms of high homocysteine

Hyperhomocysteinemia itself does not have any symptoms. However, if it develops due to a deficiency in vitamins B6, B12, or folate, a person may experience symptoms of a vitamin deficiency, including:

## Causes of high homocysteine levels [...] A doctor may recommend a homocysteine level test if someone shows signs of a vitamin B6, B12, or folate deficiency or if they are at increased risk of heart disease, stroke, or heart attacks.

To perform the test, a doctor will take a blood sample and order a test that separating red blood cells from plasma.

If a doctor has the necessary equipment, testing may take place in their office. Alternatively, they may require someone to visit a laboratory with the facilities to do this.
