Graphic adapted from “Potential factors result in diminished ovarian reserve: a comprehensive review”, Zhu et al. 2023, Journal of Ovarian Research. [...] generally lead to a higher success rate. [...] Clinically, ovarian reserve testing is used to predict how well you may respond to ovarian stimulation during an egg retrieval.² This is an important marker for success in IVF and egg freezing treatments. In general, the more eggs you retrieve in a cycle, the more opportunities you will have to create viable embryos. Of course, a single high-quality egg is better than many low-quality eggs, but statistically speaking, having a higher reserve will improve your overall chance of success.
