Image 29 [...] Laboratory studies include the following [1;), 43;)] :

    Hemoglobin level - Within the reference range  
    Reticulocyte count - Normal  
    Mean corpuscular volume (MCV) - 75-85 fL  
    Mean corpuscular hemoglobin (MCH) - Around 26 pg  

### Alpha thalassemia trait

Laboratory studies include the following:

    Hemoglobin level - Within the reference range  
    Reticulocyte count - Normal  
    MCV - 65-75 fL  
    MCH - Around 22 pg  

### Hemoglobin H (HbH) disease [...] Alpha thalassemia is frequently mistaken for iron deficiency anemia because both disorders have microcytic red blood cells. Iron therapy is not required for alpha thalassemia, and the procedures used to find a source of bleeding in patients with iron deficiency anemia have no value in patients with thalassemia. Measurements of serum iron and ferritin can exclude iron deficiency anemia; otherwise, unnecessary supplemental iron therapy for an extended period in alpha thalassemia syndrome, and the [...] Hb Bart is elevated at birth in patients with alpha thalassemia. In persons with HbH disease, 15-30% of total hemoglobin is Hb Bart, along with typical findings of adult hemoglobin A, hemoglobin A2, and hemoglobin F. In silent carriers, however, the percentage is only 1-2%, with low or normal amounts of hemoglobin A2. [44;)] In persons with alpha thalassemia trait, Hb Bart accounts for over 5-6% of total hemoglobin. [...] Laboratory studies include the following:

    Hemoglobin level - 7-10 g/dL  
    Reticulocyte count - 5-10% (the higher the reticulocyte count, the more severe the hemolysis)  
    MCV - 55-65 fL  
    MCH - 20 pg  
    Peripheral blood smear - Small misshapen red cells, hypochromia, microcytosis, and target cells  
    Brilliant cresyl blue stain - HbH inclusion bodies  

