Content: example, prenatally seeded macrophages in the intestines begin to be replaced by HSC-derived macrophages (via a monocyte intermediate) soon after birth, with a majority of adult intestinal macrophages being postnatally-seeded BMDMs . Other tissues eventually contain a mix of prenatally seeded YS-derived and postnatally-seeded BMDMs, notably the lung and skeletal muscles (Figure 2C) [75,81,82]. The fate mapping models that have enabled these discoveries are clearly powerful but are limited by [...] on the exact origins of very well-studied embryonic macrophage subsets, such as the microglia. Although most reports agree that microglia are seeded primarily by first-wave progenitors [6,68,74,76], some authors do not exclude a contribution by second-wave EMPs [75,77,78]. Regardless, it is agreed that microglia are prenatally seeded by YS-derived progenitors and persist throughout the mouse's life . However, this is not necessarily the case for other tissue-resident macrophage populations. For [...] Once macrophages are seeded in their tissues, they begin to contribute to organogenesis during development. Much of our understanding of the important role of macrophages in organogenesis comes from transgenic mouse models that allow temporal- or tissue-specific depletion of macrophages _._ When embryonic macrophages are depleted via genes such as _Csf1r, Csf1, Cx3cr1, Ccr2, Pu.1, Dap12,_ and _CR3,_ developmental defects are clearly observed in several organs, illustrating how tissue-resident [...] Differentiated YS-derived macrophages are first detected in the body and circulation of the embryo by E9–9.5 [70,74,75]. The seeding of organs by YS-derived macrophages during embryonic development is guided, at least in part, by CX3C motif chemokine receptor 1 (CX3CR1) signaling, but the exact contributions of first-wave primitive progenitors versus second-wave EMPs to tissue-resident macrophage populations to different tissues are not definitively known [70,74]. Questions are still open even [...] macrophages [95, 96, 97]. Considering the multiple roles embryonic macrophages have in normal hematopoiesis, their involvement in malignant hematopoiesis seems to be plausible both before and after birth. The discovery of a leukemia-associated macrophage population in embryos and/or newborn pups and the characterization of their crosstalk with leukemia cells may identify new therapeutic targets for infant leukemia.
