Content: In mice and humans, the skin harbors a complex network of mononuclear phagocytes, including dermal macrophages and Langerhans cells (LCs). LCs have long been classified as dendritic cells (DCs); however, efforts to understand their ontogeny using fate-mapping approaches in mice have established that they are seeded before birth, similarly to microglia in the central nervous system (CNS) and thus they have a completely different origin from conventional DCs . Situated mainly in the [...] Tissue-macrophage biology has generated renewed interest in recent years, particularly querying how the tissue-macrophage compartment is established during mammalian development. Indeed, it is now well-established that most tissue-resident macrophage (M TR) populations in mice and humans develop in the fetus and are maintained independently from adult hematopoiesis in the steady state , although an analysis of M TR ontogeny in many human organs remains limited. While microglia in the brain [...] ### Cell (2014) 
