### C-Myb(+) erythro-myeloid progenitor-derived fetal monocytes give rise to adult tissue-resident macrophages [...] Several macrophage populations have been described in the CNS in the mouse and humans. These include perivascular, subdural meningeal, dura mater, and choroid plexus macrophages, but most prominently, microglia . In terms of their ontogeny, microglia, perivascular, and subdural meningeal macrophages all derive exclusively from embryonic precursors [63., 64., 65.], while choroid plexus macrophages are more dependent on adult hematopoiesis and BM monocytes . These known macrophage subsets [...] In the lung in mice and humans, there are three major populations of lung macrophages, including AM and two distinct lineages of IM. AM are localized to the alveolar space, while CD206+Lyve high MHCII low IM are found in the bronchial interstitium in the vicinity of blood vessels and CD206- Lyve-1 low MHCII high IM are present in the alveolar interstitium and peribronchial next to nerves [and are therefore also called nerve associated macrophages (NAM)] [44., 45., 46.]. AM develop perinatally
