## Conclusion

Certain medications can trigger migraine or make migraine attacks worse. These can include drugs meant to help with chest pain, sexual dysfunction, acid reflux, nasal congestion, and other conditions like depression and anxiety. Hormone therapy can also be a culprit in triggering attacks for some people with migraine, as can pain meds or certain migraine medications themselves when used at higher frequencies. [...] Understanding that some drugs can cause headaches and impact migraine can help you be on the lookout for a potential correlation between your medications and worsening of your migraine. If you identify a change, review your medications with your healthcare provider to explore whether an adjustment could help with migraine relief.

Based on an interview with Dr. Charles and edited by Migraine Again staff.
