Here’s how antidepressants may help:

That said, antidepressants’ side effects and effectiveness vary depending on the medication and person. Consult with a healthcare professional if you have any questions or concerns. [...] The short answer — potentially yes. SSRIs may trigger or worsen migraine as a potential side effect. An antidepressant headache may manifest when an individual starts an SSRI or changes their dosage. That said, not everyone experiences this SSRI migraine side effect.

The two medications may interact in the following ways: [...] Discover How CEFALY Works to Prevent & Treat Migraine Learn More →

CEFALY
Migraine and SSRIs

# Migraine and SSRIs

Living with migraine and conditions like anxiety or depression can feel overwhelming — especially when you’re taking medications for both. If you’ve been prescribed antidepressants, you may wonder how these two conditions — and their treatments — interact. Can SSRIs trigger migraine or make it worse? Or, could they actually help alleviate migraine symptoms? [...] Understanding the relationship between the two may help you better manage migraine and SSRIs more effectively with your healthcare provider.

## What are SSRIs?

Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) are a type of antidepressant medication that helps individuals suffering from depression. They’re also used to help treat anxiety and other mood disorders. To know how they work, we need to understand serotonin’s role. [...] SSRIs block this reuptake process. So, instead of getting reabsorbed too quickly, serotonin stays in the space between your brain cells — known as the synapse — for longer. More serotonin translates to better moods and regulated emotions. However, the process is not as fast as it may sound. It usually takes a few weeks for SSRIs to build up enough to result in any noticeable mood changes.
