Content: Description The Bloom syndrome is an autosomal recessive disorder characterized by growth deficiency, microcephaly and immunodeficiency among others. It is caused by homozygous or compound heterozygous mutation in the gene encoding DNA helicase RecQ protein on chromosome 15q26. This Bloom-associated helicase unwinds a variety of DNA substrates including Holliday junction, and is involved in several pathways contributing to the maintenance of genome stability. Identification of pathogenic Bloom [...] Summary Bloom syndrome protein (BLM) is a RecQ helicase that plays a central role in maintaining genome integrity through multiple, coordinated activities. Biochemical and structural studies have demonstrated that BLM, often acting in concert with telomere‐associated proteins such as TRF2, promotes proper telomere replication and stability while stimulating its helicase activity to resolve complex DNA structures that arise during replication.1 Characterization of a minimal helicase core further [...] Name Bloom syndrome, RecQ helicase-like [...] Patrick M Grierson, Samir Acharya, Joanna Groden Collaborating functions of BLM and DNA topoisomerase I in regulating human rDNA transcription._Mutat Res (2013)_ DOI: 10.1016/j.mrfmmm.2012.12.002 PMID: 23261817

