es glucose [...] CTLA-4 is a concern for SLE-related studies due to its inhibitory role in immune responses and control of hyperactive T and B cells, the mechanism of which may be through the interaction between auto-reactive B cells and CD4+ T cells and the CD28/CD80-86/CTLA-4 axis. Simulating the effect of CTLA-4 competing with CD28 for B7, anti-CD28 was used to block CD28 signaling in NZB/NZW mice, which prevented lupus nephritis development, prolonged animal survival, reduced production of against double
