Content: Under apoptotic stimuli, Cc is translocated from the IMM to the cytosol to act as a PCD inducer [23, 51]. Cytosolic Cc interacts with Apaf-1 to assemble the apoptosome . The N-terminal Apaf-1 consists of a caspase recruitment domain (CARD), a nucleotide-binding domain (NBD) that allows the binding of dATP/ATP [29, 30, 51], a helical domain (HD1), a winged helix domain (WHD), and a second HD2 . The C-terminal half of the protein contains 15 copies of the tryptophan-aspartic (WD) domain (also [...] Besides its established functions in cell respiration, Cc fulfills a role in the mitochondrial generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) induced by apoptotic signals, through its interaction with p66Shc , an adaptor protein participating in the signal transduction pathway that regulates oxidative stress . p66Shc oxidizes Cc in the intermembrane space, thus generating hydrogen peroxide with the subsequent opening of the mitochondrial permeability transition pore (MPTP) , swelling of [...] Cytochrome c (Cc) is a protein that functions as an electron carrier in the mitochondrial respiratory chain. However, Cc has moonlighting roles outside mitochondria driving the transition of apoptotic cells from life to death. When living cells are damaged, Cc escapes its natural mitochondrial environment and, once in the cytosol, it binds other proteins to form a complex named the apoptosome—a platform that triggers caspase activation and further leads to controlled cell dismantlement. Early [...] For many years, the biological function of Cc was assumed to be confined to the mitochondria and to rely on the electron transport capabilities. This dogma was shaken in 1996, when it was discovered that several cell lines treated with staurosporine, a well-known apoptotic inducer, released Cc from mitochondria into the cytosol . The authors also found that the addition of ATP to a cell-free extract resulted in typical apoptotic hallmarks, including caspase-3 activation, nuclear DNA [...] Several studies have shown that Cc nuclear translocation may also take place under nonapoptotic conditions. Cc release from the mitochondria is a gradual process, and apoptosis is only initiated once a cytoplasmic concentration threshold is reached . In addition, release of Cc into the cytoplasm can also occur without mitochondrial membrane permeabilization . In line with this observation, Cc abundantly localizes in the nucleus, while being scarcely present in the cytoplasm, prior to the onset
