Content: Discussion

In the present study we found that the proliferative activity of DUSP4-overexpressing cancer cells was significantly suppressed by downregulation of DUSP4, leading to promotion of apoptosis and cell cycle arrest at G2/M phase through activation of the p53 signaling pathway. These findings accord with those of Lawan et al. , who showed that cellular proliferation rates were significantly reduced in DUSP4-defient mouse embryonic fibroblasts and associated with blocking of G2/M phase [...] Dual-specificity phosphatase 4 (DUSP4), a MAP kinase phosphatase, has been regarded as a tumor suppressor gene in several cancers. However, high-level expression of DUSP4 is occasionally observed in specific cancers and its functional significance in carcinogenesis is not fully understood. In the present study, we showed that downregulation of DUSP4 suppressed the proliferation of cancer cell lines exhibiting high expression of DUSP4 by inducing apoptosis and cell cycle arrest at G2/M phase. [...] Furthermore, studies have revealed that sorafenib treatment significantly elevates the expression of dual-specificity phosphatase 4 (DUSP4), also known as MAPK phosphatase 2 (MKP2) (Chen et al., 2019). DUSP4 plays a crucial role in modulating cellular processes such as proliferation, differentiation, apoptosis, and cell cycle progression through interactions with the MAPK and p53 signaling pathways (Low and Zhang, 2016; Ratsada et al., 2020). However, the precise mechanism underlying [...] Dual-specificity phosphatase 4 (DUSP4) belongs to the DUSP family, which can specifically dephosphorylate and regulate the mitogen-activated protein (MAP) kinases, ERK1/2, p38 and JNK [1,2]. These kinases drive cellular proliferation, differentiation, and apoptosis. Therefore, aberrant expression of DUSP4 would impact the cellular phenotypes leading to carcinogenesis. A relationship between DUSP4 expression and cancer progression has been demonstrated regardless of gain or loss of DUSP4 [...] •Downregulation of DUSP4 leads to growth suppression in DUSP4-overexpressing cancer cells, independently of MAP kinase. 
   •Cell cycle arrest and apoptosis are induced by downregulation of DUSP4. 
   •Activation of p53 signaling pathway is involved in the growth suppression by downregulation of DUSP4. 

