","Title: Association analysis of blood pressure with incident risks for thoracic ...
URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12382572/
Content: #### Figure 2. Associations between systolic blood pressure (SBP), diastolic blood pressure (DBP), pulse pressure (PP) and risk of abdominal aortic aneurysm or thoracic abdominal aneurysm. HR indicates the relative risk increases with per-SD higher blood pressure. Models were adjusted for assessment centres, age, sex, race, tertiles of deprivation index, smoking status, alcohol consumption frequency, BMI, genetic diseases, HbA1c, cholesterol and HDL-C. For PP and DBP, the models were [...] evidence suggests DBP may be more strongly associated with AA risk. The UK CALIBER cohort reported a 45% increase in AAA risk per 10 mm Hg increase in DBP, compared with only an 8% increase per 20 mm Hg increase in SBP.6 However, other large cohorts in middle-aged populations found similar HRs for SBP and DBP.7 8 Evidence for PP remains particularly scarce and inconsistent: CALIBER reported an inverse association between PP and AA risk, while most studies omit PP entirely.6 Moreover, the [...] As shown in figure 1, SBP was not significantly associated with AA in the multivariable regression model, with the per-SD HR=1.03 (95% CI 0.98 to 1.08, p=0.283). The risk of AA arose when the DBP level was over approximately 80 mm Hg. On average, per-SD higher baseline DBP was associated with 24% higher AA risk (HR=1.24, 95% CI 1.15 to 1.33, p<0.001). By contrast, smaller PP was associated with a higher risk of AA(figure 1, online supplemental file 1 figure S3). This association was independent [...] #### Figure 1. Associations between systolic blood pressure (SBP), diastolic blood pressure (DBP), pulse pressure (PP) and risk of aortic aneurysm. (A–C) Analyses among participants who did not take antihypertensive treatment at baseline. The per-SD HRs for SBP, DBP and PP are 1.03 (0.98, 1.08), 1.24 (1.15, 1.33) and 0.76 (0.69, 0.83), respectively. The grey legends in (D–F) indicated participants who did not take antihypertensive treatment at baseline; the other colours indicated groups taking [...] ### Conclusions

Elevated DBP and reduced PP, but not SBP, were independently associated with a higher risk of incident AA, highlighting their potential to refine clinical risk stratification.

Keywords: Aortic Aneurysm, Hypertension, Risk Factors
