Incomplete understanding of the etiology of vasospasm has hindered developing new treatment modalities. Although the pathogenesis of vasospasm after SAH is probably multifactorial, the potential imbalance between vasoconstricting and vasodilating influences on vascular tone in response to the presence of blood in the subarachnoid space almost certainly plays an integral role. [...] There is little doubt that oxyHb and/or deoxyHb (Macdonald & Weir, 1991, Pluta et al., 1997a, Pluta et al., 1997b, Asano, 1999), slowly released from erythrocytes after aneurysmal bleeding directly and/or indirectly (Pluta et al., 1996, Kranc et al., 2000, Clark et al., 2002, Wang et al., 2002), is responsible for cerebral vasospasm development (Macdonald & Weir, 1991). We have reported that when ferrous hemoglobins level peaks around day 7 after SAH (Pluta et al., 1997a, Pluta et al., 1997b),
