Content: NK1.1− CD4+NKG2D+T cells are a subpopulation of regulatory T cells that downregulate the functions of CD4+ T, CD8+ T, natural killer (NK) cells, and macrophages through TGF-β1 production. Early growth response genes 2 (Egr2) and 3 (Egr3) maintain immune homeostasis by modulating T lymphocyte development, inhibiting effector T cell function, and promoting the induction of regulatory T cells. Whether Egr2 and Egr3 directly regulate TGF-β1 transcription in NK1.1− CD4+NKG2D+T cells remains elusive. [...] Egr2 and Egr3 exert their activities by regulating other signaling pathways. For instance, Egr2 and Egr3 downregulate the activation STAT1 and STAT3 by inducing SOCS1 and SOCS3 expression in T and B cells . Egr2 and Egr3 modulate the expression of latent TGF-β binding protein-3 (Ltbp3) in CD4+ CD25− LAG3+ T cells [21,22,26]. Egr2 interacts with Batf to inhibit Th17 differentiation and IL-17 and IL-21 secretion in effector CD4+ T cells [19,27]. On the other hand, Egr2 and Egr3 themselves are [...] Early growth response (Egr) family is a group of zinc finger transcription factors with four isoforms, namely Egr1-4 [1,2], which are involved in immune regulation  and nervous system development . In general, Egr2 and Egr3 play opposing effects with Egr1 in immune cell development and function . Egr2 and Egr3 regulate T cell selection and differentiation in thymus [13,14], and induce T cell anergy in the periphery [15,16]. Egr2 and Egr3 inhibit effector T cell activities by downregulating the [...] ### J. Biol. Chem. (2015) 
