Title: Eilat virus displays a narrow mosquito vector range
URL: https://parasitesandvectors.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13071-014-0595-2
Content: The genus Alphavirus in the family Togaviridae is comprised mostly of arthropod-borne viruses that utilize mosquitoes as vectors for transmission to diverse vertebrate hosts including equids, birds, amphibians, reptiles, rodents, pigs, humans, and non-human primates . Alphaviruses also have a broad mosquito host range and can infect many species encompassing at least eight genera (Aedes, Culex, Anopheles, Culiseta, Haemagogus, Mansonia, Verrallina and Psorophora spp.) -[6 to infection with [...] Mosquito-borne alphaviruses are maintained in nature in enzootic or endemic cycles between susceptible mosquito vectors and vertebrate hosts. In this two-host cycle, adaptive virus evolution may be constrained by trade-offs required for efficient infection of widely divergent hosts [29: 6970-6975. 10.1073/pnas.0712130105."")]-[34: e1156-10.1371/journal.pntd.0001156."")]. However, EILV is restricted at both attachment/entry and RNA replication stages of infection in vertebrate cells, and is [...] EILV was isolated from a pool of A. coustani s.I. in a survey of the Negev desert in Israel during 1982–1984 [47: 471-472. 10.1016/0035-9203(86)90348-2."")]. Its apparent lack of a vertebrate host suggests that A. coustani s.I. may be the only mosquito species responsible for natural maintenance of EILV. If true, EILV would be only the second alphavirus described that utilizes Anopheles spp. as a natural vector. The other is o’nyong-nyong virus (ONNV), an African alphavirus transmitted to humans [...] epizootic (subtype IC) and enzootic (subtype ID) Venezuelan Equine encephalitis viruses. J Med Entomol. 2005, 42 (5): 857-863. 10.1603/0022-2585(2005)042[0857:SOPCDC]2.0.CO;2."")]. Recently, a newly characterized alphavirus, Eilat virus (EILV), isolated from a pool of Anopheles coustani s.I. mosquitoes was described [7: 14622-14627. 10.1073/pnas.1204787109."")]. EILV is unable to infect and replicate in vertebrate cell lines but can readily replicate in insect cells [7: 14622-14627. [...] 10.1073/pnas.1204787109."")]. The vertebrate host restriction is present at both attachment/entry as well as genomic RNA replication levels [7: 14622-14627. 10.1073/pnas.1204787109."")],. EILV is the first “insect-only” alphavirus described and represents a new complex within the genus [7: 14622-14627. 10.1073/pnas.1204787109."")],.
