Title: Alzheimer's genes: Are you at risk? - Mayo Clinic
URL: https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/alzheimers-disease/in-depth/alzheimers-genes/art-20046552
Content: _Several genes have been linked to Alzheimer's disease, but more research is needed._By Mayo Clinic Staff Certain genes increase the risk of getting Alzheimer's disease. Other genes can increase the risk of getting certain diseases, including Alzheimer's disease. Researchers have found several genes that are linked to Alzheimer's disease. The most common gene linked to late-onset Alzheimer's disease is a risk gene called apolipoprotein E, also known as APOE. Several other genes have been linked to late-onset Alzheimer's disease. Rare variants of this gene are linked to an increased risk of Alzheimer's disease. Researchers suspect they still haven't found many of the genes that affect the risk of Alzheimer's disease. Researchers also are studying gene variations that may protect against Alzheimer's disease.
