Title: Apolipoprotein E Effects on Mammalian Ovarian Steroidogenesis ...
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1043276020301351
Content: Apolipoprotein E (ApoE) is a glycoprotein consisting of 299 amino acids, highly produced in the mammalian ovaries. The main function of the ApoE is to transport cholesterol from the peripheral tissues to be metabolized in the liver. In humans, the ApoE gene is polymorphic, with three alleles in a single chromosome-19 locus: _APOE2_, _APOE3_, and _APOE4_. ApoE has also been implicated in cholesterol transport within ovarian follicles to regulate steroidogenesis. Ovarian thecal and granulosa cell [...] The carriage of ApoE4, one of the ApoE isoforms, has been associated with increased risk for developing cardiovascular and late-onset Alzheimer’s diseases. However, increased fertility and cognitive protection have been found in individuals bearing the _APOE4_ allele and living in adverse environments and afflicted with heavy enteric infections and parasitic burdens. [...] _APOE_ Alleles and Fertility of Women [...] Important questions arise as to whether _APOE4_ acts as an antagonistic pleiotropic allele, over the lifespan, affecting ovarian follicular growth and viability and overall women’s reproductive function in disenfranchised environments. More studies are needed to unravel the fine mechanisms modulating cell growth signaling pathways in the mammalian ovary under distinct environmental conditions and ApoE isoforms. [...] In order to address the effect of _APOE4_ on women’s fertility in adverse environments, it is interesting to revisit Malthusian thinking. Thomas Malthus (1776–1834), in his essay on the _Principle of Population_ of 1798, stated that population growth occurs exponentially while food production increases in arithmetic progression, hence famine, food scarcity, and poverty would control population overgrowth . Yet Malthus, in his own later writings, noted that if there were ever the possibility for
