","Title: FOXK2 Gene - Ma'ayan Laboratory, Computational Systems Biology
URL: https://maayanlab.cloud/Harmonizome/gene/FOXK2
Content: Summary FOXK2 is a multifunctional forkhead transcription factor that plays diverse roles in regulating gene expression, cellular metabolism, proliferation, and oncogenic signaling. In one context, FOXK2 interacts with Dishevelled proteins to promote their nuclear translocation, thereby positively modulating the Wnt/β‐catenin signaling pathway.1 In muscle cells and fibroblasts, FOXK2—together with its closely related homolog FOXK1—functions as a transcriptional repressor of autophagy by [...] recruiting Sin3A–HDAC complexes to limit histone H4 acetylation and suppress the expression of key autophagy genes, balancing the stimulatory effects of FOXO3.2 Moreover, FOXK2 forms part of a regulatory complex by interacting with the deubiquitinase BAP1 and the chromatin cofactor HCF‑1, thereby repressing target gene transcription and counteracting the activity of ubiquitin ligases such as Ring1B–Bmi1.3 In liver cells, FOXK2 is responsive to insulin signaling; upon activation via the Akt–mTOR [...] Christopher John Bowman, Donald E Ayer, Brian David Dynlacht Foxk proteins repress the initiation of starvation-induced atrophy and autophagy programs._Nat Cell Biol (2014)_ DOI: 10.1038/ncb3062 PMID: 25402684

 Yuki Okino, Yuka Machida, Sarah Frankland-Searby, et al. BRCA1-associated protein 1 (BAP1) deubiquitinase antagonizes the ubiquitin-mediated activation of FoxK2 target genes._J Biol Chem (2015)_ DOI: 10.1074/jbc.M114.609834 PMID: 25451922 [...] cascade, it translocates from the cytoplasm to the nucleus where it helps regulate genes involved in lipid metabolism, cell cycle progression, and mitochondrial function.4 Its activity is further modulated in a cell cycle–dependent manner; FOXK2 becomes hyperphosphorylated by cyclin-dependent kinases (primarily CDK1–cyclin B) in mitosis, and phosphorylation at specific serine residues (S368 and S423) is critical for maintaining its stability and repressor function—loss of these sites can [...] trigger apoptosis.5 In hepatocellular carcinoma, overexpression of FOXK2 has been linked to enhanced cell growth and migration through activation of the PI3K/AKT signaling cascade, with high FOXK2 levels correlating with poorer overall and disease‐free survival.6 In addition, FOXK2 cooperates with the AP‑1 transcription factor to facilitate efficient chromatin recruitment and gene activation in response to extracellular signals.7 In breast cancer cells, FOXK2 also acts as a negative regulator
