Title: Stomach Cancer Prevention
URL: https://winshipcancer.emory.edu/cancer-types-and-treatments/stomach-cancer/prevention.php
Content: Bacterial infection.Having a long-term _Helicobacter pylori_(_pylori_) bacterial infection is a leading risk factor of stomach cancer. But most infections are treatable, and the overwhelming majority of people who have an H. pylori infection never develop stomach cancer.
   Obesity. Being overweight increases a person’s risk for stomach cancer. [...] Having a gastric ulcer in the stomach caused by an H. pylori infection can increase a person’s risk of developing stomach cancer. In any event, having a stomach ulcer does not mean you will develop stomach cancer. Advances in ulcer management over the years has greatly improved stomach cancer incidence.

### Recommendations for Preventing Stomach Cancer [...] As we continue to learn more about stomach cancer and its causes, the following stomach cancer prevention recommendations may help you reduce your risk:

   Not using any tobacco products
   Not drinking alcohol
   Maintaining a healthy body weight (e.g. body mass index below 25)
   Being physically active every day
   Eating a diet rich in fruits and vegetables (particularly citrus fruits) and low in processed meats
   Seeking treatment for an H. pylori infection [...] Diet. Risk of stomach cancer is increased by eating a lot of certain foods, such as processed or charcoaled meats, and food preserved by salting, such as pickled meat.
   Alcohol use.People who drink three or more alcoholic beverages a day are at increased risk for stomach cancer.
   Smoking. People who smoke have about twice the risk for stomach cancer than nonsmokers. [...] While researchers are still investigating exactly what causes stomach cancer, there has been progress in understanding risk factors and making some recommendations for stomach cancer prevention.

### Stomach Cancer Risk Factors

The following stomach cancer risk factors can influence your chances of developing a type of stomach cancer called adenocarcinoma, which develops in the inner lining of the stomach. Adenocarcinomas account for up to 95% of stomach cancers.
