Title: Therapeutic Exercise for the Shoulder - Physiopedia
URL: https://www.physio-pedia.com/Therapeutic_Exercise_for_the_Shoulder
Content: Consensus on dosage, frequency, method of delivery, acceptable pain tolerance, inter-exercise activity levels, and specific exercise inclusion has not been achieved.. Despite this, studies have shown that shoulder-specific exercise is more effective than general exercises for managing shoulder conditions. Optimal parameters of exercise and load have yet to be determined, and the mechanism by which therapeutic response occurs remains unknown . [...] Treatment of shoulder conditions through conservative management aims to reduce pain and improve function, with exercise rehabilitation usually being the cornerstone of this conservative management plan. Exercise works to correct modifiable physical impairments thought to contribute to pain and dysfunction, rather than to treat the pathology. Therapeutic exercise is commonly used in the treatment and management of a range of shoulder disorders, is commonly advocated to address dysfunctions in [...] Both group and individual physiotherapy interventions which incorporate exercise aim to reduce pain and disability, but a consensus is a lack of evidence for the most effective mode of treatment delivery for people for exercise rehabilitation in musculoskeletal pain. Although it is clear that group and individual physiotherapy interventions that incorporate exercise are better than minimal or no treatment, it remains unclear whether either is better than the other. O'Keeffe et al, in a broad [...] study of exercise for musculoskeletal pain, found only small, clinically irrelevant differences in pain or disability outcomes between group and individual physiotherapy, and concluded that group interventions may need to be considered more often given their similar effectiveness and potentially lower healthcare costs. Specifically to the shoulder, recent evidence suggests that group exercise classes can improve shoulder pain and disability in people with non-specific shoulder pain . [...] ## The Evidence[edit | edit source]

Exercise has a useful role to play and incorporating loaded exercises is safe and not detrimental to outcome . In rotator cuff tendinopathy, both home and supervised exercise programs have been found to be more effective than no intervention or placebo and as effective as minimal comparators, e.g. functional brace, or active comparators, e.g. multimodal physiotherapy, surgery .
