Title: The Functional Relationship Between RNA Splicing and the ...
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022283624002092
Content: There is evidence that pre-mRNA splicing is, for the most part, a co-transcriptional process.9, 10 Therefore, transcription elongation rate, chromatin structure and conformation, as well as histone post-translational modifications directly influence the outcome of the splicing process.

Mechanisms of pre-mRNA Splicing [...] The pre-mRNA splicing process itself is mediated by a complex and highly regulated molecular machinery known as the spliceosome. Formed by a complex of RNA and proteins (ribonucleoprotein) the spliceosome functions in a dynamic cycle of stepwise reactions in which the complex is assembled and disassembled for every single splicing reaction.11 The splicing process is not a “cut and paste” mechanism, but rather two consecutive S N 2-type transesterification reactions involving functional groups [...] Given the evidence of the co-transcriptional nature of pre-mRNA splicing, chromatin structure/organization and modifications, as well as chromatin-related factors, also have an important influence on the splicing process. Two non-mutually exclusive mechanisms by which chromatin can influence splicing have been proposed: the “speed bump” (kinetic) model suggests that nucleosome positioning and density can regulate Pol II elongation rate by acting as physical barriers, favouring exon definition [...] H3K4me3 is another histone mark related to transcriptional activation and it is enriched at the 5′ end of genes near promoters. The first evidence of a functional link between H3K4me3 and pre-mRNA splicing was that siRNA-mediated knockdown of Ash2 (a subunit of the COMPASS/Set1 complex specific for H3K4me3) or CHD1 (a reader protein of H3K4me3 and chromatin remodeller) results in a reduction in the association of the U2 snRNP component SF3a with chromatin and reduced pre-mRNA splicing [...] Modulation of both cis- and _trans_-acting splicing factors can also affect chromatin structure and composition. Binding to the 5′ SS of pre-mRNA by the U1 snRNP can affect chromatin and overexpression of splicing factors SRSF1 and SRSF2 led to nucleosome depletion, whereas overexpression of hnRNP A1 increased nucleosome occupancy.135 The Hu family of RNA-binding proteins (HuA/R, HuB, HuC, and HuD) are a group of splicing regulators that interact with splicing factors to regulate exon
