Title: New couples-based intervention looks to improve HIV health ...
URL: https://sph.unc.edu/sph-news/new-couples-based-intervention-looks-to-improve-hiv-health-outcomes-in-malawi/
Content: This study showed that a couple-based program improved several HIV outcomes from HIV diagnosis of male partners to viral suppression in both partners (which means the virus is undetectable in an individual’s blood). This adds to a growing body of evidence on the importance of couple-based approaches, rather than individualized care.

“The message is simple,” said Rosenberg. “A couple learning their HIV status together is more valuable than each individual learning their HIV status separately.” [...] To tackle these low rates and promote improved health outcomes, Nora Rosenberg, PhD, associate professor of health behavior at the UNC Gillings School of Global Public Health, and her team developed and tested a couples-based HIV program as outlined in a recent article published in the Journal of Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome. [...] UNC Gillings logo

# New couples-based intervention looks to improve HIV health outcomes in Malawi

April 25, 2025

By: Alana D. Austin, Public Health Communications Fellow [...] In the couples-based program, both members of a couple receive HIV treatment counseling together. Located in the country of Malawi, this intervention, known as Timasamalirana, consists of a Malawian couple, specifically a pregnant woman living with HIV and her male partner, typically her husband. The sessions helped couples learn about each other’s HIV status, understand the implications of their status for the family and strategize on how to support each other with critical HIV prevention and [...] Access to HIV treatment (or antiretroviral therapy (ART)) during pregnancy and breastfeeding is now nearly universal in sub-Saharan Africa (SSA) and has led to significant improvements in maternal health and HIV transmission among partners. Despite this, pregnant women and their male partners living with HIV in SSA are not achieving the recommended targets for HIV testing, treatment retention and viral suppression.

Nora E. Rosenberg, PhD, MSPH

Dr. Nora Rosenberg

Dr. Nora Rosenberg
