Title: Main Partner Relationships and the HIV Care Cascade: Examining ...
URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11112277/
Content: Somewhat surprisingly, studies of dyadic coping with HIV have given limited attention to partner serostatus (e.g., [14, 16, 18], and findings regarding the influence of partner serostatus on coping with HIV have been mixed. Drawing on interdependence theory, at least two potential associations might be plausible. It is conceivable that SMM living with HIV would benefit most from a relationship with a serodiscordant partner. In this case, the presence of an HIV-negative main partner would [...] provide ongoing motivation for linkage to care and ART adherence in order to reduce the risk of transmission to the main partner. It is also conceivable that SMM living with HIV would benefit most from a relationship with a seroconcordant partner. In this case, partners might be best positioned to empathize with one another and engage in some aspects of coping together (coordinating medical visits or taking their medication at the same time). [...] The purpose of the current study was to test the hypothesis that both sexual agreements (i.e., monogamous, nonmonogamous) and partner serostatus (i.e., seroconcordant, serodiscordant) would be significantly associated with HIV care cascade outcomes (i.e., ART prescription, ART adherence, VL testing, and VL detectability). Specifically, we hypothesized that having a nonmonogamous agreement and having a serodiscordant partner would be positively associated with having a current ART prescription [...] medication reminders, organizing medications, attending doctor visits, scheduling appointments, communicating with providers, monitoring labs, problem-solving adherence concerns, and providing empathy and emotional support [13–18]. In related work, perceived emotional or instrumental support from one’s partner has been associated with greater engagement in HIV treatment and care [13, 15–17]. A complementary body of evidence suggests that romantic relationship characteristics, such as [...] There is reason to believe that any association between relationship status and HIV care outcomes would vary by sexual agreement. Existing evidence indicates that sexual agreements contextualize associations between relationship status and drug use. SMM in monogamous relationships are less likely to use a wide range of illicit drugs (e.g., cocaine/crack, methamphetamine, gamma-hydroxybutyrate, ketamine, and ecstasy) compared with SMM in nonmonogamous relationships and those who are single
Score: 0.7621591


Title: Primary Relationships, HIV Treatment Adherence, and Virologic ...
URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3607313/
Content: Studies have identified the presence of a primary partner as a fundamental factor in maintaining health . Married men have been found to have a greater likelihood of survival over time than their unmarried counterparts, after controlling for risk indicators , and the hypothesized mechanisms by which partnerships influence health behaviors and outcomes are varied [12, 24]. Better health outcomes have been linked to higher levels of primary relationship communication, intimacy, and satisfaction. [...] are linked to the patient’s adherence in the expected directions. That is, the partner’s more positive beliefs of treatment efficacy and fewer concerns about general medication overuse or harm were associated with the patient’s better adherence. Although never previously documented in the context of couples and HIV treatment, this finding parallels findings by Doherty, in which a male patient’s adherence to a coronary health treatment plan was predicted by his wife’s beliefs about treatment [...] , and varying age groups, treatment regimens, and measurement strategies, there is evidence of robust correlations between practical and emotional support and treatment adherence . In the context of HIV infection, social support has been linked to better adjustment [14–19], better treatment adherence , and slower progression to AIDS [13, 18, 21, 22]. The purpose of this study is to present findings from an analysis of couples in which we investigated relationship-based predictors of adherence [...] Our sole finding related to the patient’s viral load was that the greater the level of relationship commitment endorsed by the partner, the more likely it was that the patient had an undetectable viral load and thus was responding well to treatment. It is intriguing that none of the variables related to depressive symptoms, treatment beliefs, regimen complexity nor the patient’s own appraisal of the couple’s relationship quality were linked to virologic control outcomes. Because the study was [...] While psychoneuroimmunologic work has documented direct physiological and immunologic effects from stressful dyadic interactions [29, 30], influences on treatment adherence by primary partners and spouses is likely a driving factor in the associations between relationship status and health among persons with medical conditions requiring long-term treatment. Among patients with coronary heart disease, those who were married or had a partner were more likely to attend cardiac rehabilitation than
Score: 0.75355774


Title: Couple-Based Intervention to Improve HIV Care Engagement for ...
URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11773527/
Content: We conducted an initial study to test whether a behavioral treatment for couples with the main goal of improving the HIV care needs of women, could also improve the HIV-related needs of male partners. Adult women with HIV who had difficulties with HIV treatment adherence in the past year and who were in a monogamous relationship with a male partner for at least six months were recruited from HIV clinics in KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa. Twenty couples were randomly assigned (1:1) to either START [...] percentage points in START Together, but not treatment as usual. Findings suggest that START Together may be potentially beneficial for improving HIV outcomes for men. [...] Although there is some local evidence of assortative mating for HIV status in South Africa21—that people with HIV are more likely to be partnered with someone who is also living with HIV—romantic partners may still be at different points along the HIV care cascade. Couple-based interventions can therefore also be designed in such a way to address the differing HIV-related needs of both partners within a single intervention, including HIV prevention if one partner is not living with HIV.22 If [...] One approach garnering support in high HIV-burden settings is to work with couples, rather than individuals, to improve HIV treatment outcomes.3–7 This approach has proven efficacious in improving HIV testing,3,4 preventing vertical transmission of HIV,5 and reducing sexual risk behaviors6,7 in South Africa specifically, as well as in high- and low-income settings more broadly. Various couple-based interventions that center on HIV prevention have also been adapted and evaluated in the South [...] partner support buffered the negative association of stigma on ART adherence.16 At the same time, the dyadic context can also be harmful to HIV care if partners have conflicting or incorrect information about HIV treatment.19 Couples who learned about their HIV status jointly in Malawi reported being better able to support each other in subsequent health decisions.20 Couple-based interventions targeting ART adherence may therefore be an appealing intervention in this context, increasing social
