Content: We hypothesize that having a partner at the time of diagnosis reduces the emotional burden of the new diagnosis leading to better coping mechanisms, continuous retention in HIV care, and quicker and sustained viral suppression (SVS). A supportive partner has shown to improve HIV care outcomes in the MSM population 27-29 . Similar findings have also been observed in heterosexual couples in some studies in Africa 30-32 .However, knowledge of this phenomenon in heterosexual individuals is limited [...] The overall goal of this study was to evaluate HIV-related outcomes among partnered (unmarried-partnered and married) and un-partnered heterosexual individuals newly diagnosed with HIV and to explore how having a partner impacts HIV care around the time of HIV diagnosis and following the diagnosis in this population. The central hypothesis was that having a partner at the time of diagnosis reduces the emotional burden of the new diagnosis leading to better coping mechanisms, continuous [...] Previous findings on the role of an intimate partnership on HIV care outcomes have been mixed; while some show having a partner to be beneficial, others have shown a negative impact 30,31,40 . Studies that date back to the 1990s studying HIV in heterosexual couples in the US mostly reported negative impacts including new HIV diagnoses damaging the partnership 37-39 . However, with 1) increased knowledge of HIV in the general public 44 , 2) improvements in ART regimens which have increased the [...] In the US, where recent studies are available exploring the association between having a partner and HIV-related health outcomes in the MSM population, those involving heterosexual couples date back more than 20 years that mostly reported negative impacts of a new HIV diagnoses on the relationship 37-39 . While some African studies exploring the impact of partnership on HIV care outcomes in heterosexual couples have shown partnership to be helpful, the majority of the study populations were [...] Netherlands of predominantly men (90%) living with HIV (heterosexual, homosexual, and bisexual) found that married individuals experienced less depression than their unmarried counterparts 20 . Another study in women living with HIV linked depression driven by loneliness with sub-optimal ART adherence 4 . These findings indirectly suggest that having a partner may be helpful in achieving desired HIV health outcomes. Applying the concept of the interdependence model, in addition to reduced
