URL: https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/circresaha.121.319077
Content: Genetic Studies Support a Potential Causal Role of IL-6 Signaling in Atherosclerosis

Beyond prospective epidemiological studies demonstrating that IL-6 is elevated years in advance of atherothrombotic events, genetic data supporting a potential causal role for IL-6 signaling in atherosclerosis come from 3 study types, genome-wide association studies, Mendelian randomization analyses, and phenome-wide association studies. [...] As with systemic atherosclerosis, it has long been hypothesized that inflammation plays a major role in heart failure.3,93 In mice, IL-6 blockade through genetic deletion associates with decreased cardiac hypertrophy and fibrosis following angiotensin II stimulation.94 In humans, consistent with early data in the primary prevention of atherothrombotic events, biomarkers of inflammation including hsCRP, IL-6, and GDF15 predict incident heart failure, worsening outcomes among those with prevalent [...] Early experimental data reported that exogenous IL-6 enhanced the development of fatty lesions in ApoE1−/− mice.26 More recent murine work indicates that trans-signaling of IL-6 through pg130 contributes to atherosclerosis27,28 and that IL-6 destabilizes atherosclerotic plaque.29 On the other hand, in double knockout ApoE−/−–IL-6−/− mice, lifetime deficiency of IL-6 may enhance atherosclerotic plaque formation30 consistent with a dual-modulatory role for IL-6 function, which suggests that [...] Figure 6. Genetic data suggest a potential causal role for IL (interleukin)-6 in atherothrombosis. Genome-wide association study implicates _IL6R_ and _NLRP3_ as genetic loci determining plasma levels of hsCRP (high-sensitivity C-reactive protein) (upper left); phenome-wide association study implicates IL-6 signaling in coronary and peripheral atherosclerosis, as well as abdominal and aortic aneurysm formation (lower left); Mendelian randomization study indicates that genetic mediators of IL-6 [...] contrast, genetic and experimental data suggest that IL-1b and IL-6 (the upstream drivers of hepatic CRP production) do appear causally related to atherosclerotic disease, yet require research laboratories for their assessment.
