Title: Coronary artery disease predisposing haplogroup I of the Y ...
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S002191501300765X
Content: Carried by approximately 1 in 5 men of European ancestry, haplogroup I is one of the most common Y chromosome lineages in Europe. There is a clear gradient in the European distribution of haplogroup I; it is most common amongst men in Northern Europe (up to 40% male population in Scandinavia) , where CAD-related morbidity and mortality is the highest. Men who inherit haplogroup I from their fathers have approximately 50% higher risk of developing CAD when compared to carriers of other Y [...] Amongst middle-aged men, haplogroup I is associated with ≈50% higher risk of coronary artery disease than other paternal lineages of Y chromosome. We hypothesised that carriers of haplogroup I had higher levels of aggression and estrogens and/or lower levels of androgens early in life and thus might be more prone to cardiovascular disease than men with other lineages of Y chromosome.

### Methods [...] ### Results

Approximately 17% men inherited haplogroup I from their fathers. Carriers of haplogroup I showed lower scores of verbal aggression than men with other haplogroups (_β_=−0.72, SE=0.29, _P_=0.012) and when further compared to carriers of most common R1a lineage and other haplogroups (_β_=−1.03, SE=0.34, _P_=0.003). However, these associations did not survive a correction for multiple testing. Sex steroids did not show even nominal level of association with haplogroup I. [...] inflammation demonstrated by transcriptome profiling in macrophages from men with haplogroup I may fully explain their increased susceptibility to CAD . Amongst additional biologically relevant mechanisms that may account for this association, perhaps the most intuitive are highly heritable, phenotypes strongly linked to male gender (such as aggressive behaviour and/or androgens). Indeed, being competitive and aggressive is the central component of the “masculine” personality characteristics [...] disease . Indeed, our previous study revealed that one of the most ancient paternal lineages of the Y (haplogroup I) is associated with ≈50% increase in risk of CAD when compared to other lineages of the Y chromosome .
Score: 0.7339103


Title: Y-Chromosome Variant May Raise Heart Disease Risk | MDedge
URL: https://community.the-hospitalist.org/content/y-chromosome-variant-may-raise-heart-disease-risk
Content: To test the hypothesis that genetic variation in the Y chromosome influences cardiovascular risk, Dr. Samani and his coworkers turned first to the British Heart Foundation Family Heart Study. In a cross-sectional sample that comprised 811 men with CAD and 633 controls, the investigators showed that the I-haplogroup was associated with a 67% increased relative risk of CAD, compared with men whose Y chromosome did not contain the I-haplogroup. [...] To test the hypothesis that genetic variation in the Y chromosome influences cardiovascular risk, Dr. Samani and his coworkers turned first to the British Heart Foundation Family Heart Study. In a cross-sectional sample that comprised 811 men with CAD and 633 controls, the investigators showed that the I-haplogroup was associated with a 67% increased relative risk of CAD, compared with men whose Y chromosome did not contain the I-haplogroup. [...] To test the hypothesis that genetic variation in the Y chromosome influences cardiovascular risk, Dr. Samani and his coworkers turned first to the British Heart Foundation Family Heart Study. In a cross-sectional sample that comprised 811 men with CAD and 633 controls, the investigators showed that the I-haplogroup was associated with a 67% increased relative risk of CAD, compared with men whose Y chromosome did not contain the I-haplogroup. [...] The I-haplogroup is thought to have been introduced into Europe by the Gravettian culture, which migrated from the Middle East roughly 25,000 years ago. The I-haplogroup is carried by about 15% of British men. However, the prevalence exceeds 60% among men in parts of Scandinavia and Eastern Europe. [...] The I-haplogroup is thought to have been introduced into Europe by the Gravettian culture, which migrated from the Middle East roughly 25,000 years ago. The I-haplogroup is carried by about 15% of British men. However, the prevalence exceeds 60% among men in parts of Scandinavia and Eastern Europe.
