Content: Immune checkpoint therapies targeting cytotoxic T lymphocyte–associated antigen 4 (CTLA-4) and PD-1 have been extensively studied and utilized as cancer treatments. Studies have demonstrated that anti-CTLA-4 and anti-PD-1 therapies can restore T cell cytotoxic function and induce an antitumor response (1–4). In patients with melanoma, anti-CTLA-4 treatment has shown a 22% 10-y overall survival rate, with some patients experiencing durable responses lasting 15 y or more (2, 5). Anti-PD-1 [...] We have previously demonstrated that anti-CTLA-4 or anti-PD-1 leads to the expansion of terminally differentiated CD8 effector T cells, with anti-CTLA-4 specifically inducing the expansion of Th-1-like ICOS+ CD4 T cells (24). To investigate the role of CD8 and CD4 T cells in the memory responses generated by anti-CTLA-4 or anti-PD-1, we depleted CD8 or CD4 T cells separately in mice receiving B16F10 cancer vaccine and antibody treatments. After 80 d, when both T cell populations had recovered [...] To investigate whether exhausted T cells are generated after priming in our acute vaccination model, we assessed the expression of Eomes and PD-1, which are associated with terminally differentiated phenotypes during memory stage and rechallenge (13, 26–28). No significant difference was observed between the two treatment groups (_SI Appendix_, Fig. S5 _A_–_C_). Our results indicate that our model does not promote T cell exhaustion and the activation of memory T cells is comparable between the [...] depleted (Fig. 1_H_). Taken together, our findings indicate that anti-CTLA-4 or anti-PD-1 rely on CD8 T cells for memory response, but the memory response generated by anti-CTLA-4 is predominantly dependent on CD4 T cells as well. [...] naïve T cells into memory precursors, effectors, and exhausted precursors during activation (14–16). Our studies show that CTLA-4 blockade results in the expansion of CD8 T cells with higher levels of TCF-1 and lower levels of TOX, while PD-1 blockade results in the opposite pattern. Additionally, our findings demonstrate that CD4 T cells are required for the generation of CD8 T cell memory. These data provide molecular insight into the mechanisms of immunologic memory generated by immune
