URL: https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/03007995.2024.2318002
Content: Recent registry data from Sweden reported improved clinical outcomes with β-blockers in a cohort of about 11,000 patients with HFmrEF who received a β-blocker and were followed for an average of 2.8 yearsCitation 48. Data from the US PINNACLE registry found a significant association between β-blocker use and reduced HF morbidity and mortality in a cohort of 75,674 people with HFmrEFCitation 49. Analysis of the DELIVER trial (an evaluation of dapagliflozin vs. placebo in patients with HF and [...] improve HF symptoms and functional status in HF and these agents have demonstrated improved survival, as well as a reduced risk of other important clinical outcomes such as hospitalisation for heart failure, in randomised, placebo-controlled outcomes trials. In HFpEF, β-blockers are anti-ischemic and lower blood pressure and heart rate. Moreover, β-blockers also reduce mortality in the setting of HF occurring alongside common comorbid conditions, such as diabetes, CKD (of any severity), and [...] COPD. Higher doses of β-blockers are associated with better clinical outcomes in populations with HF, so that ensuring adequate titration of therapy to their maximal (or maximally tolerated) doses is important for ensuring optimal outcomes for people with HF. In principle, a patient with HF could have combined treatment with a β-blocker, renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system inhibitor/neprilysin inhibitor, mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, and a SGLT2 inhibitor, according to tolerability. [...] β-blockers are established within the evidence-based care for HFrEF, due to their beneficial effects on clinical cardiovascular outcomes, as described above. Accordingly, patients should be (a) taking β-blockers, and (b) taking them at an effective dose: in practice neither appears to happen as often as it should, as described below. [...] β-blockade to manage these conditions. A recent (2023) meta-analysis of 10 randomised studies conducted in populations with HFpEF reported significant decreases in all-cause mortality and hospitalisation for HF; meta-regression analysis suggested that the risks of these outcomes were 45% and 73% lower, respectively, for β-blocker vs. calcium channel blockers (CCB)Citation 55. This needs to be confirmed in a dedicated clinical trial, especially as the results of other meta-analyses have been
