Title: Beta Blockers in Heart Failure Management
URL: https://www.thecardiologyadvisor.com/features/beta-blockers-in-heart-failure/
Content: For patients with HFrEF, beta blockers can boost clinical status by improving left ventricular function as well as reducing symptoms of heart failure, and the risk of hospitalization and mortality.2 Optimal treatment with beta blockers requires a thorough understanding of their mechanism of action, indications, adverse effects, and use in patients with common comorbidities.

## Beta Blocker Mechanism of Action in Heart Failure [...] Beta blockers are the mainstay heart failure treatment for patients with an LVEF of up to 40%, as seen in HFrEF.2 High-quality evidence from multiple RCTs indicates that beta blockers can boost clinical status by improving left ventricular function and reducing symptoms of heart failure, and the risk of hospitalization and mortality.2 In recent guidelines for managing heart failure, it is recommend that all patients receive treatment with 1 of the 3 beta blockers shown to reduce mortality: [...] High-quality evidence from multiple randomized controlled trials (RCTs) indicates that beta blockers can improve left ventricular function and heart failure symptoms.2 Unless contraindicated or not tolerated, beta blockers should be initiated as soon as an HFrEF diagnosis is established, and continued long-term, even in patients who are asymptomatic, have mild symptoms, have symptoms that do not improve, or have symptoms that improve with other treatments.2 [...] analysis of a trial that included 1376 patients with heart failure, researchers found that treatment with beta blockers was associated with reduced rates of all-cause mortality.11 In another analysis of a registry that included 958 patients with HFrEF and comorbid atrial fibrillation, researchers found that at 10-year follow-up, patients treated with beta blockers had more favorable outcomes.12 [...] During heart failure, chronic β-adrenergic receptor activation leads to excess stimulation of catecholamines, eventually decreasing norepinephrine levels and leading to myocardial apoptosis and fibrosis.4 By blocking β-adrenergic receptors, beta blockers impede adrenergic activation on the heart, thereby decreasing heart rate, blood pressure, and cardiac output.4
