Title: Beta blockers linked to reduced 1-Year mortality in critically ...
URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-17483-3
Content: patients with CHF. This aligned with findings from a recent study28.""). It found that β blockers therapy demonstrated benefits in reducing in-hospital mortality and hospitalization duration in ICU patients with CHF28.""). Nevertheless, our research distinguished it by concerning the long-term clinical outcomes but not in-hospital mortality, and utilizing an updated MIMIC database, a larger sample size, and more sophisticated statistical methodologies particularly including OW and IPTW. In this [...] In this extensive cohort, the use of β blockers was associated with reduced 1-year all-cause mortality in critically ill patients with CHF. This benefit was particularly pronounced in subgroups characterized by younger age, male gender, hypertension, diabetes, sepsis, and those undergoing CRRT or mechanical ventilation, as well as in patients exhibiting elevated baseline heart rates or reduced LVEF. Sensitivity analyses adjusting for all baseline variables further supported these findings. [...] for enhancing clinical outcomes30.""). In HFrEF patients, reductions in heart rate following β blockers were associated with a lower incidence of adverse events31.""). Changes in heart rate held significant prognostic value in patients with atrial fibrillation, particularly those presenting with higher baseline heart rates31.""). Furthermore, patients with reduced LVEF levels often demonstrated a more pronounced response to β blockers therapy11.""),32.""). Meanwhile, from control subjects to [...] patients11.""),12."")and the PRESERVE-HR trial suggested that β blockers may enhance exercise tolerance in individuals with HFpEF13.""). In contrast, the DELIVER trial, which included over 80% of participants on β blockers, demonstrated the reduced heart failure events and cardiovascular mortality14.""). A recent systematic review reported decreased all-cause mortality with β blockers therapy in HFpEF, though readmission rates remained unchanged15: A systematic review and meta-analysis. Curr. [...] ventilation, has been associated with improved outcomes, suggesting that the pharmacological benefits may extend to those with acute conditions, where β blockers could help stabilize hemodynamics and reduce stress responses. Moreover, the distinctive interactive RCS analysis indicated that patients exhibiting higher baseline heart rates and reduced LVEF derive greater benefit from β blockers therapy. β blockers was particularly effective in HFrEF patients, where managing heart rate was crucial
