Content: Beta-blocker treatment, introduced in heart failure therapy by Waagstein et al. in 1975 , showed significant clinical benefits in adults with myocardial failure as shown in large, prospective, randomized studies ,  and a small trial in children with cardiomyopathies . Recently, we reported about the favorable preliminary results of β-blocker treatment in six infants with severe congestive heart failure due to left-to-right shunts . Consequently, we performed the prospective, randomized [...] _Aim:_ Infants with congenital heart disease and left-to-right shunts may develop significant clinical symptoms of congestive heart failure in spite of therapy with digoxin and diuretics. We investigated the effects of β-blockade in infants with severe heart failure. _Methods and results:_ We performed a prospective, randomized, open monocenter trial in infants treated with digoxin and diuretics (_n_=10) in comparison to 10 infants receiving additional β-blocker therapy. After 17 days on [...] average β-blocker treated infants (propranolol:1,6 mg/kg/day) improved significantly with respect to Ross heart failure score (3.3±2.3 vs. 8.3±1.9, _P_=0.002), lower renin levels (338±236 vs. 704±490 μU/l, _P_=0.008) and lower mean heart rates in Holter ECG (118±10 vs. 142±11 beats/min, _P_<0.001). While digoxin and diuretic treated infants had unchanged mean heart rate (149±8 vs. 148±10 beats/min), less decrease of symptoms (Ross Score: 8.5±1.7 vs. 6.8±2.3, _P_=0.02) but a significant increase [...] Introduction

The prevalence of heart failure in patients with congenital heart disease is as high as 20%, but prospective clinical studies of the medical treatment are limited. Moreover the small, most of all retrospective studies showed contradictory results for digitalis , , ,  and angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitors , . [...] CHF-PRO-INFANT (Congestive heart failure in infants treated with propranolol) trial in order to confirm the effects of β-blockade with propranolol on clinical signs of heart failure, neurohormonal activation and hemodynamics. This paper focuses on the clinical, laboratory and noninvasively obtained hemodynamic data during the 1st month of treatment while dosage was titrated.
