# Pharmacists on Rounding Teams Reduce Adverse Drug Events

Laurie Barclay, MD

September 23, 2003

Sept. 22, 2003 — Having a pharmacist on the rounding team of a general medical unit reduced adverse drug events (ADEs) by 78%, according to the results of a controlled study published in the Sept. 22 issue of the Archives of Internal Medicine. [...] ""Fatal ADEs were the sixth leading cause of death in the US in 1994, with 10.9% of all hospital patients experiencing some adverse drug reaction and 2.1% of admissions resulting in serious events,"" write Suzan N. Kucukarslan, PhD, from the Henry Ford Hospital in Detroit, Michigan, and colleagues. ""Having a pharmacist available when patients are evaluated during the rounding process may reduce the likelihood of preventable ADEs."" [...] Study limitations include lack of baseline data for the intervention and control groups, nonrandomized design, and restriction to patients admitted to the general medicine unit.

""In most practice settings, the pharmacist is placed distant from the medication selection step of the patient care process,"" the authors write. ""Pharmacist participation with the medical rounding team on a general medicine unit contributes to a significant reduction in preventable ADEs."" [...] In this single-blind, standard-care controlled study, including a pharmacist with the rounding team on general medical units reduced the rate of preventable ADEs by 78%, from 26.5 per 1,000 hospital days to 5.7 per 1,000 hospital days. Of 150 documented interventions recommended by the pharmacist, 147 were accepted by the team. Interventions recommended most frequently were dosing-related changes and suggestions to add a drug to ongoing therapy. [...] Arch Intern Med. 2003;163:2014-2018

Reviewed by Gary D. Vogin, MD

Medscape Medical News © 2003 

