Content: lipoprotein (LDL) but with the addition of glycoprotein apolipoprotein(a) [apo(a)] that bears evolutionary similarity to plasminogen and is attached to apolipoprotein B-100 of the LDL particle (Figure 1) covalently. Although Lp(a) shares a similar apo(B) component with LDL particles, the LDL receptor does not appear to play a significant role in Lp(a) clearance. The exact site and pathway of Lp(a) catabolism remain unknown, but it is believed that the kidney may be involved in Lp(a) clearance.6 [...] Bile acid sequestrants, infrequently used as adjunct therapy with statins, can lower LDL-C by up to 25% by upregulating LDL receptors on the hepatocyte membrane as a compensatory mechanism in response to the interruption of enterohepatic circulation of bile acids.13, 29 A nested case–control study found a non-significant 20% increase in Lp(a) with cholestyramine, whereas another small cross-over study involving 19 patients observed no correlation with Lp(a) levels.62, 63 Limited evidence exists [...] PCSK9 is an enzyme produced by the liver that binds to the LDL receptor and facilitates the degradation of LDL receptors; therefore, therapies that reduce the functionality or synthesis of PCSK9 result in increased availability of LDL receptors and removal of LDL-C from the circulation.29 Inhibition of PCSK9 using mAbs (alirocumab, evolocumab) reduces LDL-C levels by approximately 60% and ASCVD risk in patients with clinical ASCVD.43, 44 Although the mechanism of PCSK9-mediated LDL-C lowering [...] A Mendelian Randomization study involving over 80,000 patients and 150,000 controls demonstrated that the risk of coronary heart disease was directly proportional to the absolute change in plasma Lp(a) mass concentration. The study determined that a reduction of approximately 100 mg/dL in Lp(a) concentration is likely needed to achieve potential benefits in cardiovascular risk reduction.72 This supports the need for therapies capable of more significant reductions in Lp(a) than what is [...] populations. Despite the current evidence from observational and genetic studies supporting the hypothesis that reducing Lp(a) in patients with elevated levels may decrease cardiovascular risk, it is important to note that causality has not yet been established in randomized controlled trials.
