Content: Several approaches have been shown to reduce plasma levels of Lp(a), including lipid-lowering drugs originally developed to decrease LDL cholesterol levels and compounds specifically targeting Lp(a) (Figure 2). Among the latter, several classes of drugs have been developed, characterized by different mechanisms of action and pharmacokinetic features (Table 1). Drugs targeting Lp(a) are rapidly emerging as an important breakthrough in cardiovascular prevention, with many compounds tested for [...] Lp(a) is a LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol variant, codified by the _LPA_ gene and biosynthesized in the liver, containing apo B 100 (apolipoprotein B 100) covalently linked to apoA (apolipoprotein A) (Figure 1).12 ApoA encircles a lipoprotein somewhat like ordinary LDL, producing an outer hydrophilic shell composed of multiple tridimensional heavy-chain highly glycosylated loops of 80 to 90 amino acids (kringles), characterized by 2 types of domains (kringle IV [KIV] and kringle [...] Although no drug of this class has been developed yet to directly target Lp(a), post hoc analyses of trials of monoclonal antibodies targeting PCSK9 have surprisingly shown a reduction in Lp(a) levels through mechanisms that have not been fully elucidated. These drugs bind to the PCSK9 receptor, preventing its interaction with the hepatic LDL receptor, whose recycling is promoted, leading to upregulation and higher clearance of LDL particles.64 In addition, inhibiting PCSK9 indirectly [...] PCSK9 (ie, alirocumab and evolocumab) prevent the degradation of the LDL receptor, increasing its expression on the cell surface, therefore promoting LDL endocytosis and degradation; however, the mechanism of Lp(a) reduction with PCSK9 inhibitors is currently not known. Small molecules (eg, muvalaplin) selectively prevent the binding of apolipoprotein A (apo A) to apolipoprotein B 100 (apo B 100), undermining the assembly and functioning of the Lp(a) complex. Small interfering RNAs (siRNAs) [...] Evolocumab has been tested in patients with elevated LDL cholesterol, showing a 12-week mean reduction of Lp(a) levels ranging from 15.5% to 31.3%, depending on dose.52 In a subanalysis of the FOURIER study (Further Cardiovascular Outcomes Research With PCSK9 Inhibition in Subjects With Elevated Risk), evolocumab resulted in an average reduction of 26.9% in Lp(a) levels after 48 weeks.53 Patients with higher baseline Lp(a) values experienced greater absolute reductions in Lp(a), correlating
