Title: Rethinking tubulin acetylation: From regulation to cellular ...
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S095506742500050X
Content: cargos is needed to control critical cellular functions, including cell survival and neurotransmission . Loss of acetylation was shown to impair polarized axonal transport across species  and, moreover, increasing microtubule acetylation by treatment with HDAC6 inhibitors rescues transport defects resulting from mutations in _Huntingtin_, _LRRK2_, _HSPB1_ and _FUS_ genes [65, 66, 67, 68]. While the role of tubulin acetylation in promoting intracellular transport remains unclear, several studies [...] microtubule acetylation is rapidly induced in response to hyperosmotic stress and may impair retrograde transport of early endosomes . The authors propose this mechanism could be important to reduce endocytosis, thereby protecting cells against drastic changes in cell volume and membrane tension . Increased microtubule acetylation was also observed in response to centrosome amplification, although whether acetylated microtubules could enable these cells to cope with challenges that arise from [...] to enhanced microtubule acetylation . Levels of acetylated microtubules could also be modulated by inhibitory phosphorylation of SIRT2 at S331, as expression of a phosphomimicking SIRT2 S331D mutant increases tubulin acetylation . Moreover, metabolic changes could impact microtubule acetylation levels since SIRT2’s deacetylase activity was shown to be NAD+-dependent and consistently, NAD depletion results in hyperacetylation of the microtubule network [10,52]. Taken together, these studies [...] In normal conditions, most proliferating cells maintain relatively low levels of tubulin acetylation and increased acetylation has been associated with microtubule age . This could be partly explained by the fact that long-lived microtubules are more likely to be exposed to mechanical stresses that can damage the lattice, creating more luminal entry sites for αTAT1 . In addition, acetylation may enable microtubules to better resist mechanical stress, consequently increasing their longevity [...] plated on stiff substrates, recruitment of αTAT1 to focal adhesions and consequent increase in microtubule acetylation promotes the release of GEF-H1 from microtubules into the cytoplasm, leading to increased RhoA activity, cell contractility and traction forces that enable fast migration . Microtubule acetylation could also regulate cell migration by modulating cell-substrate adhesions. In migrating astrocytes, increased microtubule acetylation enhances migration speed by promoting faster
