Content: The urokinase-type plasminogen activator receptor (uPAR) is upregulated on podocytes in proteinuric kidney diseases including primary podocytopathies, and results in the activation of the integrin-α v β 3 pathway.3,4 This in turn activates the Rho family small GTPases particularly the Rac family small GTPases (Rac1) and the cell division control protein 42 homolog (Cdc42), leading to cytoskeletal rearrangement, podocyte damage, and altered GFB permeability.5,6 uPAR can also interact with formyl peptide receptors (FPRs) through its 88 Ser-Arg-Ser-Arg-Tyr 92 amino acid sequence, mediating leukocyte chemotaxis and cytoskeletal rearrangement 7 through the activation of Rac1 and Cdc42.8 FPR expression and its interaction with integrin-α v β 3 have been also recently reported in mouse podocytes.9 uPAR interaction with FPRs can be blocked by the synthetic tetrapeptide UPARANT (UPA) (Ac-L-Arg-Aib-L-Arg-D-Cα(Me)Phe-NH 2), which inhibits FPR downstream signaling.10 Preliminary data have shown that UPA administration in a diabetic nephropathy rat model resulted in protection from kidney damage.11
