Title: DNA methylation modulates nucleosome retention in sperm and ...
URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-55441-1
Content: In the germ line and during early embryogenesis, DNA methylation (DNAme) undergoes global erasure and re-establishment to support germ cell and embryonic development. While DNAme acquisition during male germ cell development is essential for setting genomic DNA methylation imprints, other intergenerational roles for paternal DNAme in defining embryonic chromatin are unknown. By studying germ cells single and double conditionally deficient for *Dnmt3a* and/or *Dnmt3b* we identify developmental and genomic context specific contributions of each enzyme to safeguarding and de novo depositing DNAme during spermatogenesis. By exploiting chromatin perturbations specific to germ cells, we identify two roles for DNAme in sperm in (a) modulating nucleosome occupancy and H3K4me3 enrichment at CpG-rich regions in developing sperm and (b) preventing premature acquisition of H3K4me3 specifically on paternally inherited alleles in the early embryo.
