Content: Vaults, the largest known ribonucleoparticles, have intrigued scientists for over a decade, particularly since it was discovered that their levels are correlated with tumor aggressiveness and poor prognosis. Their main component, major vault protein (MVP) is identical with lung resistance protein, a clinically relevant tumor marker.1, 2, 3, 4, 5 MVP protein is often linked to non-P-glycoprotein multidrug resistance and is upregulated in certain types of cancers, for example glioblastomas 6 and [...] Major vault protein (MVP) is the main constituent of vaults, large ribonucleoprotein particles implicated in resistance to cancer therapy and correlated with poor survival prognosis. Here, we report the structure of the main repeat element in human MVP. The ∼55 amino acid residue MVP domain has a unique, novel fold that consists of a three-stranded antiparallel β-sheet. The solution NMR structure of a two-domain fragment reveals the interdomain contacts and relative orientations of the two MVP [...] Structurally, vaults are composed of three proteins: major vault protein (MVP), vault poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase (VPARP) and telomerase associated protein (TEP), plus one or more small RNA molecules (vRNAs).9, 11, 22, 23 The stoichiometry was determined by electron microscopy to be 96, 2 and 11(±5) molecules of MVP, TEP and VPARP, respectively.22 Electron cryomicroscopy studies at 32 Å and 22 Å resolution characterized vaults as smooth 420 Å×750 Å hollow barrels with a wall thickness of ∼20–35 [...] between vaults and drug resistance has been proposed to occur by sequestering of drugs by vaults, followed by efflux using pump proteins.3, 11 This is not supported by recent findings, which indicate that vaults are not involved directly in anthracycline sequestration or in their efflux from the nucleus.12, 13 Instead, the association of MVP with cancer may arise from a role in cell signaling. [...] very recently it was discovered that its mammalian ortholog functions as a critical negative regulator of p53, targeting p53 and c-Jun for ubiquitination and degradation.19, 20, 21 Phosphorylation-dependent interactions between MVP and COP1 may explain the role of MVP in cancer and, more specifically, in drug resistance.17
