Content: Semaglutide is one of the first highly effective anti-obesity medications. It is a weekly subcutaneous injection starting at a dose of 0.25 mg and increasing every 4 weeks to reach a maintenance dose of either 1.7 mg or 2.4 mg per week. Semaglutide recently received another FDA approval for its cardioprotective role in secondary prevention of adverse cardiovascular events in patients with established cardiovascular disease like coronary artery disease and stroke. It can help patients achieve [...] Although anti-obesity medications represent a critical component of comprehensive obesity management, they are most effective when integrated into a holistic treatment approach that includes lifestyle modifications and ongoing patient support. By understanding their mechanisms, tailoring therapies to individual needs and staying informed with reliable resources, health care providers can optimize outcomes for patients navigating the challenges of obesity.

## References: [...] The landscape of anti-obesity medications is diverse, with options ranging from oral medications like phentermine and its combinations to injectables such as GLP-1 analogs and gastrointestinal lipase inhibitors like orlistat. These medications vary in efficacy and side-effect profiles but collectively aim to achieve meaningful weight reduction ranging from 5% to 22% of total body weight, depending on the specific medication and patient response. [...] Anti-obesity medications work in various ways to promote weight loss. Many medications primarily target central pathways in the brain that regulate appetite and satiety. For instance, phentermine, a widely used sympathomimetic amine, suppresses appetite by acting on neurotransmitter systems in the brain. Similarly, Qsymia (phentermine and topiramate, Vivus) and Contrave (bupropion and naltrexone, Currax) work on appetite suppression and energy expenditure through different pathways. [...] The primary goal of using medications in obesity management is to effectively treat this chronic disease by reducing excess body weight. In doing so, these medications aim to alleviate associated metabolic comorbidities such as diabetes, hypertension and dyslipidemia. They are typically prescribed for adults with a BMI of 30 kg/m² or greater, or for those with a BMI exceeding 27 kg/m² who also experience adverse consequences of obesity. This approach improves overall health outcomes and
Score: 0.75312227


Title: Prescription Weight Loss Drugs: GLP-1s, Tirzepatide, and More
URL: https://www.webmd.com/obesity/weight-loss-prescription-weight-loss-medicine
Content: The newest prescription drugs approved to treat obesity are semaglutide (Wegovy) and tirzepatide (Zepbound). Other older medications include liraglutide (Saxenda), naltrexone-bupropion (Contrave), orlistat (Alli, Xenical), and phentermine-topiramate (Qsymia). [...] Prescription weight loss drugs can be an effective treatment for obesity. However, they aren't often covered by insurance, and some are very expensive. You could lose 10% of your body weight or more with these drugs, but you may have to take them for life to keep the weight off.

### Related Links [...] But doctors say obesity should be considered the same way as other chronic conditions that can be treated and kept under control through medication.

## Clinical Trials for Weight Loss Drugs

Clinical trials are an important part of the process by which new drugs become available. Researchers test experimental medications to find out if they're safe and effective. [...] The newest generation of weight loss drugs is changing the conversation about obesity. As researchers learn more about the biological processes involved in appetite and metabolism, weight management shifts from a matter of willpower to a complex but medically treatable condition. [...] Over the course of a year, people who take prescription weight loss drugs in addition to following a program of exercise and healthy eating lose 3%-12% more of their starting weight than people who make lifestyle changes alone. That's according to the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases.
