Title: Serious side-effects for popular weight loss drugs raising concerns
URL: https://wjla.com/news/spotlight-on-america/serious-side-effects-for-popular-weight-loss-drugs-raising-concerns-risk-benefits-health-obesity-glp1-zepbound-wegovy-mounjaro-semiglutide-medication
Content: WASHINGTON (SOA) — Weight loss drugs are exploding in popularity across the country with many calling them game-changers when it comes to treating obesity and all the medical conditions that can come with it. But as more people use these GLP-1 injectable drugs, there are growing concerns over potentially serious side effects. Some patients have reported everything from intestinal blockages to sudden blindness. Spotlight on America investigates whether these so-called “miracle drugs” could be a [...] “The greatest side effects are usually gastrointestinal, meaning nausea, more extreme would be vomiting, diarrhea, or constipation,” Dr. Rizo explained.

She says she has seen more serious side effects in her practice, but not frequently.

“I have seen people who have had, in very rare situations, for example, obstruction,” Dr. Rizo said. [...] Some of the more serious complications reported include:

“We've seen individuals who suffer blindness, bowel blockages that result in some sort of perforation of the bowel, sepsis and can ultimately lead to death. We've had individuals who, because of the paralysis of the stomach, ultimately develop vomiting and aspirate resulting in death,” Orent said, describing some of the more serious complications he says plaintiffs have experienced while taking the drugs. [...] More than 2,000 patients have filed lawsuits against the manufacturers of GLP-1 drugs Novo Nordisk and Eli Lilly, alleging they suffered serious injuries while taking GLP-1 medications. Their individual cases have been centralized in a federal multi-district litigation (MDL) in the U.S. District Court for the Eastern District of Pennsylvania. The master suit names several GLP-1 drugs, including Ozempic, Wegovy, Rybelsus, Saxenda, Victoza, Mounjaro, Zepbound, and Trulicity. [...] That quickly led to more drugs— including Novo Nordisk’s WeGovy, FDA approved to treat obesity in 2121; followed by Eli Lilly’s drug Mounjaro, FDA approved to treat Diabetes in 2022; and then, Eli Lilly’s Zepbound, FDA approved to treat obesity in 2023.

Dr. Rizo says she’s found the benefits for those who use GLP-1 medications have been significant.

“Health benefits for sugars, health benefits for high blood pressure, health benefits for cholesterol,” she said.
