Content: The treatment of these patients has been controversial over the last years. Despite the traditional underrepresentation of patients aged >65 years in clinical trials [3,4], several studies have recently addressed the efficacy of chemotherapy and biological agents in the ‘non-frail’ elderly population with metastatic CRC (mCRC) [5–18]. A pivotal trial in elderly and/or frail mCRC patients demonstrated that single-agent fluoropyrimidines were not inferior in terms of treatment outcomes and [...] This subgroup analysis provides evidence that oxaliplatin–fluoropyrimide-based chemotherapy is an active and safe approach for the treatment of elderly patients with previously untreated advanced CRC, extending observations made by other investigators. The first key aspect of the 03-TTD-01 study is that almost one-third of patients were ≥70 years of age, which is in accordance with the increasing tendency to involve elderly patients in clinical trials, as shown by the SWOG Cooperative Group [...] resulted in better quality of life than a combination chemotherapy including oxaliplatin .   Show abstract Frail elderly patients with metastatic colorectal cancer (mCRC) are not candidates for chemotherapy. Monotherapy with anti-epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) monoclonal antibodies may be an option for these patients with few systemic toxic effects. Single-arm, multicentre, phase II trial including patients ⩾70 years with wild-type (WT) _KRAS_ (exon 2) mCRC, Eastern Cooperative [...] to age (<70 years vs. ≥70 years). As no significant differences were found in the parameters of activity (TTP, OS and RR) between the two treatment regimens, pooled data of both regimens would be combined for the analysis, whereas the safety profile has been evaluated according to the treatment schedule because significant differences have been found between FUOX and XELOX in the rates of grade 3/4 diarrhea and grade 1/2 stomatitis, hyperbilirubinemia and hand-foot syndrome . [...] From April 2002 to August 2004, 348 patients were randomized to receive FUOX (_n_=174) or XELOX (_n_=174), and 342 (_n_=171 in each treatment group) were eligible for evaluation. A total of 109 patients (31.9%) were ≥70 years of age, and 233 patients (68.1%) were <70 years. Baseline clinical and demographic data are summarized in Table 1. Median age was 73 years in the elderly group (range 70–81), and 59 years in the younger group (range 32–69). Prognostic characteristics were well balanced
