Title: Nucleoside triphosphate
URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nucleoside_triphosphate
Content: A nucleoside triphosphate is a nucleoside containing a nitrogenous base bound to a 5-carbon sugar (either ribose or deoxyribose), with three phosphate groups bound to the sugar. They are the molecular precursors of both DNA and RNA, which are chains of nucleotides made through the processes of DNA replication and transcription ""Transcription (biology)""). Nucleoside triphosphates also serve as a source of energy for cellular reactions and are involved in signalling pathways. [...] and act as a downstream effector. [...] ## Nucleoside triphosphate metabolism

[edit] [...] The term nucleoside refers to a nitrogenous base linked to a 5-carbon sugar (either ribose or deoxyribose). Nucleotides are nucleosides covalently linked to one or more phosphate groups. To provide information about the number of phosphates, nucleotides may instead be referred to as nucleoside (mono, di, or tri) phosphates. Thus, nucleoside triphosphates are a type of nucleotide. [...] necessary because the formation of ATP is energetically unfavourable.
