Title: Nucleoside Triphosphate (NTP): Definition, Structure and ...
URL: https://www.bocsci.com/resources/nucleoside-triphosphate-ntp-definition-structure-and-applications.html?srsltid=AfmBOorUdrGthP4heseW158RpPJfTNu6SZ-kYlleNaR-083I76sZ6sxS
Content: Nucleotide triphosphates (NTPs), also known as nucleoside triphosphates, are nucleotides containing three phosphate groups that exist in organisms and play important roles. NTP molecules are building blocks of DNA and RNA and participate in signal conduction and energy conversion processes within cells. For example, ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is the most direct source of energy in organisms. It can release and store energy inside cells and support various biochemical reactions. The NTP [...] Nucleoside triphosphate is a specific class of nucleotides containing three phosphate groups. They fall into two categories:

Ribonucleoside triphosphate (NTPs: ATP, GTP, CTP, UTP): Ribose as a sugar base. Among them: ATP is the main energy carrier, releasing energy through hydrolysis to drive a variety of biochemical reactions. GTP plays an important role in protein synthesis and signal transduction. CTP and UTP are involved in the synthesis of phospholipids and polysaccharide biomolecules. [...] Drug development: Nucleoside triphosphates and their derivatives play an important role in the development of antiviral and anticancer drugs. For example, nucleoside triphosphates are often used as precursors of antiviral drugs, chemically modified to form triphosphorylated forms with antiviral activity. These compounds inhibit the replication of viruses such as HIV and herpes viruses. In addition, nucleoside triphosphates are also used in the development of anticancer drugs, such as [...] Gene therapy and molecular diagnosis: Nucleoside triphosphates are basic building blocks of DNA and RNA synthesis and play an important role in gene therapy. They can be used as substrates for DNA polymerases to build new gene sequences for gene repair or replacement. In addition, NTPs are also widely used in molecular diagnosis, such as real-time fluorescent quantitative PCR technology, which uses NTPs as reaction substrates for detecting and quantifying nucleic acid sequences. [...] Energy metabolism and cellular signaling: Nucleoside triphosphates play a central role in cell energy metabolism and are the source of energy for many biochemical reactions. They are involved in intracellular signal transduction, protein synthesis, and the replication and transmission of genetic information. For example, ATP serves as the energy currency of cells and plays an important role in muscle contraction, nerve transmission and heart function.
