URL: https://americanheadachesociety.org/news/late-life-ischemic-stroke-risk-not-associated-with-early-onset-migraine
Content: In a post-hoc analysis intended to evaluate the association between the age of onset for migraine with aura and ischemic stroke, researchers identified 447 subjects with migraine with aura and 1,128 subjects with migraine without aura among 11,592 participants, excluding patients who had previously experienced a stroke. Androulakis and colleagues noted an association between ischemic stroke and migraine with aura that developed when patients reached at least 50 years of age, compared with [...] “We thought that, most likely, the longer you had migraine, the more likely it was to be associated with an increased risk of stroke,” she says. “We thought that cumulative exposure to migraine with aura would most likely be linked to an increased risk of stroke, and that those with a shorter amount of exposure were not as likely to have an increased risk of ischemic stroke later in life. But what we found was quite the opposite.” [...] Additionally, researchers found that migraine without aura was found to not be associated with increased risk of stroke, regardless of the age of onset.

### Unexpected findings

Androulakis says the results of the analysis were contrary to what she and her colleagues hypothesized. [...] Androulakis cautions, however, that ICHD 3 does not feature detailed descriptions/classifications of migraine with visual aura, despite the existence of well-defined research on how exactly the condition presents.

“I think that if we can further define it and give it more-definitive criteria, then design a study based on that, that could potentially shed more light on stroke risk in this population,” she says.

### Interpreting the results [...] patients who did not experience migraine. The onset of migraine before patients reached 50 years of age, however, was not associated with stroke.
