Title: Myelin dynamics: protecting and shaping neuronal functions
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S095943881730168X
Content: In this review, we discuss some of the emerging mechanisms by which oligodendrocytes wrap myelin and are metabolically coupled to axons, how they sense spiking activity and adapt their metabolic support, and how myelination is involved in shaping brain plasticity and cognitive functions. Apart from their effect on conduction, oligodendrocytes within the myelin sheath also provide nutrition, particularly lactate, to the underlying axons, fuelling axonal mitochondria, and so crucially supporting highly energy-demanding axonal transport.7–9 Potassium clearance, through oligodendrocyte-specific potassium ion channels, is also crucial for axonal function and integrity.10,11   Show abstract The treatment of multiple sclerosis has been transformed by the successful development of immunotherapies that efficiently reduce disease activity and related clinical relapses during the relapsing-remitting phase of the disease.
