Content: Much like the insulation around the wires in electrical systems, glial cells form a membraneous sheath surrounding axons called myelin, thereby insulating the axon. By contrast, in the CNS, the oligodendrocyte sends cell processes to myelinate multiple segments on many axons (Figure 2). In the PNS, neuregulin 1 type III protein is expressed on the axon surface and interacts with glial ErbB receptors, and it has a pivotal role for Schwann cell differentiation and myelination (for review, see Nave & Salzer 2006). For example, during the development of PNS myelinated nerve fibers, a molecule called gliomedin is secreted from myelinating Schwann cells then incorporated into the extracellular matrix surrounding nodes, where it promotes assembly of nodal axonal molecules.
