Content: Certain non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) are known to have anticancer effects. However, it is unclear whether all NSAIDs have anticancer effects, and thus far, very few studies have compared the antitumor effects among multiple NSAIDs. Therefore, we aimed to identify NSAIDs that enhance the anticancer effect of cisplatin (CDDP); the effects of 17 NSAIDs in lung cancer cells and their spheroids as cancer stem cells (CSCs) were evaluated. Some of the NSAIDs showed cytotoxic effects [...] •Some NSAIDs show anticancer effects against cancer cells and CSCs. 
   •Diclofenac additively or synergistically enhanced the antitumor effects of CDDP. 
   •Celecoxib reduced the cytotoxic effect of CDDP on cancer cells by increasing xCT. 
   •Celecoxib enhanced the effect of CDDP on CSCs while increasing xCT. 

Abstract [...] Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) are anti-inflammatory agents that act by inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX) activity (Vane, 1971). COX has two isoforms: COX-1 is constantly expressed and COX-2 expression is induced during inflammation. NSAIDs inhibit COX non-selectively or COX-2 selectively (Loftin et al., 2002). The World Health Organization recommends the use of NSAIDs for cancer pain (World Health Organization, 1996); thus, anticancer agents and NSAIDs are often used in [...] HSP70, but the safety of this combination was not satisfactory due to the large dose of ibuprofen involved .   Show abstract As one of the key features of tumor, inflammation attracts much attention of many researchers for antitumor study, in which non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) have been widely investigated in anti-inflammatory treatments in cancers. Herein, we report a series of novel platinum(II) complexes derived from conjugation of several NSAIDs derivatives with two [...] combination in cancer treatment. In addition, it has reported that some NSAIDs not only have anti-inflammatory effects but also anticancer effects (Moon et al., 2014). Therefore, appropriate NSAID and anticancer agent combinations can be expected to enhance the antitumor effects of the anticancer agent, while attenuating cancer pain. However, it is unclear which NSAIDs have anticancer effects, and there are few reports comparing the antitumor effects of NSAIDs under similar conditions. Hence,
