Title: RUNX transcription factors: biological functions and ...
URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10908630/
Content: Among the RUNX family, RUNX1 exhibits a particularly complex role across different types of cancer. RUNX1 is one of the genes significantly mutated in luminal estrogen-receptor-positive (ER+) breast cancer. Its expression is lost during the development of ER+ breast cancer, suggesting the tumor-suppressive role of RUNX1 . A follow-up study supported the idea that RUNX1 mainly acts as a tumor suppressor in ER+ breast cancer, and it can exert oncogenic effects by suppressing the estrogen-mediated [...] well-differentiated tissues, while displaying reduced expression in poorly differentiated and undifferentiated tissues, suggesting its tumor-suppressive role in neuroblastoma . Moreover, RUNX1 is also aberrantly upregulated in human pituitary tumors, contributing to tumor progression . In an osteosarcoma study, the expression levels of RUNX1 mRNA and protein were found to be higher in tumor tissues than in normal tissues adjacent to the tumor . Similarly, Jin et al. substantiated that RUNX1 is [...] Moreover, diminished RUNX1 expression is associated with reduced OS in patients with NSCLC, serving as a predictive factor for adverse prognosis in this malignancy . Research by Ramsey et al. substantiates that RUNX1 functions as a tumor suppressor in lung adenocarcinoma (LUAD), where its downregulation correlates with worse OS . In high-grade serous ovarian cancer, RUNX1 stands as an independent prognostic marker for patient outcomes or therapeutic response . Elevated expression levels of both [...] human pancreatic cancer samples and they confirmed the role of RUNX1 in promoting pancreatic cancer cell proliferation . Another study on pancreatic cancer showed consistent results, with quantitative polymerase chain reaction results indicating that the mRNA level of RUNX1 was significantly higher in human pancreatic cancer samples than in normal pancreatic tissues . Pharmacological inhibition of RUNX1 can significantly suppress tumor growth in patient-derived organoids of primary pancreatic [...] epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT) [25, 60]. To sum up, RUNX1 serves as a double-edged sword in cancer development, acting as either a tumor suppressor or a pro-tumor agent, depending on the type of cancer.
