Content: A recent study has shed light on the molecular basis of epithelioid transformation (27). Using a lineage-tracing approach in the transparent zebrafish, the authors showed that as the tuberculous granuloma forms, its macrophages (both infected and uninfected) undergo a mesenchymal-epithelial reprogramming to express canonical epithelial determinants and structures, e.g., E-cadherin, that form the adherens junctions. Paradoxically, epithelioid transformation that has been surmised to be host [...] harm to the parent. Finally, even xenografts can provoke granulomas in worms, much as they do in hamsters, indicating that the granulomatous response participates in nonself recognition in both vertebrates and invertebrates (20, 25). Given this body of work, we should not in retrospect have been surprised to see that bona fide epithelioid granulomas developed in response to mycobacterial infection of zebrafish larvae, which do not yet have adaptive immunity (26). [...] signaling and B cells can contribute to fibrosis. Necrosis, at least in active tuberculous granulomas, may be induced by an overall failure to control infection, a deficiency or excess of TNF or a myeloid deficiency that interferes with the replenishment of granuloma macrophages dying therein. [...] Granulomas are organized aggregates of macrophages, often with characteristic morphological changes, and other immune cells. These evolutionarily ancient structures form in response to persistent particulate stimuli—infectious or noninfectious—that individual macrophages cannot eradicate. Granulomas evolved as protective responses to destroy or sequester particles but are frequently pathological in the context of foreign bodies, infections, and inflammatory diseases. We summarize recent [...] Turning to whole animals, it is abundantly clear that invertebrates form bona fide granulomas in response to irritants, foreign bodies, and infection. In response to such stimuli, bivalves, snails, worms, and insects develop compact structures with spatial organization that contain concentric layers of phagocyte-like cells that undergo epithelioid transformation and become necrotic and proliferating fibroblasts that enable a fibrous capsule to form around the inciting agent (18–23). Not only
