Title: Coeliac disease and gluten sensitivity | Better Health Channel
URL: https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/conditionsandtreatments/coeliac-disease-and-gluten-sensitivity
Content: Evidence shows that uncontaminated oats are well tolerated by most people with coeliac disease. However, in some people with coeliac disease, oat consumption can trigger a potentially harmful immune response. Please note that the absence of symptoms when consuming oats does not necessarily indicate they are safe – bowel damage can still occur despite the absence of symptoms. [...] Coeliac disease (pronounced SEE-lee-ak) is a significant medical condition that can cause serious problems if it is not diagnosed and treated properly. It affects the small intestine – the part of the digestive system responsible for absorbing nutrients. If you have coeliac disease, eating gluten – a protein found in wheat, rye, barley, and oats – causes damage to the lining of your small intestine. It also causes inflammation in other parts of your body. [...] The normal lining of the small intestine (also called the small bowel) is covered with tiny, finger-like projections called villi. The cells on villi break down and absorb nutrients in food. If you have coeliac disease, the mucosa (lining) of your small intestine is damaged. This causes inflammation of the villi, referred to as villous atrophy. As a result of this inflammation, the surface area of your small intestine, which enables the absorption of nutrients and minerals, is seriously [...] It is recommended that individuals who wish to consume oats as part of their gluten free diet do so under medical supervision to ensure appropriate review and safety. Undertaking a gastroscopy and small bowel biopsy before and after three months of regular uncontaminated oat consumption can help guide whether an individual with coeliac disease can safely consume oats.

## Avoid cross-contamination with gluten [...] Coeliac disease is an autoimmune disease where the immune system reacts abnormally to gluten.
 For people with coeliac disease, even small amounts of gluten can damage the lining of the small intestine (bowel), which prevents the proper absorption of food nutrients. Inflammation also occurs elsewhere in the body.
 If you have coeliac disease, inflammation and damage can occur even if you have no symptoms.
 Correct diagnosis of coeliac disease in adults can only be made by gastroscopy.
