Title: Registry-Based Randomized Controlled Trials: A New Paradigm for ...
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022480420303449
Content: Randomized controlled trials (RCTs) represent the gold standard level of evidence for establishing treatment benefit due to their ability to isolate the effect of an intervention while minimizing internal bias.1 Still, one in five surgical RCTs is discontinued early, and one in three completed surgical RCTs goes unpublished.2 In considering reasons for RCT failure, traditional RCTs may be hindered by high costs, need for numerous dedicated personnel to coordinate study adherence, and burdensome [...] Since March 2017, our group has started 9 registry-based RCTs. We have completed enrollment in 4 of these trials with the remaining 5 continuing to enroll. Key trial characteristics are demonstrated in Table 2. Three trials have focused on open ventral hernia repair, two have focused on minimally invasive ventral hernia repair, two have focused on minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair, and parastomal and paraesophageal hernias have accounted for one trial each. To date, all but one of our [...] (2011) 
   S.J. Chapman _et al._
### Discontinuation and non-publication of surgical randomised controlled trials: observational study

### BMJ (2014) 

There are more references available in the full text version of this article.

Cited by (23)

   ### Two decades of surgical randomized controlled trials: worldwide trends in volume and methodological quality [...] documentation requirements.3 Restrictive inclusion criteria may slow enrollment and limit the generalizability of results, threatening external validity.4 Study-specific labs, imaging, and visits that deviate from typical practice may further increase risk of study drop-out and protocol deviations and impose additional burden on physician investigators.5 Together, these factors represent significant barriers to the conduct of high-quality surgical RCTs, lead to substantial waste of resources [...] Since 2017, our group has completed enrollment in 4 registry-based RCTs with another 5 trials ongoing. Of these, 4 trials have been multicenter. Over 1000 patients have been enrolled in these studies, with follow-up rates of 90% or greater. Most of these trials are on track to complete enrollment in approximately 2 y from their start date. Beyond salary support, resource utilization is low. None of our trials has been terminated due to lack of resources or futility.

### Conclusions
