Content: Randomized controlled trials (RCTs) represent the gold standard level of evidence for establishing treatment benefit due to their ability to isolate the effect of an intervention while minimizing internal bias.1 Still, one in five surgical RCTs is discontinued early, and one in three completed surgical RCTs goes unpublished.2 In considering reasons for RCT failure, traditional RCTs may be hindered by high costs, need for numerous dedicated personnel to coordinate study adherence, and burdensome [...] Since March 2017, our group has started 9 registry-based RCTs. We have completed enrollment in 4 of these trials with the remaining 5 continuing to enroll. Key trial characteristics are demonstrated in Table 2. Three trials have focused on open ventral hernia repair, two have focused on minimally invasive ventral hernia repair, two have focused on minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair, and parastomal and paraesophageal hernias have accounted for one trial each. To date, all but one of our [...] (2011) 
