Cited by (17) [...] already have a mild anaemia. The degree of anaemia was about three times affected by co-infection (Pillai's trace=0.551, _P_=0.001) as was affected by malaria alone (Pillai's trace=0.185, _P_=0.001). B19V alone did not significantly affect the development of anaemia in a non-anaemic child. Microcytic anaemia was associated with B19V and co-infection with B19V and malaria more than normocytic normochromic anaemia. [...] ### Conclusions

B19V was associated with malaria in cases of severe anaemia. The association posed a significant risk for exacerbation of anaemia in mild anaemic children. B19V and co-infection with B19V and malaria may be associated with microcytic anaemia rather than normocytic normochromic anaemia as seen in cases of B19V infection among persons with red cell abnormalities.

Access through your organization

Check access to the full text by signing in through your organization. [...] Comments There are conflicting reports on whether co-infection with malaria and B19V can exacerbate anaemia. Some findings suggest that such co-infection is associated with anaemia, while others suggest not. This report finds that co-infection of B19V and malaria is associated with microcytic anaemia, which is novel and calls for further investigations to establish this effect. (Details on Page 136) [...] Volume 3, Issue 2, February 2013, Pages 129-139

