URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1551741119305777
Content: 2025, Research in Social and Administrative Pharmacy  Show abstract Pharmacists’ participation in interprofessional ward rounds has been shown to reduce adverse drug events, improve medication appropriateness and communication about medicines. However, pharmacists do not routinely participate in interprofessional ward rounds with reports of participation varying from 10% to 39%. Knowledge about how, under what circumstances and why integration of pharmacists into interprofessional ward round [...] ### Financial impact of medication reviews by clinical pharmacists to reduce in-hospital adverse drug events: a return-on-investment analysis [...] therapy, or ‘clinical interventions’. The clinical and economic benefits of such interventions have been widely established in the literature, with clinical pharmacists' contributions being shown to significantly improve patients' medication management, reduce morbidity and mortality, and decrease the rate of adverse drug reactions and the cost of care.3, 4, 5, 6, 7 There has, however, been little research into the comparative impact of the various activities undertaken by clinical pharmacists, [...] associated with significantly quicker interventions (p < 0.001). All major interventions were made by WR pharmacists; 80% were made on rounds. Major interventions were estimated to have decreased lengths of stay, intensive care requirements and procedure costs. [...] Participation in multidisciplinary WRs is a time-consuming activity, taking over 20% of clinical pharmacists’ time.9 While it has been established that pharmacist participation on multidisciplinary WRs is beneficial,10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15 there have been few quantitative studies analysing the impact of interventions made during WRs, and no previous studies have compared the specific impact of WR participation with that of other clinical duties. Further investigation into this area is essential
