Content: communication between key team members and patients.6 It is known that the participation of clinical pharmacists in ward rounds can reduce adverse drug events and improve medication appropriateness.7, 8, 9, 10 [...] ### Conclusion

Clinical pharmacists participate in ward rounds when they develop a positive mindset about ward round participation and perceive ward rounds as an enabler to the establishment of trusted professional relationships with medical practitioners. This trusted relationship creates an environment where the pharmacist develops confidence in making relevant recommendations.

   Previous article in issue
   Next article in issue

Keywords

Ward round

Interprofessional relationship [...] round participation was included to capture serendipitous events (e.g., unscheduled ward rounds, activities which lead to or followed on after ward rounds). In addition, the observation of clinical pharmacist activities outside of ward rounds was performed because some clinical pharmacists did not routinely participate in ward rounds. Observation of these clinical pharmacists enabled observation of barriers to ward round participation and events that lead to their non-participation in ward [...] In the example below, the clinical pharmacist is explaining what a busy day looks like in the ward. Participating in the ward round was seen as an additional task, and therefore was not prioritised.
> “… I guess having time to do assessments [for residency program] … attending clinical meetings as well as focusing on the ward rounds takes time away from ward type duties example: discharge review and medication [history taking …”]
> [...] Those clinical pharmacists in this study who initiated and participated in the ward rounds shared how they had a strong belief in their ability to produce positive outcomes when they participated.
> “… everything I try and do centres around ward round … [it’s about] switching your mindset to prioritising ward round … [because participation in a ward round is] a massive learning experience …”
> 
> Interview excerpt from clinical pharmacist Bailey (ID: PB interview transcript 9)
