Content: The pre-mRNA splicing process itself is mediated by a complex and highly regulated molecular machinery known as the spliceosome. Formed by a complex of RNA and proteins (ribonucleoprotein) the spliceosome functions in a dynamic cycle of stepwise reactions in which the complex is assembled and disassembled for every single splicing reaction.11 The splicing process is not a “cut and paste” mechanism, but rather two consecutive S N 2-type transesterification reactions involving functional groups [...] There is evidence that pre-mRNA splicing is, for the most part, a co-transcriptional process.9, 10 Therefore, transcription elongation rate, chromatin structure and conformation, as well as histone post-translational modifications directly influence the outcome of the splicing process.

