Title: The cytokines in tumor microenvironment: from cancer initiation ...
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1040842824000544
Content: Interleukins-6 (IL-6) is a pleotropic pro-inflammatory cytokine and act as a potent growth factor for both immune cells and malignant cells (Candido and Hagemann, 2013, Chakraborty et al., 2020). It drives a major role in both acute and chronic inflammation. IL-6 is secreted by fibroblast, monocytes, macrophages, MSCs and T-helper cells (Chakraborty et al., 2020). IL-6 exerts its molecular action in an autocrine and paracrine fashion, after binding to its receptor (IL-6Rα; gp80, CD126) and [...] Infection,inflammation and tumor development [...] TGF-β is a well-known pleotropic cytokine that contributes a significant role in cancer initiation, elongation and progression. TGF-β are generally secreted from neutrophils and macrophages (Lan et al., 2021). TGF-β have three isotypes (TGF-β1, TGF-β2, TGF-β3). TGF-β exerts its action after binding with type II receptor (TGF-β RII) which upregulates type I receptor (TGF- β RI) phosphorylation, leads to a heterotetrameric complex that works through SMAD-dependent pathway (Landskron et al., 2014 [...] (Landskron et al., 2014, Liongue et al., 2015, Ragu et al., 2020). Inflammation is a biological process in which our immune system defends us from outside pathogens (bacteria, viruses, parasite etc.) as well as helps in repairing and healing of damaged tissue (Deshmukh et al., 2019). However, if inflammation is unregulated and remains for a prolonged period of time, it can turn into a chronic inflammation state that develops tumor growth (Deshmukh et al., 2019). Chronic inflammation can be [...] •Cancer originates from the site of chronic infection and inflammation. 
   •Inflammation is closely related with tumor microenvironment (TME) that drives a pivotal role in neoplasia. 
   •TME is largely orchestrated by several inflammatory cells and cytokines. 
   •Cytokines are the essential components of TME, which contributes to tumor growth and progression. 
   •Cytokines increase the risk of developing cancer symptoms in cancer patients. 

Abstract
