Title: Molecular Pathways: Understanding the Role of Rad52 in ...
URL: https://aacrjournals.org/clincancerres/article/18/23/6400/283673/Molecular-Pathways-Understanding-the-Role-of-Rad52
Content: Single-ended DSBs can occur at telomeres or at broken replication forks. These can be repaired by homologous recombination through a single-ended invasion process known as break-induced replication (BIR; ref. 5). Most BIR events are dependent on Rad51 and other homologous recombination factors used in DSBR and SDSA. This process can also occur without Rad51 protein although the Rad51-independent process is thought to be only a minor component of this reaction. [...] One of the most threatening forms of DNA damage is the DNA double-strand break (DSB), as both strands of the DNA duplex are impaired simultaneously. The major repair pathways that cope with DSBs are nonhomologous end-joining (NHEJ) and homologous recombination. In many organisms, Rad52 is a key protein involved in the homologous recombination pathway. This review will focus on Rad52, its role in homologous recombination, and the translational opportunities available through understanding this [...] ### Additional functions of _S. cerevisiae_ Rad52

ScRad52 is required for the Rad51-independent SSA and BIR reactions (4, 5). In concordance with its role in SSA, ScRad52 is able to anneal DNA strands that are either bare or coated with RPA (16, 18). There is some evidence to suggest that Rad52-mediated annealing of RPA-coated ssDNA strands is important for second ssDNA end capture in the DSBR pathway of homologous recombination (19).

