Content: independent of the genetic and cytoplasmic background and sex of the flies. Rapamycin treatment in yeast has been previously shown to extend replicative (Medvedik et al., 2007) and chronological (Powers et al., 2006) life span, and a recent study has shown that rapamycin also extends life span in mice when administered late in life (Harrison et al., 2009). These data, together with our observation that rapamycin treatment also extends life span in _Drosophila_, suggest that the antiaging [...] We administered rapamycin to adult flies by feeding and found that it robustly extended life span, exclusively through the TORC1 branch of the pathway. Rapamycin-mediated life span extension was not associated with alterations in either IIS or AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) activity but was blocked by alterations to both autophagy and translation. In addition, rapamycin extended life span beyond the maximum seen with DR and lessened the reduction in life span with full feeding, suggesting [...] (Selman et al., 2009). Furthermore, we found that removal of the cytoplasmic endosymbiont _Wolbachia_, which can modulate life span of some _Drosophila_ mutants (Ikeya et al., 2009, Toivonen et al., 2007), did not affect extension of life span by rapamycin (Figures S2C and S2D). Thus, rapamycin consistently extended fly life span in diverse genetic and cytoplasmic backgrounds and in both sexes. [...] Rapamycin is generally thought to be a specific inhibitor of TORC1, whereas TORC2 is considered to be rapamycin insensitive (Wullschleger et al., 2006). However, in some cell lines, prolonged rapamycin treatment can also inhibit TORC2 activity (Sarbassov et al., 2006). Because we administered rapamycin from early adulthood until death, and also because TORC2 has been implicated in aging in worms (Soukas et al., 2009), we determined whether TORC2 activity was reduced by rapamycin. TORC2 [...] Inhibition of TOR activity can delay the aging process, as evidenced by increased life