### Randomized trial of the effects of risedronate on vertebral fractures in women with established postmenopausal osteoporosis. Vertebral Efficacy With Risedronate Therapy (VERT) Study Group [...] Patients receiving risedronate 75 mg on 2 consecutive days for 1 yr had a 79% reduction in the risk of new vertebral fractures compared with historical control patients matched for key characteristics. This finding is consistent with the 1-yr vertebral fracture relative risk reductions of 65% and 61% observed in studies of risedronate 5 mg/d therapy in which there were concurrent controls 1, 2. [...] matched for key clinical characteristics from 2 placebo-controlled trials. Fracture rates in historical groups were compared with those of the 2CDM study treatment groups. At 12 mo, vertebral fractures occurred in 5.1% and 1.0% of the placebo and 5-mg risedronate historical patients, respectively. In the risedronate 5 mg/d and 75 mg 2CDM groups, fracture incidence was 1.5% and 1.1%, respectively. Based on comparisons with the historical control group, risedronate 75 mg 2CDM appears as effective [...] Risedronate 5 mg/d reduces vertebral fracture risk and increases bone mineral density (BMD) in patients with postmenopausal or glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. However, given the inadequate long-term adherence and persistence with bisphosphonate treatments among women with osteoporosis 6, 7, maximizing compliance is an important focus of clinical management. An option to help increase compliance with bisphosphonate therapy is to provide the patient with a choice of [...] To evaluate the 1-yr antifracture efficacy of risedronate 75 mg on 2CDM, as in the 2CDM study, historical control and risedronate 5 mg/d groups were constructed from previous trials that included both placebo and treated patients. The historical groups were matched with women in the 2CDM study on key variables that affect fracture risk (i.e., age, years since menopause, BMD and prevalent vertebral fractures), and the vertebral fracture incidence was compared with that of the 2CDM study group.
