Abstract

Importance: Few studies have analyzed contemporary data on outcomes at US teaching hospitals vs nonteaching hospitals.

Objective: To examine risk-adjusted outcomes for patients admitted to teaching vs nonteaching hospitals across a broad range of medical and surgical conditions. [...] Conclusions and relevance: Among hospitalizations for US Medicare beneficiaries, major teaching hospital status was associated with lower mortality rates for common conditions compared with nonteaching hospitals. Further study is needed to understand the reasons for these differences.

