Title: University Teaching Trauma Centers: Decreased Mortality ...
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022480420306831
Content: Teaching hospitals are often regarded as excellent institutions with significant resources and prominent academic faculty. However, the involvement of trainees may contribute to higher rates of complications. Conflicting reports exist regarding outcomes between teaching and nonteaching hospitals, and the difference among trauma centers is unknown. We hypothesized that university teaching trauma centers (UTTCs) and nonteaching trauma centers (NTTCs) would have a similar risk of complications and [...] Oftentimes, teaching academic centers are associated with advanced clinical capabilities and increased research and innovation efforts.3,4 Furthermore, Boscarino's review of patient surveys in over 150 hospitals demonstrated teaching status to be associated with positive public opinion and higher perceived quality.5 In support of this, Burke _et al._’s 6 retrospective study on teaching status and mortality found lower mortality rates in teaching hospitals than in minor teaching and nonteaching [...] From 895,896 patients, 765,802 (85%) were treated at UTTCs and 130,094 (15%) at NTTCs. After adjusting for covariates, UTTCs were associated with an increased risk of RCs (odds ratio (OR) 1.33, confidence interval (CI) 1.28-1.37, _P_<0.001), VTEs (OR 1.17, CI 1.12-1.23, _P_<0.001), and ICs (OR 1.56, CI 1.49-1.64, _P_<0.001). However, UTTCs were associated with decreased mortality (OR 0.96, CI 0.93-0.99, _P_=0.008) compared with NTTCs.

### Conclusions [...] hospitals (8.3% _versus_ 9.2% _versus_ 9.5%, _P_<0.001). However, other studies have found increased rates of complication and mortality at teaching hospitals in association with treatment by trainees. Van Groningen _et al._’s 7 retrospective study on patients with colon cancer found that teaching and academic hospitals had higher serious complication rates, when compared with patients at general hospitals. Ricciardi _et al._ 8 had similar findings when they retrospectively studied weekend [...] admissions and found that mortality after a weekend admission was 17% higher at hospitals with resident trainees than that at hospitals without resident trainees. Conversely, a large systematic review performed by Ayanian, which included 22 studies, found moderately to substantially better overall quality of care in major teaching hospitals than in nonteaching hospitals, although this varied with medical conditions.9
