Title: Current Trends in Sepsis-Related Mortality in the United ...
URL: https://journals.lww.com/ccmjournal/Fulltext/2021/08000/Current_Trends_in_Sepsis_Related_Mortality_in_the.5.aspx?generateEpub=Article%7Cccmjournal:2021:08000:00005%7C10.1097/ccm.0000000000005017%7C
Content: From 2005 to 2018, 6.7% of decedents had a diagnosis of sepsis. The overall sepsis-related mortality rates remained stable in both males (57 deaths per 100,000) and females (45.1 deaths per 100,000) during this period. Compared with Whites, the sepsis-related mortality rates were higher in Blacks (rate ratio = 1.78), Native Americans (rate ratio = 1.43), and Hispanics (rate ratio = 1.04) and were lower in Asians (rate ratio = 0.73). Sepsis-related mortality rates declined in Blacks, Hispanics, [...] and Asians but increased in Whites and Native Americans. The majority of sepsis-related deaths occurred in the hospital. The percentage of deaths in the nursing home decreased, whereas deaths occurring at home and hospice increased. [...] Abstract

### OBJECTIVES:

Sepsis is a life-threatening condition and is one of the leading causes of death in the United States. The burden of sepsis-related mortality in the United States in recent years is not well characterized. We sought to describe sepsis-related mortality rates and mortality trends in the United States from 2005 to 2018.

### DESIGN:

Retrospective population-based study.

