Title: A Nationwide Cohort Study on Antidepressant Use and Stroke Risk ...
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149291825002206
Content: Conclusions

In this nationwide population-based cohort study, the use of antidepressants increased the risk of stroke in adults aged 18–44 years. Indications for antidepressants should be carefully assessed to minimize the risk of stroke. Further robust, prospective, controlled clinical trials are warranted to investigate the association between different types of antidepressants and stroke risk in this population.

Funding [...] psychiatric or neurological disorders, antidepressants did not appear to relatively increase stroke risk in users compared to non-users (HR, 2.43; 95% CI, 0.65–9.14 and HR, 3.73; 95% CI, 0.45–30.92, respectively). However, in patients without psychiatric or neurological disorders, stroke risk was significantly higher in antidepressant users than in non-users (HR, 5.47; 95% CI, 2.58–11.61 and HR, 4.10; 95% CI, 2.12–7.95, respectively; Table 3). In the case of continuous antidepressant use, the [...] Therefore, caution should be exercised when applying our results to populations outside the studied age range. However, in our analysis of other age groups (Supplement Table S6) we found that although stroke risk was lower in patients aged over 45 years compared to younger adults, antidepressant use was associated with a significantly increased risk of stroke compared to non-users in these older groups. Future studies encompassing a broader and older age group are warranted to better understand [...] non-users, our findings demonstrated that stroke risk varied with the use of different antidepressant classes in adults aged 18–44 years; the initial treatment with TCAs was associated with the highest risk, followed by that with SARIs. However, the use of SSRIs, SNRIs, and other types of antidepressants was not associated with an increased stroke risk. Chan et al. only enrolled SSRI users and observed that stroke risk was highest in patients aged 20–39 years (HR, 5.15; 95% CI, 3.89–5.15).16 [...] Coupland et al. did not observe an association between an increased stroke risk and antidepressant use among patients aged 20–64 years (HR, 0.69; 95% CI, 0.10–4.69),36 which contrasts with our study’s findings. In addition, they only included patients with depression or anxiety,36 despite major depressive disorder, schizophrenia, bipolar disease, and dementia being established stroke risk factors in the literature.37, 38, 39, 40, 41 In our study, all new antidepressant