Title: Antidepressants: Which cause the fewest sexual side effects?
URL: https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/depression/expert-answers/antidepressants/faq-20058104
Content: Add a medicine to improve sexual function, such as sildenafil (Viagra), tadalafil (Cialis), vardenafil and avanafil (Stendra). These medicines are approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) to treat sexual problems in men only. Limited research suggests that sildenafil may improve sexual problems caused by antidepressants in some women. More information is needed to know that it works and is safe in women. [...] Wait a few weeks to see if sexual side effects get better.
   Adjust the dose of your antidepressant to lower the risk of sexual side effects. Always talk with your healthcare professional before changing your dose.
   Switch to another antidepressant that may be less likely to cause sexual side effects. [...] Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) citalopram (Celexa), escitalopram (Lexapro), fluoxetine (Prozac), paroxetine (Paxil) and sertraline (Zoloft). Paroxetine has the highest risk of sexual side effects.
   Serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI) venlafaxine (Effexor XR). [...] 1.   Bakr AM, et al. Pharmaceutical management of sexual dysfunction in men on antidepressant therapy. Expert Opinion on Pharmacotherapy. 2022; doi:10.1080/14656566.2022.2064218.
2.   Rush AJ. Major depressive disorder in adults: Initial treatment with antidepressants.  Accessed July 18, 2024. [...] Add a second antidepressant or another type of medicine to counter sexual side effects. For example, adding the antidepressant bupropion may ease sexual side effects caused by another antidepressant.
