Content: In addition to its role in repairing IR-induced or endonuclease-induced DSBs, the SMC5/6 complex has been shown to be important for repair of collapsed replication forks. During DNA replication, replication forks stall at sites of DNA damage . If the replication fork cannot proceed, it will eventually collapse, potentially generating a DSB . Previous studies suggest that one mechanism by which collapsed replication forks can be restarted is through HR . Hypomorphic alleles of [...] The SMC5/6 complex maintains rDNA integrity [...] The SMC5/6 complex is composed of two SMC family proteins, SMC5 and SMC6, as well as several non-SMC elements (NSE; Fig. 1) , , , , , , , , , . Although the primary sequences of SMC5 and SMC6 are more divergent than SMC1–4, these two proteins are speculated to form functional ATPases in an analogous manner, by intramolecular coiled-coil domains bringing together Walker A and B motifs (Fig. 2). SMC5 and SMC6 interact in their hinge region to form the core of 

The SUMO ligase MMS21/NSE2 [...] Components of the SMC5/6 complex were originally identified in a 1970s genetic screen for radiation sensitive mutations in fission yeast . Hypomorphic alleles of any of its components result in hypersensitivity to a broad spectrum of DNA damaging agents, such as ionizing radiation, ultraviolet radiation, methylmethane sulfonate (MMS), mitomycin C, and HU , , , , . In fission yeast, SMC6 is required for IR-induced DSB repair . Inhibition of the SMC5/6 complex does not [...] complex and determining their role in DNA damage repair. The E3 SUMO ligase MMS21/NSE2 has emerged as a critical component of the SMC5/6 complex in facilitating HR . In this review, I discuss the function of the SMC5/6 complex and its SUMO ligase activity in several HR-mediated cellular processes.
