Content: The present study used HRV to perform longitudinal observations of individual pregnant women and to investigate autonomic nervous system trends during the gestational period. The results indicate that during the period from 20 gestational weeks to 30 gestational weeks, there was increased sympathetic activity and decreased parasympathetic activity. This trend was maintained until 36 gestational weeks or immediately prior to delivery. Although our study subjects were normal pregnant women with [...] The present study used HRV to perform longitudinal observations of individual pregnant women to investigate autonomic nervous system trends during the gestational period. The results indicated that during the elapsed time from 20 gestational weeks to 30 gestational weeks there was increased sympathetic activity (increased VLF) and decreased parasympathetic activity (decreased HF); this trend was maintained until 36 gestational weeks or immediately prior to delivery. Prior researches has [...] Regarding autonomic nervous system activity in pregnant women, it has been reported that, compared with the non-pregnant phase, there is a decrease in sympathetic activity and increased parasympathetic activity during the first trimester; as the number of pregnancy weeks increases to the third trimester, this changes to increased sympathetic activity and decreased parasympathetic activity , , , , , , , , , , , . It has also been suggested that the increased sympathetic activity and decreased [...] •Pregnancy increased sympathetic activity and decreased parasympathetic activity. 
   •Anxiety during pregnancy decreased heart rate variability. 
   •Anxiety in second trimester pregnancy promoted a subsequent increase in sympathetic activity. 
   •Autonomic nervous activity may be the mechanisms underlying pregnancy complication. 
   •Our study suggest the need for early intervention for pregnant women with anxiety. 

Abstract

### Objectives [...] ### Conclusions

Anxiety during pregnancy decreased heart rate variability. Anxiety in second trimester pregnancy promoted a subsequent increase in sympathetic activity.

