Content: It has been suggested (Owman, 1981) that degeneration of uterine sympathetic nerves along pregnancy is a complex phenomenon, which might involve at least three controlling factors; (a) the hormonal environment of pregnancy; (b) local influences from the foetus and placenta; and (c) the mechanical stretching of the uterine wall produced by the growing foetuses. Plasticity in uterine sympathetic nerves was formerly explained as a selective effect of sex hormones on the system of ‘short’ adrenergic neurons supplying the uterus (Falk et al., 1975, Thorbert, 1978, Owman, 1981, Owman and Stjernquist, 1988). The finding that uterine-projecting sympathetic neurons demonstrate an enriched expression of ER-α (Zoubina and Smith, 2002) raised the possibility that, in addition to changes in the target availability of neurotrophic factors, sex hormones could regulate plasticity in uterine sympathetic nerves by altering the neuronal receptivity to neurotrophins.
