Content: Despite the abovementioned direct effects of ILC2 cells (via Met-Enk) and eosinophils (via IL-4) on precursors or even mature beige/brown adipocytes, M2 macrophages appear to be the main actors in the thermogenic activity of beige and (to a lesser extent) brown adipose tissues. It was initially proposed that M2 macrophages, recruited to BAT and WAT after cold exposure or another thermogenic stimulation, release norepinephrine (NE) to act on beige/brown cells and their precursors in a manner [...] In 2011, an unexpected finding was reported: non-pro-inflammatory, alternatively activated macrophages (M2 type) were proposed to be directly involved in promoting BAT thermogenesis (Nguyen et al., 2011). BAT activation elicited by cold exposure of mice was associated with increased expression levels of M2-phenotype marker genes (e.g., arginase 1, CD206 [Mrc1], or CD301 [Clec10a]) in tissue-resident macrophages and decreased levels of pro-inflammatory markers (iNOS, TNFα, and MCP-1). Using [...] (Rao et al., 2014). METRNL is secreted by adipose depots upon cold stimulus and induces both thermogenic genes and non-inflammatory type 2 cytokines in adipose tissue. These effects are not mediated through a direct interaction with brown adipocytes or M2 macrophages, but rather through resident eosinophils, whose abundance is increased by METRNL. In a second study, eosinophilicity in WAT depots was found to increase the non-inflammatory cytokines IL-4 and IL-13 as well as M2 macrophages [...] M1 macrophages exert negative effects on brown adipocyte activity and WAT browning by reducing local NE levels, repressing sympathetic innervation, and directly targeting adipocytes, whereas M2 macrophages exert positive effects. Other immune cells, such as eosinophils, ILC2 cells, and iNKT cells, positively influence WAT browning through activation of M2 macrophages or direct actions on white, beige, or brown adipocytes or precursor cells. Multiple cytokines and chemokines mediate the [...] In the last few years, several non-adrenergic mechanisms have been shown to induce WAT browning and BAT activation via molecules arising from the liver, skeletal muscle, and even heart (Villarroya and Vidal-Puig, 2013). It is not yet known whether M2 macrophages can secrete any of these molecules. Reitman (2017) specifically addressed this point and listed more than twenty putative candidates. Granneman and collaborators proposed that M2 macrophages promote the browning of adipose tissue
