β, a nuclear transcription factor, is a central player of sustaining a stable state of innate immunity in the gut. Disruptions and imbalances in the NF-κβ pathway lead to chronic inflammation, dysregulation of natural immune responses (6, 7), and altered immunity in IBD (8). Pathogenesis in both CD and UC is heavily marked by expression of multiple proinflammatory cytokines (9), many of which are regulated through NF-κβ. In fact, several of the key genes associated with IBD, such as
