Content: Fig. 1. RIPK1 induces the TNF-mediated NF-κB inflammation, necroptosis and apoptosis pathways. When TNF stimulates tumor necrosis factor receptor 1 (TNFR1), receptor-interacting Ser/Thr protein kinase 1 (RIPK1) is recruited to TNFR1 or TNFR1-associated death domain (TRADD) via its death domain (DD). TRADD recruits E3 ubiquitin (Ub) ligases, cellular inhibitor of apoptosis proteins (cIAPs) (cIAP1 and cIAP2) to RIPK1 via TNF receptor-associated factor 2 (TRAF2), and the linear ubiquitin chain [...] Considering the above studies, it is clear that RIPK1 can independently induce inflammation, possibly by activating NF-κB signaling, but the specific pathway is still not clear. Thus, elucidating how RIPK1 acts as both a friend and a foe, with complex functions, is critical for our understanding of certain pathological mechanisms, and continuing research is needed to determine its precise effects.

5. RIPK1 in inflammatory and autoimmune diseases [...] study of gene-knockout mice revealed that the scaffold structure of RIPK1, not its kinase activity, reduced intestinal epithelial cell apoptosis and necroptosis rates to inhibit inflammation in barrier tissues. It has also been found that lack of the RIPK1 scaffold function might lead to degradation of proteins recruited to complex I and inhibit NF-κB-mediated expression of TNF and the release of some cytokines, which eventually triggers cell death ( Hence, the RIPK1 scaffold is crucial in [...] transfer of the released NF-kB and then NF-kB activation. RIPK1 also mediates the activation of mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPKs), extracellular signal-regulated kinase (ERK), P38 and c-Jun N-terminal kinase (JNK), which are induced by TNF-α. When cells transition from a state of inflammation to death, A20 and cylindromatosis (CYLD) induce complex I degradation by deubiquitinating its components, forming complex IIa or complex IIb. Complex IIa includes the adaptor FAS-associated death [...] degradation of IkB. Thus, NF-kB is released and transferred to the nucleus, which in turn promotes transcription of many proinflammatory genes that mediate the inflammatory response. NF-κB can also prevent cell death by inducing the production of antiapoptotic genes, such as FLICEinhibitor protein (c-FLIP) and cIAPs. RIPK1 also mediates the activation of MAPKs, extracellular signal-regulated kinase (ERK), P38 and c-Jun N-terminal kinase (JNK), which are induced by TNF-α [27,54].
