Title: Reconstruction of the yeast Snf1 kinase regulatory network reveals ...
URL: https://www.embopress.org/doi/10.1038/msb.2009.67
Content: The yeast Snf1 regulates carbon metabolism during growth on various carbon sources (Celenza and Carlson, 1986; Carlson, 1999). Growing evidence, however, suggests a much broader role for Snf1 as a master regulator of both carbon and energy metabolism. Elucidating the organization and interactions of the Snf1 regulatory network is important for uncovering the complexity of global AMPK function and, ultimately, for using yeast as a model to study the role of AMPK in humans. To achieve this goal, [...] There is, however, growing evidence that suggests a much broader role of Snf1 as a master regulator of carbon and energy metabolism. Genome‐wide transcriptional profiling in yeast batch cultures has identified that active Snf1 is required for more than 400 of 1500 gene expression changes under glucose exhaustion (DeRisi _et al_, 1997; Young _et al_, 2003). At the level of protein interactions (BioGRID database) (Stark _et al_, 2006), Snf1 associates with 209 proteins, only 10% of which are [...] The yeast Snf1 regulates carbon metabolism during growth on various carbon sources (Celenza and Carlson, 1986; Carlson, 1999). In a complex with its regulator Snf4 and scaffolding protein Gal83, Snf1 regulates the usage of alternative carbon sources through the transcription factors (TFs) Mig1 and Cat8 (Schuller, 2003). Two other Snf1 scaffolding proteins Sip1 and Sip2 determine distinct Snf1‐substrate specificity, sub‐cellular localization and function (Vincent _et al_, 2001). [...] ### Snf1 regulates carbon metabolism—validity of our systems approach analysis [...] We first collected a global dataset for wild‐type _S. cerevisiae_ CEN.PK113‐7D and three Snf1 complex knockout mutants Δ _snf1_, Δ _snf4_, Δ _snf1_ Δ _snf4_ (Supplementary Table I) grown in triplicate in carbon‐limited chemostat cultivations at a fixed dilution rate _D=_ 0.100 h−1 (see Materials and methods). Abundances of gene, protein, and intracellular metabolites were quantified using Affymetrix GeneChip Yeast Genome 2.0 Arrays (Wodicka _et al_, 1997), multidimensional protein
Score: 0.8412138


Title: Mutations in SNF1 complex genes affect yeast cell wall strength
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0171933514000028
Content: Homologs of the _Saccharomyces cerevisiae_ SNF1 complex regulate energy metabolism in eukaryotic cells from fungi, plants and mammals and are generally known as AMP-activated kinases (AMPK; reviewed in Carling et al., 2012). In general, they form a trimeric complex composed of an α-subunit with protein kinase activity toward metabolic target proteins and transcription factors, a β-subunit, which serves as a scaffold for complex assembly and may determine intracellular localization of the [...] Despite the fact that central carbohydrate metabolism and cell wall polysaccharide biosynthesis should be linked in a yeast cell, little attention has been paid so far to the interactions between the regulatory circuits governing these processes in _S. cerevisiae_. We here show that the SNF1 kinase complex, a key regulator of yeast carbohydrate metabolism, also plays a role in the maintenance of cell wall integrity. Thus, the glucan layer of the cell wall is reduced by approximately 25% in [...] Baker's yeast (_S. cerevisiae_) is well known for its capacity to ferment a variety of sugars and to utilize a range of non-fermentable carbon sources, such as glycerol and ethanol (Gancedo, 1998). Derepression of the respective genes upon glucose depletion depends on the activation of the yeast SNF1 complex (SNF=_S_ ucrose _N_ on-_F_ ermenters; Carlson et al., 1981, Zimmermann et al., 1977) and serves as a paradigm for the functions in higher eukaryotes. The first mutants were used for cloning [...] Yeast _snf1_ mutants are defective in the release from carbon catabolite repression (CCR) upon a shift from fermentable to non-fermentable carbon sources (reviewed in Gancedo, 1998, Schüller, 2003). A variety of other functions have also been assigned to the yeast SNF1 complex: it takes part in the regulation of other nutrient responses, such as the initiation of meiosis, sporulation and aging (Ashrafi et al., 2000, Honigberg and Lee, 1998) and it is involved in stress responses to high salt, [...] In addition to the functions mentioned above, the SNF1 complex is also important in controlling glycogen metabolism. Thus, _snf1_ mutants fail to accumulate glycogen as a storage carbohydrate and have a drastically reduced level of glucose-6-phosphate in stationary phase cultures, suggested to account for the glycogen accumulation defect (Cannon et al., 1994, Huang et al., 1997, Thompson-Jaeger et al., 1991). However, logarithmically growing cells present only a slight reduction in the
