Content: End-stage kidney disease remains a significant public health concern globally.1 In 2020, more than 480,000 Americans were receiving dialysis, and 132,522 patients were newly registered as having end-stage kidney disease.2 In the same year, Medicare spent 7% ($50.8 billion) of its annual budget toward the care of patients with end-stage kidney disease, who constituted only 1% of Medicare beneficiaries. Despite such enormous expenditure, outcomes in patients receiving dialysis remain dismal. [...] The UNOS KPD program was launched in 2009 as a pilot program designed and run by members of the transplantation community, allowing it to be transparent, regulated, and accessible to all programs. In 2012, the UNOS KPD program was integrated into UNet, a system that supports national waitlisting and matching and placement of deceased-donor organs. This integration has paved the way for improved efficiency by automatic enrollment of pairs, viewing match results and potential living-donor [...] undergoing dialysis, and effectively narrows the gap between kidney supply and demand for transplant.5, 6, 7 [...] Enhance Participation by Overcoming Financial Hardship [...] Kidney transplant offers a significant survival advantage over dialysis and triples the life expectancy of many waitlisted patients.3 In 2022, nearly 140,000 patients were listed for a kidney transplant, of whom 26,309 received one, whereas approximately 9,000 patients were removed from the waiting list as a result of death or deterioration of their health status.4 Living-donor kidney transplant (LDKT) provides superior outcomes versus deceased-donor kidney transplant, reduces time spent
