Content: the diagnostic accuracy of CRP level for pneumonia in children presenting at an emergency department . Of the 687 children, 286 went through CRP measurement and chest radiography and 148 had pneumonia. The study found that higher CRP levels were related to a higher proportion of pneumonia and that CRP level was independently associated with pneumonia, even after adjusting baseline characteristics. However, low CRP levels did not exclude pneumonia. The study suggests that CRP could have a [...] and creatinine level of >1.1mg/dl), and trauma were excluded from the study. The exclusion criteria was set to eliminate factors that could confound CRP levels and affect the accuracy of the study. Patients with a history of surgical intervention and trauma were excluded due to the potential for surgery-induced or trauma-induced in␀ammation, which could falsely elevate CRP levels. Children with renal insu␀ciency were excluded because renal dysfunction can alter CRP metabolism, leading to [...] resource-poor settings. The study analyzed 31 observational studies and found that CRP and procalcitonin were the most effective in distinguishing bacterial from viral pneumonia. Their results demonstrated moderate accuracy with the though their accuracy was moderate, with sensivities from 60% to 85% and speci␀cities from 76% to 83%. CRP slightly outperformed procalcitonin. The study concluded that while CRP and PCT were useful diagnostic tools . Another study by Tarhani F et al. , evaluated [...] which supports its effectiveness as a diagnostic tool. However, the 9 false positives and 23 false negatives observed in the study indicate potential limitations in CRP diagnostic accuracy. Moreover, the lower speci␀city (52.94%) indicates that while CRP is useful in detecting pneumonia, it may not be su␀cient as a standalone marker. [...] D I S C U S S I O N
