Content: Cytokines signal those immune cells to fight the invaders. Your body’s cells release cytokines when there’s a threat. Cytokines can also signal your body’s cells to release more cytokines to increase your body’s inflammatory response. For example, an immune cell may detect a harmful substance in your body, like a virus, and release cytokines in response. Cells with cytokine receptors are located throughout your body. Having cells with cytokine receptors throughout your body allows inflammation to occur in widespread locations. The cells that make up your immune system, or immune cells, release most of your body’s cytokines. Still, many cells throughout your body — not just immune cells — can release cytokines. Immune cells that release cytokines include:
