Title: SNPs for disease prognosis | Nature Immunology
URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/ni.2775
Content: The clinical course and outcome of disease varies among affected people, but the effect of genetic variability on prognosis is poorly understood. In _Cell_, Lee _et al_. identify a noncoding, T-to-G single-nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) in the gene encoding the transcription factor Foxo3a (_FOXO3A_; SNP rs12212067). Although this SNP does not affect susceptibility to disease, it is associated with milder phenotypes of Crohn's disease and rheumatoid arthritis and a greater risk of severe malaria. [...] The minor _FOXO3A_ 'G' allele has a transcription rate up to twofold greater than that of the _FOXO3A_ 'T' allele and, consequently, results in faster reaccumulation of FOXO3 in the nucleus. Faster nuclear recovery of FOXO3 initiates a TGF-β-dependent pathway that diminishes the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF, IL-1β and GM-CSF and increases the production of anti-inflammatory IL-10 in monocytes. Polymorphisms in _FOXO3A_ have also been associated with human longevity. [...] Anyone you share the following link with will be able to read this content:

