Title: Five numbers that sum up China's one-child policy - BBC News
URL: https://www.bbc.com/news/magazine-34666440
Content: Since the one-child policy was introduced, infant girls have become more likely to die than boys.

In the 1970s, according to the United Nations, external, 60 males per 1,000 live births died under the age of one. For girls the figure was 53. In the 1980s, after the one-child policy became official, the rate for both was 36.

By the 1990s, 26 males per 1,000 live births died before the age of one - and 33 girls. The 2000s saw 21 boys per 1,000 live births dying and 28 girls. [...] Another expert, Cai Yong of the University of North Carolina, has suggested the one-child policy may have caused anxiety, prompting people to have children younger, which could increase the likelihood of having a second.

Newborn babies in hospital

