Content: Scan View;)

Childhood fractures may indicate bone density problems

Broken bones may seem like a normal part of an active childhood. About 1 in 3 otherwise-healthy children suffers a bone fracture. Breakage of the bone running from the elbow to the thumb side of the wrist (distal forearm fracture) is the most common. It occurs most often during the growth spurt that children typically undergo in early adolescence. [...] SPEAKER:Approximately one in three otherwise healthy children will suffer a fracture during growth.And fractures are the leading cause of hospital admission following injury in children.So there's a particular interest in whether children who suffer certain types of fractures have underlying skeletal deficits.And so working with the CCaTs at Mayo in the Clinical Research Unit,we performed a study looking at children and adolescents who had recently had a distal forearm fracture occurring at the [...] throughout life.And so again,if we could target these individuals who are suffering these mild trauma fractures during growth,which is a critical time when the bone is changing very rapidly,we could optimize the bone strength so that when they reach adulthood they're not at higher risk for subsequent fractures. [...] And we wanted to know whether these particular individuals had any unique skeletal deficit associated with their fracture.And so we compared them with gender matched controls who had no fracture history.And a previous study by Dr.Coastland colleagues here at Mayo actually showed that distal form fractures are the most common type of fractures suffered in children.And that the incidence of these types of fractures has actually accelerated over the past 30 years,suggesting that they are becoming [...] Refer a Patient
