Title: Tamoxifen Pathway, Pharmacokinetics - ClinPGx
URL: https://www.clinpgx.org/pathway/PA145011119
Content: The response to tamoxifen has a high degree of inter-individual variability, the cause of which may be due, in part, to differences in its metabolism in vivo [Article:[15159443](https://www.clinpgx.org/pmid/15159443)] (Figure 1). Tamoxifen is extensively metabolized predominantly by the cytochrome P450 (CYP) system to several primary and secondary metabolites, some of which exhibit more antiestrogenic effects in breast cancer cells than tamoxifen itself [Articles:[15159443](https://www.clinpgx.org/pmid/15159443), [21451508](https://www.clinpgx.org/pmid/21451508), [22041137](https://www.clinpgx.org/pmid/22041137)]. Because of this added effect, endoxifen is likely to be the primary metabolite responsible for the success seen in tamoxifen treatment [Article:[19244106](https://www.clinpgx.org/pmid/19244106)]. Observational studies examining the treatment of tamoxifen side effects with SSRIs first pointed to the role of CYP2D6 as a modulator of efficacy [Article:[14652237](https://www.clinpgx.org/pmid/14652237)].
