Title: Reactive Oxygen Species and Metabolism in Leukemia
URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9218220/
Content: The causes explaining oxidative stress in leukemic cells can be diverse, including activation of ROS production by oncogene signaling. _BCR-ABL_ is the prototypical leukemia-driving oncogene. BCR-ABL triggers ROS production, leading to an enhanced rate of mutagenesis and genetic instability (51). FLT3-ITD, another kinase involved in AML, could play a similar role (52). _RAS_ mutations occur frequently in leukemia, and are commonly associated with a dismal prognosis. Enhanced ROS production has [...] Enhanced mitochondrial ROS production has been also reported for some leukemic cells, such CLL (48). It has been suggested that mitochondrial ROS production enhances genomic instability in leukemic stem cells (LSC), allowing CML progression (44). Although additional studies are needed to fully understand the precise relevance of mitochondrial ROS in the leukemic process, damage to mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) by oxidative stress may be a key event. mtDNA is not packed in chromatin, which makes it [...] ### Figure 1.

