Content: Tirasemtiv entered clinical testing in 2009. The potential muscle booster is currently being evaluated as a treatment of a growing number of neuromuscular diseases including myasthenia gravis.

The drug, according to phase IIB results, also appears to significantly improve the strength of certain skeletal muscles in the elbows, wrists, knees and ankles. [...] The drug, originally known as CK-2017357 (CK-357), aims to make the most of deteriorating neuromuscular junctions (muscle-motor nerve connections) by amplifying the signals generated by motor neurons that ‘tell’ the muscles to move.

