URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9476939/
Content: The transcription factor EB (TFEB) lies at the junction of cellular metabolism and host defense against infection (10–13). Best characterized as a “master regulator of autophagy and lysosomal biogenesis,” TFEB was found recently to be activated by _Staphylococcus aureus_ and _Salmonella_ infection in mammalian macrophages (13–17). Indeed, TFEB (along with related transcription factor TFE3) was found to control a large part of the macrophage transcriptional response to infection (16). [...] The mechanism of TFEB activation in mammalian macrophages relies on an elegant signaling cascade requiring pathogen engulfment, reactive oxygen species (ROS), localized calcium release, and the phosphatase calcineurin (15, 16). Similarly in _C. elegans_, TFEB activation during infection with _S. aureus_ involved calcineurin and calcium release (15). Interestingly, neither of these mechanisms have involved the critical TFEB regulator mTORC1. Previously, we demonstrated that infection of [...] genes (12, 15). In macrophages, TFEB activation was required not only for the induction of the macrophage transcriptional response to infection but also for effective bacterial killing and clearance (15–17). Despite recent demonstrations of the critical role of TFEB in the induction of immunity, little is known about the roles of TFEB activation in the infected epithelium or nonimmune cells in general. [...] Following previous findings that, during infection, TFEB activation plays a key role in the transcriptional induction of the immune response, we performed an RNA sequencing (RNA-seq) experiment to determine the transcriptional roles of TFEB both at baseline and during infection in our cellular model. As we anticipated based on previous reports using other cellular systems (10, 12, 27, 43), we observed at baseline that TFEB-KO HeLa cells had a decreased expression of lysosomal (_LAMP1_, _RRAGC_, [...] In infected macrophages, the mechanism of TFEB nuclear import involves NOX/PHOS, ROS, calcium release, and calcineurin activation (15, 16). Thus, at least in macrophages, it appears that mTORC1 may not be a crucial player in TFEB activation during infection. In contrast, here, we demonstrate that in infected epithelial cells, mTORC1 inactivation caused by amino acid starvation was required for TFEB activation by invading bacteria. Additionally, membrane damage induced by invading bacteria was
Score: 0.8747745


