Title: Why Have Tuberculosis Rates Increased in England? - Medscape
URL: https://www.medscape.com/viewarticle/why-have-tuberculosis-rates-increased-england-2025a100028p
Content: ## Factors Driving the Increase

photo of Andy Whittamore

Dr Andy Whittamore, clinical lead at Asthma + Lung UK, linked the rise in TB cases in the UK to increasing poverty. ""With increasing poverty in the UK, particularly child poverty, the rise in tuberculosis cases is unfortunately not surprising,"" he told Medscape News UK. [...] The national rate of TB infection stands at 9.5 per 100,000 people. The number of people notified with TB increased in all regions in 2024 compared with 2023, with London seeing a 13.3% increase and the West Midlands an increase of 22.9%.

Paul Sommerfeld, executive trustee of the charity TB Alert, told Medscape News UK: ""Major urban areas are most affected. Around 40% of cases are seen in the London conurbation and much of the rest in big cities."" [...] The majority of people with TB in 2024 (81.5%) were born outside the UK, consistent with previous years. The TB notification rate in people born in the UK rose by 3.9% in 2024, compared with the previous year.

""GPs aren't likely to see any cases of TB unless they work in a certain area of a city which has a higher rate,"" Sommerfeld observed. In 2023, two local authority districts had a 3-year average notification rate above 40 per 100,000. These were Leicester City, and Newham in London. [...] Medscape Logo

# Why Have Tuberculosis Rates Increased in England?

Siobhan Harris

Medscape UK article logo

January 29, 2025

This article has been edited to include the most recent data on TB notifications published by the UK Health Security Agency on 30 January 2025.

Cases of tuberculosis (TB) in England increased by 12.9% in 2024 compared with 2023, continuing an upward trend seen over the last few years. [...] Provisional figures showed 5480 notifications of TB last year – up from 4850 in 2023, the UK Health Security Agency (UKHSA) said.

## Highest Risk Groups

The impact of poverty, migration, and the lingering effects of the COVID-19 pandemic are likely to be the biggest factors behind the rise, according to experts.

