Content: TB granuloma comprises various immune cell subsets of adaptive immunity (Fig. 1; Table 1). These include γδT-cells, CD4+, CD8+, mucosal-associated invariant T (MAIT) cells, CD1 T cells, Th17, Tregs, and other immune cells like NKT, DCs, macrophages, B-cells, neutrophils, and myeloid-derived suppressor cells . [...] Unconventional or non-classical CD8 T cells recognize antigens presented by molecules such as HLA-E, glycolipids associated with CD1, and MHC I related molecules including MR1 . These CD8 T cells play a key role in the host immune response. Within tuberculosis lesions, they are found in granulomas, where they express Perforin and Granulysin . Additionally, these cells secrete a range of cytokines, including IFN-γ, IL-17, TNF, IL-10, IL-2, and TGF-β . [...] Despite significant advances in research over the past century, Tuberculosis (TB) remains a formidable global health concern. TB granulomas are organized structures composed of immune cells, that serve as the body's primary defense against the spread of Mycobacterium tuberculosis (_Mtb_). The immune landscape of TB granulomas involves a complex array of immune cells, including CD4+ and CD8+T cells, B cells, NK cells, and others, which collectively influence the fate of the granuloma. B cells [...] The structural dynamics, resolution, and immune response of TB granulomas differ between humans and animal models . Human granulomas are typically more organized, persistent, and prone to fibrosis, whereas animal models, particularly mice, often exhibit faster resolution and simpler immune interactions. These differences highlight the limitations of animal models in fully replicating human granuloma biology. However, animal models remain essential for studying granuloma formation and [...] landscape of TB granuloma, focusing on the dynamic cellular interplay within the granuloma and its profound influence on disease pathogenesis.
