Title: Acquired resistance to tyrosine kinase targeted therapy: mechanism ...
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1368764624001341
Content: However, the emergence of acquired resistance accelerates treatment failure and disease progression(Jiao, et al., 2018; Camidge et al., 2014; Todsaporn et al., 2022). This resistance can arise through various mechanisms, including genetic mutations that alter the structure of the target kinase, activation of alternative signaling pathways that compensate for the inhibited kinase, and tumor heterogeneity leading to the emergence of resistant subpopulations(Yang et al., 2022; Cooper et al., [...] 2024). The evolution of TRK inhibitors exemplifies this trend. The first-generation TRK-TKIs like larotrectinib and entrectinib show efficacy in NTRK fusion-positive cancers, but acquired resistance soon emerged. This resistance manifests through SF mutations (e.g., TRKAG595R and TRKC G623R), mutations in the activation loop xDFG motif (e.g., TRKA G667C/S and TRKC G696A), and gatekeeper mutations (e.g., TRKA F589L)(Xiang et al., 2023; Kheder et al., 2018). To address these resistance [...] ALK kinase inhibitors provide another illustrative example of this evolutionary trend. Crizotinib, a first-generation ALK-TKI, was initially the treatment of choice for ALK-positive NSCLC. However, resistance mutations, including L1196M, C1156Y, and G1269A, emerged following crizotinib treatment(Cui et al., 2011; Dagogo-Jack et al., 2016). The second-generation ALK-TKIs, ceritinib and alectinib, demonstrated higher affinity but elicited new resistance mutations, predominantly at the solvent [...] The development trajectory of numerous TKIs, including those targeting EGFR and ABL, follows a similar pattern. Early-generation inhibitors typically targeted the ATP-binding pocket (type I and II inhibitors). However, as point mutations conferred acquired resistance, novel inhibitory mechanisms emerged(Jia et al., 2016). The evolution of fourth-generation EGFR-TKIs exemplifies this trend, with compounds like EAI045, JBJ‐04‐125‐02, and JBJ‐09‐063 exploiting allosteric sites, mirroring the [...] mutations, can induce conformational changes and site-blocking effects, leading to TKI resistance. For instance, TRKA G667C/A/S mutations specifically confer resistance against second-generation TRKA inhibitors, such as selitrectinib and repotrectinib, underscoring the clinical relevance of these mutations in drug resistance (Lu et al., 2020, Xiang and Lu, 2024).
