"
Vitamin D is an important factor in the relationship between calcium and parathyroid hormone.,1,"Title: Hyperparathyroidism and Vitamin D | Dr. Babak Larian
URL: https://www.hyperparathyroidmd.com/hyperparathyroidism-vitamin-d/
Content: Diagram showing the activation of vitamin D and calcium regulation in the body, involving the skin, liver, kidneys, parathyroid, bones, and intestines.

Hyperparathyroidism results when this system is disrupted:

As you can see, the relationship between hyperparathyroidism, vitamin D levels, and calcium is both intricate and essential to a healthy body. [...] Since vitamin D, calcium levels, and parathyroid gland function are intimately involved. To understand this relationship better, we have to first discuss how the body creates and activates vitamin D. There are two ways the body makes the chemical that ultimately becomes vitamin D: 

Both of these processes are usually working simultaneously for each of us. 

diagram of vitamin d, the skin, liver, and kidney

diagram of vitamin d, the skin, liver, and kidney [...] All in all, it is important to seek treatment for hyperparathyroidism, no matter your vitamin D level. The high level of calcium in the blood, caused by the overactive parathyroid glands, can have detrimental long-term effects on the kidneys, bones, muscles, and other organs. Many patients will live with hyperparathyroidism for quite some time without knowing they have the condition. When symptoms do present, most patients benefit greatly from the removal of the abnormal parathyroid gland(s). [...] Activated vitamin D3 enters the bloodstream and travels throughout the body. It affects the calcium balance in several key ways:

diagram of parathyroid and vitamin d interaction

diagram of parathyroid and vitamin d interaction

On a cellular level, vitamin D enters parathyroid cells and stops the production of PTH. It also promotes the production of a protein called p21. The p21 protein stops the parathyroid gland from duplicating and essentially helps keep the gland from growing. [...] A vitamin D deficiency is a condition in which an individual doesn’t have sufficient vitamin D in the body. When vitamin D levels are low, calcium absorption in the intestines decreases, which causes the blood calcium level to lower.  Consequently, the parathyroid glands become more active and produce more PTH. This causes calcium to come out of the bones, weakening them over time. A vitamin D deficiency may cause a wide range of health problems if left unaddressed for an extended period.
