Title: Parathyroid hormone and vitamin D: from bench to bedside
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1877132721000701
Content: PTH and vitamin D are intimately related in maintaining calcium and phosphate concentrations, forming a negative feedback loop working synergistically in response to drops in calcium levels and increases in phosphate. PTH, which is produced by the parathyroid glands, acts directly on the bone to mobilize calcium and phosphate stores and also on the kidneys to enhance calcium resorption and phosphate excretion. [...] Introduction

Parathyroid hormone (PTH) and vitamin D are crucial endocrine regulators primarily responsible for maintaining calcium (Ca 2+) and phosphate (PO₄³−) homeostasis. [...] Parathyroid hormone and vitamin D are essential endocrine regulators primarily responsible for maintaining calcium and phosphate homeostasis. Parathyroid hormone is responsible for the activation of vitamin D and forms a self-regulating negative feedback loop in response to changes in calcium and phosphate serum concentrations. This review discusses the synthesis, mechanisms of action the cellular and genomic targets of parathyroid hormone and vitamin D in both ‘calcaemic’ and ‘non-calcaemic’ [...] As outlined, the PTH/vitamin D axis has a significant role in bone development and health through calcium and phosphate homeostasis. It is therefore of particular importance to the orthopaedic surgeon in both the trauma and elective settings. [...] Vitamin D is primarily synthesized endogenously in the skin and activated by PTH within the kidneys. It can also be absorbed from the diet. The principal role of vitamin D is to increase calcium absorption within the gut.

PTH and vitamin D are said to have a ‘calcaemic’ effect which reflects their role in the homeostasis of blood calcium levels and ‘non-calcaemic’ which refers to their effect on tissues.
