Content: This can be quantitatively illustrated by examining decoding times for a hypothetical codon with a near-cognate tRNA abundance of 1 µM and a cognate tRNA abundance of 2 µM, to another codon with a near-cognate tRNA abundance of 5 µM and a cognate tRNA abundance of 10 µM. Although the cognate tRNA abundance differs by fivefold between these codons, the near-cognate:cognate ratio is identical. In consequence, the decoding times for these codons differ by <5% (computational models predict decoding [...] often facilitated by the modification of the first anticodon nucleotide (nucleotide 34 in standard tRNA structures). For example, editing of adenine to inosine enables decoding of codons that end in C, U or A by the same tRNA (Fig.4) , whereas uridines can base pair to A or G, and this ability can be controlled by a family of complex uridine modifications [37, 48]. [...] Codon decoding occurs when a charged cognate tRNA interacts with the codon in the ribosomal A-site, and the cognate codon:anticodon complex is recognised by the ribosome. The abundance of the different classes of tRNA and their competition for the ribosomal A-site determine how many tRNAs need to be rejected, and how long the rejection process takes, before such a cognate complex can form. At the most basal level, ribosome speed is, therefore, dependent on the processes that generate tRNAs [...] in HeLa cells and _C. elegans_ , consistent with slower decoding of such codons in vivo. [...] decoded codons. However, this only introduces minor delays into the decoding process compared to the long time required for tRNA selection. Importantly, however, wobble-decoded tRNAs also tend to leave the ribosome more frequently without peptidyl transfer having occurred, in which case the entire selection cycle has to be undergone once more. From the in vitro data, it can be calculated that tRNA Ala UGC has a 45% probability of being rejected while interacting with its cognate, wobble-decoded
