Content: MiR-142-5p is known as a multifunctional miRNA in multiple diseases (Shrestha et al., 2017). For example, studies have shown that it is involved in cancer progression and immunity in the tumor microenvironment (Jones et al., 2012; Jia et al., 2017; Yao et al., 2019; Yu et al., 2019; Yan et al., 2019; Cheng et al., 2019; Lou et al., 2017). In addition, miR-142-5p has been shown to play an important role in regulating the inflammatory response. Another study revealed that it suppressed the lung [...] MiR-142-5p, a mature form of miR-142, is reported to play multiple roles in cancer progression, antitumor immunity, immune disorders and inflammation (Jones et al., 2012; Jia et al., 2017; Yao et al., 2019; Yu et al., 2019; Yan et al., 2019; Cheng et al., 2019; Wei et al., 2019; Lou et al., 2017). A previous reported study on Hashimoto's thyroiditis, an autoimmune disease, indicated dysregulation of miR-142-5p (Zhu et al., 2016). Other studies showed that miR-142-5p is overexpressed in fever [...] the CDK5 transcription and translation processes by binding to its 3’-UTR region (Djuranovic et al., 2011; Nilsen, 2007). CDK5 has been shown to exert its effects on both central nervous system and non-central nervous system processes, CDK5 contributed to inflammation-induced thermal hyperalgesia (Fang-Hu et al., 2015). It is possible that miR-142-5p may regulates inflammatory cytokines secretion through the CDK5/MAPK/NF-κB inflammatory cytokines in pathway, thus further study is necessary. The [...] Cyclin-dependent kinase 5 (CDK5), a downstream target gene of miR-142-5p, is a special member of the CDK family of serine/threonine protein kinases compared with classic cyclins due to its unique regulatory pathway and atypical effects (Cortés et al., 2019). CDK5 has also been shown to play an important role in many processes by targeting more than 30 substrates in different diseases (Lenjisa et al., 2017; NavaneethaKrishnan et al., 2018; Zhang et al., 2017; Hsu et al., 2011; Liang et al., [...] The traditional standardized microscopic surgical treatment of AMEC includes canal-wall-down and canal-wall-up mastoidectomy. There is an urgent need for molecularly targeted drugs for patients with this condition. MiR-142-5p plays an important role in AMEC by directly targeting the CDK5-mediated regulation of the inflammatory response. However, miR-142-5p and CDK5 have a multiple-to-multiple relationship in a complex regulatory network, thus how to precisely regulate the expression of CDK5
