A20 Cell Line Derived Xenograft

A20 is a murine B lymphoma cell line that was derived from a BALB/c mouse with spontaneous reticulum cell neoplasms. It is commonly used as a model for studying B cell lymphomas and their response to various treatments. [...] Researchers have used the A20 cell line to study the molecular mechanisms of B cell lymphoma, including the genes and pathways involved in cancer cell growth and survival. The A20 cell line has also been used to test the efficacy of various cancer treatments, including chemotherapy, radiation therapy, and immunotherapy.

Overall, the A20 cell line has contributed significantly to our understanding of B cell lymphoma biology and has helped in the development of new treatments for this disease. [...] Why use Xenograft Models?
