Title: Alpha and Beta Thalassemia
URL: https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2009/0815/p339.html
Content: Persons with beta thalassemia major often die from cardiac complications of iron overload by 30 years of age. | Persons with beta thalassemia major require periodic lifelong blood transfusions to maintain hemoglobin levels higher than 9.5 g per dL (95 g per L) and sustain normal growth. | Persons with beta thalassemia major require chelation therapy for iron overload. If the synthesis from both genes is severely reduced or absent, the person has beta thalassemia major, also known as Cooley anemia. Persons with beta thalassemia major require periodic and lifelong blood transfusions to maintain a hemoglobin level higher than 9.5 g per dL (95 g per L) and sustain normal growth.2,15 The need for blood transfusions may begin as early as six months of age.
