URL: https://www.ecdc.europa.eu/sites/default/files/documents/variant-creutzfeldt%E2%88%92jakob-disease-annual-epidemiological-report-2020.pdf
Content: ECDC NORMAL SURVEILLANCE REPORT Variant Creutzfeldt−Jakob disease Annual Epidemiological Report for 2020 Key facts • No cases of variant Creutzfeldt−Jakob disease (vCJD) were identified in the EU/EEA in 2020. Following the identification in 2016 of the first case of chronic wasting disease (CWD) – a transmissible spongiform encephalopathy (TSE) that affects cervids (deer, elk and moose) in wild European cervid populations [11], six Member States (Estonia, Finland, Latvia, Lithuania, Poland and Sweden) began EU-mandated surveillance in cervid populations in 2020 as part of a three-year targeted surveillance programme [12]. Although TESSy supports data collection of vCJD cases, continued human and animal surveillance at the national and EU level is important to monitor all forms of CJD and other human prion diseases in order to identify possible sources of public health risk.
