Content: tandem arrangement and that this determines editing patterns . Moreover, ADAR1 forms heteromeric complexes; for example, with Dicer . Using BioID followed by mass spectrometry, Freund and colleagues determined the interactomes of ADAR1 and ADAR2 . Among many other proteins, this analysis identified a family of DZF-domain-containing factors, including ILF3, ILF2, STRBP, and ZFR. ILF3 interacts with both ADAR1 and ADAR2 in an RNA-dependent manner and acts as a global negative regulator of RNA [...] In addition to the dsRNA substrate, protein–protein interactions impact ADAR1’s activity. ADAR1 forms homodimers via protein–protein interactions involving its third dsRBD, a process that is RNA independent but regulates editing activity [19., 20., 21., 22., 23., 24.]. It is possible that two ADAR1 monomers forming a homodimer bind and edit two RNA substrates independently or the same substrate cooperatively. Another study suggests that multiple ADAR1 molecules simultaneously bind dsRNA in a [...] ADAR1 has been described to regulate cancer progression by editing transcripts from oncogenes and tumour suppressor genes, endogenous dsRNAs, and miRNAs. This in turn can either promote or suppress cancer cell proliferation. For instance, ADAR1-mediated editing of the antizyme inhibitor 1 (AZIN1) mRNA results in the incorporation of a different amino acid, increasing AZIN1’s binding affinity to antizyme . This inhibits the tumour suppressive role of antizyme, thereby promoting cancer cell [...] ### Tumour cells require ADAR1 to escape antitumour immunity

Cancer cells often show high mutation rates and employ various mechanisms to evade the host’s antitumour immune responses. One such mechanism involves hijacking the factors responsible for maintaining immune system balance and homeostasis. ADAR1 is one of these factors and controls the activation of innate immune responses to pathogenic RNAs or endogenous RNAs that resemble viral RNAs. [...] ADAR1-mediated editing occurs in both coding and noncoding RNA substrates, with varying efficiencies across species . ADAR1’s editing activity is modulated by various factors. ADAR1 exhibits a sequence preference based on the flanking sequence of edited As, favouring A or U as the 5′ nearest neighbour (A = U > C > G) . While stable dsRNA regions are crucial for ADAR1 binding and activity, selective editing has been observed in dsRNAs with bulges and mismatches . Recent work suggests that ADAR1
Score: 0.5221784


