Content: Another potential regulatory mechanism of peripheral _Aire_ expression is found in murine keratinocytes, where _Aire_ expression seemed to be dependent on expression of the intermediate filament protein keratin 17 (K17). Interestingly, expression of multiple K17-dependent target genes in tumor-prone keratinocytes (e.g. CCL19, CCL22, CXCL5, CCR7) is also _Aire_-dependent within mTECs, although expression of _Aire_ itself is not K17-dependent within the thymus. Within the tumor-prone [...] Furthermore, in mice it has been discovered that certain lymph node stromal cells residing in the lymph node cortex can express _Aire_. Strikingly, these cells also expressed specific TRA, including antigens from the eye, intestine, pancreas, liver, central nervous system and skin. Not only did these cells express the mRNA of the antigen, but this was also translated into full length proteins that could potentially be used for antigen presentation and tolerance induction in a similar way as in [...] isolated from the inflamed synovium of RA patients . Furthermore in human oral squamous cell carcinoma, AIRE-expressing cells have been identified  and _AIRE_ mRNA was detected in the PC3 prostate cancer cell line . The putative functions of AIRE in these cells and pathological conditions will be discussed in more detail later. [...] Based on these different observations, it is possible that eTACs represent not just one cell type, but that AIRE expression is present in multiple cell types with different origins. This is backed-up by several murine studies pointing towards a stromal origin of certain eTACs, as these cells were found to lack CD45 expression [9,24]. However, one study did show that many markers shared with antigen presenting cells, including MHCII were present on these AIRE expressing cells . In contrast, many [...] Beside secondary lymphoid tissues, other tissues may also harbor AIRE-expressing cells. When investigating human peripheral blood, Suzuki and colleagues found that B cells (defined as CD19+) and cells from the monocyte/macrophage lineage (CD14+) expressed low levels of AIRE mRNA and protein, whereas T cells (defined as CD65−CD14−CD19−) showed no positivity . Under pathological circumstances, AIRE-expression has also been identified in stromal cells, namely fibroblast-like synoviocytes (FLS)
