Title: Higher Risk of Kidney Failure Associated With Angiotensin ...
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0002934325003146
Content: Findings from multiple randomized controlled trials (RCTs) have demonstrated that the inhibition of the renin-angiotensin system (RAS) with either angiotensin-covering enzyme inhibitors (ACEIs) or angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs) reduce the risk of kidney failure in patients with chronic kidney disease.1., 2, 3, 4 In RCTs of renin-angiotensin system inhibitors (RASI) in patients with heart failure, the RASI group had worsening kidney function but kidney failure was not examined as an [...] ### Conclusion

In patients with heart failure, ARBs (vs. ACEIs) are associated with a higher risk of incident kidney failure. These findings need to be confirmed in future clinical trials.

Introduction [...] The findings from our study demonstrate that in patients with heart failure initiated on RASIs, when compared with ACEIs, ARBs were associated with a significantly higher risk of kidney failure, which was attenuated during more recent years. The higher risk of kidney failure was homogeneous across clinically important subgroups including by race, EF, eGFR and RASI dosages. Although there was a small but significant lower risk of death in the ARB group, this was not observed in the subgroup [...] Conclusions

The findings of our study suggest that in patients with heart failure initiated on RASIs, the receipt of ARBs versus ACEIs was associated with a higher risk of kidney failure, which was similar regardless of race, EF, eGFR or doses. Although ARB group had a lower associated risk of death, this was not observed in the high-doses subgroup, doses that are associated with improved clinical and kidney outcomes.8,9,28,29 These findings need to be confirmed in future RCTs. [...] low-dose and high-dose subgroups. The associated risk of death was 5% (95% CI, 3–7%) lower in the ARB group, which was only significant in the low-dose group (7% vs 0%; interaction p, 0.007).
