Content: As for articles between January 1st, 2019 and December 31st, 2020 (followed up until June 28th, 2022), our study confirms that OA is significantly and independently associated with higher citation counts in Journal of Hepatology. Thus, authors who want to increase the chances of their work in Journal of Hepatology being cited, would be better off publishing in OA. The reason behind this could be that OA articles are available quickly and easily, owing to their free access without subscription [...] A total of 322 original articles were included in this study, of which 56 were OA and 266 were subscription access articles. The main characteristics of these articles are shown in Table 1. OA articles were significantly more frequently cited than subscription access articles (67.86% vs. 45.49% in the citation-high group, p = 0.002). In addition, OA articles have more international collaboration (62.50% vs. 43.23%, p = 0.009) and more authors (p = 0.003). Other metrics (number of months online [...] restriction, which increases the readership of articles and may increase their probability of being cited. This study also found that OA articles have more authors and more international collaboration. It may be speculated that teams consisting of more authors or even from different countries are willing to afford the cost of OA publishing and produce high-quality OA articles to boost the influence of their work. In addition, clinical articles tend to gather more citations than [...] and dentistry4

