Content: Increasing evidence has demonstrated that PI(3,4)P 2 influences cell migration and invasion by regulating focal adhesion turnover, integrin trafficking to and from invadopodia/podosomes and their stability [∗∗47, ∗48, ∗49]. Given that Class I PI3K inhibitors attenuate numerous small G-protein GEFs and GAPs, it is tempting to speculate that PI(3,4)P 2 may have a specific role in regulating a subset of G-proteins necessary for actin dynamics [50,51]. Focal adhesions are large macromolecule [...] Clathrin-mediated endocytosis (CME) is the most prevalent form of endocytosis in most mammalian cells and can either be constitutive (e.g. transferrin receptor) or stimulated (e.g. receptor endocytosis) . CME is a multistep process that culminates of the release of endocytic carriers from the plasma membrane and the uncoating of the clathrin coat. Before clathrin-coated vesicles undergo fission from the plasma membrane, PI3KC2α is required to generate PI(3,4)P 2 at the neck (i.e. the pit–plasma [...] In breast cancer cells, focal adhesions are regulated by the levels of PI3KC2α . Specifically, increased expression of this kinase in a subset of breast cancer cell lines correlated with decreased focal adhesion stability, higher rates of migration and invasion ability . [...] Cell migration also depends on efficient trafficking and recycling of integrins to and from the plasma membrane. A recent study demonstrated that Class I PI3K and SHIP2 produced PI(3,4)P 2, which is required for the endocytosis of β3 integrins from the invadopodia . Snx9 serves as the vital PI(3,4)P 2 effector and leads to the recruitment of dynamin 2 at the trailing edge of cells. The PI(3,4)P 2-rich membrane protrusions then recruit the effector proteins AKTIP (AKT-interacting protein) and [...] 43.   43D.Y. Graham, M.F. Go Helicobacter pylori: current status  Gastroenterology, 105 (1993), pp. 279-282, 10.1016/0016-5085(93)90038-e90038-e) View PDFView articleView in ScopusGoogle Scholar 
