Content: CX3CR1-deficient mice restored the cardinal features of asthma, and CX3CR1-blocking reagents prevented airway inflammation in CX3CR1-deficient recipients injected with WT T(H)2 cells. We found that CX3CR1 signaling promoted T(H)2 survival in the inflamed lungs, and injection of B cell leukemia/lymphoma-2 protein (BCl-2)-transduced CX3CR1-deficient T(H)2 cells into CX3CR1-deficient mice restored asthma. CX3CR1-induced survival was also observed for T(H)1 cells upon airway inflammation but not [...] 2 cell survival in a mouse model of asthma. Chemokines are important for accumulation of inflammatory cells in the lung in diseases such as asthma. In this report, the authors show that the chemokine receptor CX3CR1 is critical to the development of airway inflammation in a mouse model of asthma. CXC3CR1-deficient mice and mice receiving CXC3CR1-blocking reagents had significantly impaired airway hyperresponsiveness, cellular infiltrate and Th2 cytokine production. Adoptive transfer of [...] Th2 cells rescued this defect in cell survival. Th2 survival may not be the whole story, however, as mice receiving CXC3CR1-blocking reagents underwent a very short protocol that would not appear to be significantly affected by T cell survival. Regardless, CXC3CR1 signaling may represent an important mechanism for maintenance of Th2 inflammation in asthma and is thus a potential therapeutic target. [...] CX3CR1/CX3C ligand 1 (CX3CL1) in the pathogenesis of allergic airway inflammation and asthma. Utilizing either Leishmania homologue of receptors for activated C-kinase (LACK) or ovalbumin as antigens for sensitization and challenge in a murine model, the authors show that CX3CR1 is selectively expressed in T helper (TH)2 lymphocytes within the lung and bronchoalveolar lavage fluid (BALF) only after sensitization and challenge. Through a number of transgenic studies, the authors further [...] Agrawal D and Agrawal V: H1 Connect Recommendation of [Mionnet C et al., Nat Med 2011 