Content: and immune counterregulatory cytokines (e.g., IL-10), recruitment of regulatory cells (regulatory T cells), and activation of the nervous system. Our results show that CX 3 CR 1+ AMs, a subset of pulmonary macrophages, have a pivotal role in the resolution of eosinophilic allergic lung inflammation. In allergic inflammation, eosinophils have a significant deleterious impact by releasing highly toxic products that promote inflammation, tissue damage, airway remodeling, and organ dysfunction. [...] (Figure 6F) (22, 37). Despite belonging to the eotaxin family, CCL26 has only approximately 35% sequence homology to the other two eotaxins (CCL11 and CCL24) and exerts different biologic functions (50, 51). CCL26 is 10-fold less potent than CCL11 (eotaxin-1) in activating the eotaxin receptor CCR3 (23, 24). Whereas CCL11 and CCL24 act in the earlier phase of Th2 inflammation, CCL26 is induced at a later phase after the peak of Th2 inflammation by the cytokines released by Th2 cells in a [...] revealed that CX 3 CR 1+ macrophages also reside in the tissues of the lung, skin, and joints at steady and diseased states, and they have highly sophisticated functional specifications in the tissues (10–12). Some tissue-resident CX 3 CR 1+ macrophages suppress the initiation of inflammation and act as negative regulators of local inflammation, quite different from the proinflammatory phenotypes of macrophages. In addition, depletion of CX 3 CR 1+ tissue macrophages resulted in the [...] conditions (9). Our data show that CX 3 CR 1+ macrophages are a unique subset of AMs (Figure 1). Tissue-resident CX 3 CR 1+ macrophages have a highly sophisticated functional specification under steady and diseased states (10–12). Some tissue-resident CX 3 CR 1+ macrophages suppress the initiation of inflammation and act as negative regulators of local inflammation, playing a key role in maintaining immune homeostasis (40). These functions are diametrically opposed to those of the [...] Depletion of CX 3 CR 1 Delays the Clearance of Tissue Eosinophilia in Allergic Lung Inflammation
