Content: CX3CR1 has been shown to have a critical role in airway inflammation via its effect on T-cell survival.20 In mice sensitized and challenged with a range of allergens, Cx3cr1 gfp/gfp knock-in mice showed reduced infiltration of inflammatory cells into the lung, less mucus production, and better lung function compared with WT Cx3cr1+/+ littermate controls. This effect was due to CX3CR1 expression on CD4+ Th2 cells, which was critical to the development of airway disease. CX3CR1 was shown to be [...] Taken together, these data demonstrate a role for CX3CR1 in Th1 and Th2 cell survival in inflamed airways but not under homeostatic conditions. It remains to be seen whether CXC3R1 contributes to T-cell survival in other disease states, for example, atherosclerosis, in which CD4 T cells (mainly Th1) have been shown to accelerate lesion progression.21 No studies have addressed whether fractalkine can regulate the survival of human T cells that express CX3CR1 on both CD4+ and CD8+ subsets. [...] dispensable for Th2 cell migration to the lung but conferred an essential survival signal to these cells. Furthermore, enforced survival of Cx3cr1 gfp/gfp Th2 cells by transduction with the antiapoptotic protein Bcl-2 and transfer into Cx3cr1 gfp/gfp recipients restored lung inflammation. CX3CR1 was also shown to induce survival of Th1 cells during airway inflammation. [...] Mionnet C, Buatois V, Kanda A, Milcent V, Fleury S, Lair D, Langelot M, Lacoeuille Y, Hessel E, Coffman R, Magnan A, Dombrowicz D, Glaichenhaus N, Julia V. CX3CR1 is required for airway inflammation by promoting T helper cell survival and maintenance in inflamed lung. _Nat Med_. 2010;_16_:1305–1312.

