Content: Investigators sought to compare the cost-effectiveness of standard treatment (12-week monotherapy) with enhanced varenicline (extended varenicline treatment or nicotine replacement therapy [NRT] in combination with varenicline) use among individuals trying to quit smoking. Point prevalence abstinence of 7 days at 52 weeks (biochemically confirmed) was the primary outcome. [...] The incremental cost-effectiveness ratio (ICER) was $4681 per individual who quit smoking in the 12-week varenicline monotherapy group with $4579 per quality-adjusted life-year (QALY) added. The ICER for 24-week varenicline combination therapy relative to 12-week monotherapy was significantly higher per additional individual who quit smoking. [...] Among all participants (mean [SD] age, 49.1 [11.9] years; 54.0% women; 69.3% White, 22.9% Black), 881 (70.4%) completed the 52-week follow-up. The investigators found smoking cessation through follow-up was similar between groups (25.1% 12-week monotherapy; 24.4% 24-week monotherapy; 23.6% 12-week combination therapy; 25.1% 24-week combination therapy). Adherence varied by treatment group, highest in 12-week monotherapy (44.1%), lowest in 24-week combination therapy (30.2%). [...] Study limitations included COVID-19 restrictions prohibiting some participants from biochemically verifying abstinence at 52 weeks, significant nonadherence, and significant numbers of participants lost to follow-up (13.4%) or withdrawal (9.4%). [...] varenicline monotherapy (n=315); 24-week varenicline monotherapy (n=311); 12-week NRT patch combined with varenicline (n=314); or 24-week NRT patch combined with varenicline (n=311). Participants were blinded to treatment; all participants received 24 weeks of varenicline pills and NRT patches, some of which were active and some placebo, depending on assignment group. All participants received 6 counseling sessions (3 face-to-face, 3 via phone, 15 minutes each).
