Content: Crosstalk between macrophages and innate lymphoid cells (ILCs) in diseases - ScienceDirect Crosstalk between macrophages and innate lymphoid cells (ILCs) in diseases Innate lymphoid cells (ILCs) and macrophages are tissue-resident cells that play important roles in tissue-immune homeostasis and immune regulation. Under the influence of the transcription factors ID2, NFIL3, TOX and so on, early ILC precursors (EILP) can differentiate into NK cell precursors (NKPs) cells or into CHILPs (common helper innate lymphoid progenitors), which themselves give rise to LTiPs (lymphoid tissue inducer progenitors) and ILCPs (innate lymphoid cell precursors). Activated ILC2s then recruit other immune cell infiltrates, including eosinophils and M2 macrophages, which in turn induce airway hyperresponsiveness (AHR) and airway inflammation, , .
