Content: The dexamethasone group had a 2.5% (3/119) rate of postoperative bleeding, while the rate was 1.6% (2/119) in the control group. No patients required multiple operations for control of bleeding. The degree of postoperative pain (2.1±0.5 vs 3.4±0.9) and the occurrence of postoperative nausea (21% vs 31.9%), as well as vomiting (15% vs 24.4%) in the dexamethasone group, was significantly lower compared with the placebo group.

### Conclusions [...] Our study first reported dexamethasone use in TT. We found that the rate of postoperative bleeding between the dexamethasone group and nonsteroid group had no significant difference, and no children required multiple operations for control of bleeding, suggesting the high safety of dexamethasone use in TT. Moreover, dexamethasone use improved postoperative pain, nausea, and vomiting significantly, which was consistent with the studies in the TE.

