Content: Forkhead box protein O3a (FOXO3a) are an evolutionarily conserved family of transcription factors distinguished by a DNA-binding domain consisting of three α-helical, two ring-shaped, and “wing-shaped” domains, involved in regulating cellular function and survival within the nervous system, particularly in relation to oxidative stress response, neuronal death, and autophagy [16,17]. FOXO3a is found at high levels in the cerebral cortex and hippocampus, and activation of FOXO3a in acute neuronal [...] injury (e.g., ischemic stroke and traumatic brain injury) induces cell death, lipid peroxidation, and neuroinflammation [18,19]. It has been reported that activated FOXO3a can directly bind to HIF-1α to inhibit the expression of HIF-1α-dependent genes and promote the accumulation of lipid peroxidation . Moreover, other study revealed that FOXO3a is involved in ROS production, and inhibition of GCN2 alleviates apoptosis and endoplasmic reticulum stress during cerebral ischemia by reducing FOXO3a [...] can significantly improve neuronal injury after SAH . Solute carrier family 7 member 11 (SLC7A11) is an important component of amino acid transporters in the phospholipid bilayer and part of an essential antioxidant system . P53 can inhibit cystine absorption and glutathione (GSH) levels by inhibiting SLC7A11 expression, leading to reactive oxygen species (ROS) accumulation and iron death . Therefore, the specific mechanism of SLC7A11 in EBI after SAH deserves further exploration. [...] dependent ROS production . Therefore, we speculated that FOXO3a may be involved in the ferroptosis process of neurons, but its specific role remains unclear. Preliminary experiments have revealed that FOXO3a can bind to the BAP1 promoter and regulate the expression of BAP1. Therefore, we speculate that the transcription factor FOXO3a regulates the level of BAP1 and affects the ferroptosis of neurons after SAH. [...] However, the activity of FOXO3a protein is modulated by a diverse array of post-translational modifications, such as phosphorylation, ubiquitination and acetylation . These alterations influence the subcellular distribution and functional activity of the FOXO3a protein. The nuclear FOXO3a protein plays a transcriptional regulatory role as a transcription regulator, while the cytoplasmic FOXO3a protein is inactive and susceptible to 26S proteasome degradation . However, FOXO3a is inactivated in
