Content: In recent years, a novel mechanism in budding yeast preventing the accumulation of free histones and which is superimposed upon the regulation of histone gene transcription and mRNA stability has been described (#pgen.1000964-Gunjan2). This mechanism involves the use of the DNA damage checkpoint protein kinase Rad53 as part of a surveillance process that not only monitors the accumulation of excess histones, but also induces their degradation. This degradation is controlled by phosphorylation [...] As we mentioned in the Introduction, Rad53 is involved in the detection and subsequent degradation of excess histones without becoming phosphorylated and independently of its role in the DNA damage checkpoint (#pgen.1000964-Gunjan2). Since FACT is involved in chromatin transactions during transcription, we hypothesized that the dysfunction of Spt16 might cause an increase in free histones, which would need to be targeted for degradation by Rad53. The mutations lowering the H2A–H2B dosage have [...] of the evicted histones, which become toxic to the cell if not targeted for degradation by Rad53. [...] and is carried out by the proteosome in an ubiquitylation-mediated manner (#pgen.1000964-Singh1). Excess histones are thought to be generated at the end of the normal S-phase or in response to an abrupt decrease of DNA synthesis following DNA damage. This mechanism is dependent on neither the checkpoint kinases Mec1 and Tel1 nor other DNA-damage checkpoint proteins (reviewed in (#pgen.1000964-Singh2)). [...] 32°C, the double spt16-197 rad53K227A mutant was unable to grow at this temperature (Figure 4A). Similar results were obtained with the complete deletion of RAD53 (Figure S3). Unlike what happens following DNA damage, Rad53 was not hyperphosphorylated in the spt16-197 mutant at a restrictive temperature (Figure 4B). Hence we conclude that Rad53 kinase activity is required to alleviate the deleterious effects of the spt16-197 mutation, irrespectively of the role it plays in the DNA damage
