URL: https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/health-and-medicine/glycolysis
Content: During glycolysis, glucose is transformed into two three-carbon molecules known as pyruvate, resulting in a net gain of two ATP molecules, which are crucial for cellular energy. This process is fundamental not only for energy production in various cells, including muscle and red blood cells, but also plays a significant role in conditions such as oxygen debt during strenuous activity, where lactate is produced through fermentation when oxygen is limited. **Definition:** The chemical process of splitting a molecule of glucose to obtain energy for other cellular processes; at times of intense activity, glycolysis produces most of the energy used by muscles The two ATPs produced per glucose molecule used are simply not enough to supply the energy needs of most human cells.
