Content: In recent years, high‐sensitivity cardiac troponin (hs‐cTn) assays have tremendously impacted the diagnostic pathway of patients with suspected acute myocardial infarction (MI) without ST elevation.1 This is due to their increased sensitivity and small coefficient of variation (eg, 10%) at concentrations at or below the 99th percentile upper reference limit in the general population, allowing an accurate detection of modest elevations and changes in serial testing.2, 3, 4 [...] Upon interpreting our analysis, the following aspects need to be considered. We adjudicated our final diagnosis based on the Third Universal Definition of MI, which simplifies the diagnosis in case of symptomatic troponin elevations, but does not emphasize the diagnoses of chronic or acute myocardial injury.3, 7 Our definition of rising and falling patterns is narrow on purpose and based on 2 measurements only. Overall, it excludes all patients with a stable hs‐cTn who would also qualify for a [...] Although rising and falling troponin patterns are equally considered by high‐sensitivity cardiac troponin–based 0/1‐ and 0/3‐hour diagnostic algorithms for the diagnostic management of patients with suspected acute myocardial infarction, the positive predictive value for acute myocardial infarction was significantly reduced in patients with a falling troponin pattern compared with those with a rising troponin pattern.

• [...] A key concept in the definition of acute myocardial injury is that the dynamic change in hs‐cTn over time may have either a rising or a falling troponin pattern. This builds on the idea that any event resulting in an acute myocardial injury will lead to a dynamic troponin release. Thus, an event that surpasses the actively damaging phase would still be detectable shortly thereafter when the hs‐cTn concentration decreases again.3 [...] The introduction of hs‐cTn assays significantly reduced the time period between presentation of a patient and the decision to rule out or rule in MI.1, 4, 10, 14 Overall, faster decision making may allow an earlier initiation of specific therapies in cases of rule‐in, but also an earlier discharge and/or the initiation of further diagnostic workup for differential diagnoses in cases of rule‐out.3, 4 Accordingly, the 2020 ESC guidelines recommend the use of an accelerated 0/1‐ or 0/2‐hour
