Content: Nuclear receptors (NRs) are a diverse and essential class of transcription factors that regulate gene expression in response to small molecule ligands ( These ligands include hormones, lipids, vitamins, and other endogenous or exogenous signaling molecules, allowing NRs to modulate a wide range of physiological processes, including metabolism, development, immune responses, and cell differentiation ( ( By acting as molecular switches, they translate chemical signals into changes in gene [...] NRs are ligand-dependent transcription factors that regulate gene expression by recognizing specific hormone response elements (HREs) in the promoter or enhancer regions of their target genes. These include elements such as estrogen response elements (EREs), glucocorticoid response elements (GREs), thyroid hormone response elements (TREs), and peroxisome proliferator response elements (PPREs) ( ( Upon ligand binding, NRs undergo a conformational change in their LBD, particularly exposing the [...] This conformational shift facilitates the selective recruitment of coactivators—such as the SRC (steroid receptor coactivator) family, CBP/p300, and P/CAF, which possess histone acetyltransferase (HAT) activity ( ( ( These coactivators acetylate histones and promote chromatin relaxation, enabling RNA polymerase II to access DNA and initiate transcription. Some coactivators also promote transcription via methylation or ubiquitination of histones or non-histone substrates. [...] In the absence of ligands, or when antagonists are present, NRs often adopt a repressive conformation. In this state, the AF-2 surface facilitates the recruitment of corepressors such as NCoR (nuclear receptor corepressor) and SMRT (silencing mediator of retinoic acid and thyroid receptors) ( ( ( These corepressors in turn recruit histone deacetylases (HDACs), leading to chromatin condensation and transcriptional repression. This cofactor switch mechanism, between activation and repression, is [...] Upon ligand binding, NRs usually undergo conformational changes. The AF-2 region in the LBD becomes more affinity-rich, allowing recruitment of a range of coactivators. Examples include the SRC family (SRC-1, SRC-2, SRC-3), CBP/p300, and p/CAF, which are multifunctional transcriptional complexes ( ( These complexes have histone acetyltransferase (HAT) activity and interact with multiple components of the transcription initiation complex, enhancing the transcription efficiency of target genes.
