Title: Glutamate: What It Is & Function
URL: https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/22839-glutamate
Content: Glutamate # Glutamate Glutamate is the most abundant excitatory neurotransmitter released by nerve cells in your brain. ### What is glutamate? Glutamate is recycled and made by glial cells in your brain. Neurotransmitters, like glutamate, are made by nerve cells and are stored in thin-walled vesicles called synaptic vesicles located at the axon terminal, which is at the end of each nerve cell. * Too much glutamate is released by nerve cells. Too much glutamate in the brain can cause nerve cells to become overexcited. Some neurodegenerative diseases associated with having too much glutamate exciting nerve cells include Parkinson’s disease, Alzheimer’s disease and Huntington’s disease. Problems in making or using glutamate have been linked to mental health disorders including autism, depression and schizophrenia.
