Title: Human T cell development, localization, and function throughout life
URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5826622/
Content: In this review, we discuss how human T cells develop and provide essential immune protection at different life stages and highlight that tissue localization and subset delineation are key determinants of the T cell functional role in immune responses. In early life, newly generated naïve T cells and Treg cells populate major lymphoid and mucosal sites in the body and memory T cells begin to develop largely in mucosal sites such as small intestine and lung (Thome et al., 2016a). We will discuss these multiple aspects of human T cell responses starting from T cell development, the role of naïve and regulatory T cells, differentiation to effector and memory subsets and how tissue localization impacts T cell function and maintenance. [DOI] [PubMed] [Google Scholar%20versus%20effector%20memory%20and%20terminally%20differentiated%20memory%20cells%20in%20CD8(+)%20compartment&author=P%20Saule&author=J%20Trauet&author=V%20Dutriez&author=V%20Lekeux&author=JP%20Dessaint&volume=127&publication_year=2006&pages=274-281&pmid=16352331&doi=10.1016/j.mad.2005.11.001&)] doi: 10.1016/j.cell.2015.03.031. [DOI] [PubMed] [Google Scholar%20T%20Cells,%20Facilitating%20Protection%20from%20Local%20Cytomegalovirus%20Infection&author=JT%20Thom&author=TC%20Weber&author=SM%20Walton&author=N%20Torti&author=A%20Oxenius&volume=13&publication_year=2015&pages=1125-1136&pmid=26526997&doi=10.1016/j.celrep.2015.09.082&)] doi: 10.1016/j.cell.2014.10.026.
