Title: Heart Failure Prevalence Expected to Continue to Increase ...
URL: https://www.thecardiologyadvisor.com/news/heart-failure-prevalence-expected-to-continue-to-increase-by-2050/
Content: The prevalence of HF in women is 1.9%, compared with 2.7% in men. Lower rates were observed in women vs men among those aged 40 to 59 years (1.2% in women vs 2.3% in men) and 60 to 79 years (4.2% in women vs 7.5% in men). The prevalence is higher in women aged older than 80 years (10.9% in women vs 7.15% in men). HF prevalence increased from 1999 to 2004 in women (4.9% to 8.0%) and men (6.2% to 12.2%) based on NHANES data. [...] Based on data from the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) for 2011 to 2012 and 2017 to 2020, a downward trend was observed in HF prevalence over time in adults aged 80 years and older and those aged 70 to 79 years. An increased prevalence occurred in younger age groups, specifically those aged 20 to 39, 40 to 59, and 60 to 69 years, which had the highest increases since 2016. [...] HF prevalence was highest among non-Hispanic Black individuals followed by non-Hispanic White and Mexican American individuals. Heart failure was more prevalent in younger and middle-aged Black adults compared with younger and middle-aged White adults. The age-adjusted HF prevalence has reportedly increased for Black men (38.1/1000 person-years [PY]) and Black women (30.5/1000 PY) compared with White men (20.7/1000 PY) and White women (15.2/1000 PY) from 2005 to 2014. [...] The lifetime risk for HF was about 25% based on data from 1990 to 2014 from the Framingham Heart Study cohort. Overall, HF incidence varies according to age, comorbidities, sex, race, and ethnicity, as well as methodology and time frame.

HF occurs mostly frequently in adults aged 60 years and older. The risk for HF is increased about 20-fold for individuals aged older than 60 years compared with those younger than 60 years. [...] In the United States, the northern Great Plains area and Western states have a low HF prevalence, and the Midwestern and Eastern states have the highest prevalence.

HF mortality rates have increased since 2012, especially from 2020 to 2021. For US individuals aged 65 years and older hospitalized for HF, the estimated 1-year postdischarge mortality rate is 35%. In this population at 30 days postadmission and 30 days postdischarge, the estimated mortality rates are 7% and 5%, respectively.
