n the diagnosis, [...] of HF in men remained unchanged, whereas in women it dropped from 31% to 40%. Nevertheless, this study only included the white population and there might have been some bias in selecting a more privileged population with better access to preventive and therapeutic measures. The increase in the use of antihypertensive therapy, with the resulting decrease in the prevalence of hypertension, could favorably affect the incidence of heart failure in women even more than men. In men, the higher
