Content: either PTEN or INPP4A/B. PTEN and INPP4A/B dephosphorylate PI(3,4)P 2 into PI(4)P and PI(3)P, respectively. b. PI(3,4)P 2signal transduction is initiated on the plasma membrane (PM) by receptor tyrosine kinases. PI(3,4)P 2 distribution is heterogeneous on the PM, which may be explained by signalling localised to clathrin-coated pits (CCPs). However, EGFR signalling is the. only RTK in which this has been verified. Following RTK stimulation on the PM, Class I PI3K converts PI(4,5)P 2 into PIP 3, [...] removing the 3-phosphate and generating PI4P . Alternatively, 4-phosphatases INPP4A and B can convert PI(3,4)P 2 into PI3P [12,13]. These degradation mechanisms may be significant in the endocytic pathway as PI3P and PI4P mark different endosomal populations [11,14, 15, 16]. Furthermore, it highlights the importance of maintaining PI(3,4)P 2homeostasis, as INPP4B and PTEN are known tumour suppressors [4,13,17]. [...] The levels of PI(3,4)P 2 remain low even during stimulation. For reference, in resting cells, PI(3,4)P 2 is≈10-fold more abundant than PIP 3; however, this is still ≈40-fold less abundant than PI4,5P 2, which itself is modest compared to phosphatidylinositol and other bulkphospholipids [4,9,10].  The levels are strictly controlled due to the actions of 3- and 4-phosphatases. The phosphatase PTEN is widely known for converting PIP 3 into PI4,5P 2, but it also attenuates PI(3,4)P 2 signalling by [...] subunit (p85α, p85β, p55α, p55γ, or p50α) are classified as Class IA PI3Ks, while those that complex with p87/p101-type regulatory subunits are classified as Class IB PI3Ks. The conversion of PI(4,5)P 2 to PI(3,4)P 2 is also mediated by bacterial effector proteins SopB and IpgD. However, the precise mechanism is uncertain and may involve inter- or intra-molecular molecular transfer of phosphate. For instance, either a permutation of phosphotransferase and phosphatase activity (PI(4,5)P 2 ->PIP [...] 3 ->PI(3,4)P 2) or by phosphoisomerase activity (PI(4,5)P 2 ->PI(3,4)P 2). Phosphatidylinositol (PI) can also be converted to PI(3,4)P 2 by the sequential action of PI4K and Class II PI3K. PI is phosphorylated into PI(4)P, which can then be further phosphorylated into PI(3,4)P 2 by Class II PI3K. There are three isoforms of Class II PI3K: Class II PI3Kα and Class II PI3Kβ are ubiquitously expressed, whereas Class II PI3Kγ is expressed primarily in hepatocytes. PI(3,4)P2 can be degraded by
