Macropinocytosis contributes to a cell's supply of amino acids via the intracellular uptake of protein.,1,"Title: Is macropinocytosis more than just a passive gulp?
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0955067425000511
Content: Macropinocytosis is thought to be essentially nonselective, and used to scavenge extracellular macromolecules, including amino acids, proteins, cell debris, extracellular vesicles, lipids, and nucleotides . However, recent evidence suggests that macropinocytosis could contribute to the uptake of a subset of transmembrane receptors bound to extracellular cargoes [∗4, 5, 6] regulating their intracellular trafficking, signaling, and function. [...] Macropinocytosis is a form of fluid-phase uptake mediated by actin-based membrane ruffles (macropinocytic cups) and generates 0.2–5 μm large intracellular vesicles (macropinosomes). Macropinosomes eventually fuse with lysosomes where their content is degraded by lysosomal proteases to generate biomolecules that serve as building blocks for the cell . Macropinocytic cups are formed upon activation of RAC1 and/or CDC42 GTPases, which promote actin polymerization through the suppressor of cyclic [...] 7.   7C. Commisso, S.M. Davidson, R.G. Soydaner-Azeloglu, S.J. Parker, J.J. Kamphorst, S. Hackett, E. Grabocka, M. Nofal, J.A. Drebin, C.B. Thompson, _et al._ Macropinocytosis of protein is an amino acid supply route in Ras-transformed cells  Nature, 497 (2013), pp. 633-637 CrossrefView in ScopusGoogle Scholar [...] cancer cells deprived of amino acids can also use PAK1-dependent macropinocytosis to internalize ECM components. Lysosomal degradation of ECM proteins increased the intracellular amino acid content, particularly of tyrosine and phenylalanine, to support growth, migration, and invasion . Of note, ECM internalization can apparently be independent of degradation by matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) . In agreement, some melanoma cells use amoeboid cell migration to generate paths through dense ECM, [...] Apart from large extracellular proteins, biomolecules such as amino acids and lipids can be internalized by macropinocytosis to fuel metabolism (Figure 1c). For example, inhibition of the tumor suppressor Ataxia-Telangiectasia mutated (ATM), in nutrient-deprived ovarian cancer cells induced macropinocytosis of branched-chain amino acids (BCAA) potentially driven by mTOR inactivation . Macropinocytosis is also involved in the trafficking of lipids and lipid particles (Figure 1d).
