Title: Macropinocytosis: Biology and mechanisms
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2667290121000474
Content: 28.   Commisso et al., 2013C. Commisso, S.M. Davidson, R.G. Soydaner-Azeloglu, S.J. Parker, J.J. Kamphorst, S. Hackett, E. Grabocka, M. Nofal, J.A. Drebin, C.B. Thompson, J.D. Rabinowitz, C.M. Metallo, M.G. Vander Heiden, D. Bar-Sagi Macropinocytosis of protein is an amino acid supply route in Ras-transformed cells  Nature, 497 (2013), pp. 633-637 CrossrefView in ScopusGoogle Scholar [...] As Lewis speculated, it is now clear that many cancers use macropinocytosis to supplement their diet of small molecules (Lewis, 1937; Commisso et al., 2013; Commisso, 2019). This is possible because proteins, and especially serum albumin, form the great majority of the biomass in human serum, far exceeding glucose and free amino acids (Palm, 2019). Macropinocytosis also allows cancer cells to consume necrotic cell debris (Kim et al., 2018), which can be abundant in tumour environments. This [...] When growth factors stimulate cells, they also trigger a burst of macropinocytosis, which causes proteins from the medium to be delivered to lysosomes and digested. The resulting amino acids are sensed and help to activate TORC1, in cooperation with the protein kinase Akt, whose activation occurs through the cytoplasm (Yoshida et al., 2015; Swanson and Yoshida, 2019). The purpose of this baroque procedure may be to ensure that TORC1 only pushes cells towards growth when there are sufficient [...] T-cells may also feed to some extent by macropinocytosis, since they are strongly macropinocytic, especially after activation, and the protein taken up is digested and required for their growth (Charpentier et al., 2020). It seems inevitable that other cells with high macropinocytic activity, such as macrophages, must also utilize ingested proteins as food, despite the existence of a dedicated retrieval pathway for serum albumin and IgG (Toh et al., 2020). [...] In fact, macropinocytosis has not been entirely abandoned in the gut as a way of absorbing food. Many invertebrates only partially digest their food, and electron microscopy suggests that enteric phagocytes of the gut take up the resulting fragments by phagocytosis and macropinocytosis, so that digestion can be completed intra-cellularly (Hartenstein and Martinez, 2019). This is not known in mammals, except possibly in newborns, where gut enterocytes use both receptor-mediated and fluid phase
Score: 0.6867278


