Content: Interferon gamma (IFNγ), the prototypic cytokine associated with Th1 immune responses, was formerly considered essential for the development of autoimmune disease (Billiau and Matthys, 2009). Yet, in some well-studied animal models, IFNγ has been shown to possess protective functions. Mice deficient in IFNγ (IFNγ−/−) or its receptor, IFNγR, are hypersusceptible to a wide variety of autoimmune disease models (Chu et al., 2000, Matthys et al., 1999, Tarrant et al., 1999), including experimental [...] protective qualities extend to suppressing autoimmune inflammation. Experimental autoimmune myocarditis (EAM) was initiated in BALB/c mice by immunization with myocarditogenic peptide. During EAM, activated cardiac NK cells secreted interferon γ, perforin, and granzyme B, and expressed CD69, tumor necrosis factor–related apoptosis-inducing ligand treatment, and CD27 on their cell surfaces. The depletion of NK cells during EAM with anti-asialo GM1 antibody significantly increased myocarditis [...] Defects in lymphocyte apoptosis in IFNγ−/− mice have been reported in several autoimmune and infectious disease models, and have been hypothesized to mediate protective functions (Chu et al., 2000, Tarrant et al., 1999). IFNγ−/− CD4+ T cells are resistant to activation-induced cell death, through an autonomous mechanism dependent on the regulation of the proximal proapoptotic enzyme caspase 8 (Refaeli et al., 2002). Inducible nitric oxide synthase (iNOS, _Nos2_) is one of the canonical [...] ### Cell Death Differ. (2004) 
