Content: PF-03732010 (Pfizer, New York, NY, USA) is a monoclonal antibody directed against CDH3. Based on preclinical results, the proposed mechanism of action of this drug is inhibition of tumor growth and metastatic potential via reduction in β-catenin and suppression of mesenchymal type changes to the cytoskeleton. Patients with any tumor demonstrating overexpression of CDH3 on biopsy were eligible for inclusion in the study. Forty-three patients were enrolled. Dosing started at 0.5 mg/kg [...] 79%) experienced likely treatment-related adverse events, with 13 of those patients experiencing adverse events of grade 3 or higher. The most common adverse event was thrombocytopenia, which occurred in 15 patients, six of whom had platelet levels less than 50 k/µL. Other common adverse events included elevated aspartate aminotransferase (AST) in 11 patients, elevated alanine aminotransferase (ALT) in nine patients, and nausea in 10 patients. After evaluation of DLTs, MTD was determined to be [...] HKT288 (Novartis Pharmaceuticals, Basel, Switzerland) is an ADC directed against CDH6 that entered phase I trials in 2016. HKT288 combines a humanized IgG1 antibody to a maytansinoid payload.[45,46] The proposed mechanism of action is similar to PCA062. Disruption of microtubule dynamics by maytansinoids should ultimately result in arrest of mitosis and secondary cell death. For this initial trial, five patients with papillary or clear-cell RCC and four with serous OC were enrolled. Dose levels [...] patients and began in January of 2016. Any solid malignancy refractory to all other treatments was eligible. Dosage was measured by radioactivity ranging from 5–25 mCi/m 2 based off modeling for expected off-target radiation to the lungs. Patients received an ∼10-mL injection once every 4 weeks at a rate of 1 mL/min. Half-life of the active antibody-isotope complex was approximately 33.4 hours. The half-life of the naked antibody and of free 90 Y after isotope disassociation was approximately [...] initiated in December 2020. Patients with renal cell carcinoma (RCC) and (OC) were eligible. Initial dose escalation evaluated safety and efficacy of the drug as monotherapy, starting at 1.6 mg/kg once every 21 days. Doses were then escalated by 1.6 mg/kg until patients reached a maximum dosage of 9.6 mg/kg. Interim data were presented on part 1a of the trial in November 2021. At that point the study had enrolled 22 patients, including 15 with OC and the remainder with RCC. One patient each
