The major cause of death in patients with prostate cancer is progression from androgen-dependent disease to castration-resistant disease. A new study has shown that this resistance is associated the expression of N-cadherin. Therapeutic targeting of N-cadherin might delay the progression of prostate cancer, Tanaka and colleagues report. [...] In a gene-expression analysis of xenografts and tumor samples, N-cadherin expression was notably higher in castration-resistant prostate cancer than in androgen-dependant disease. Moreover, the researchers found that N-cadherin expression in androgen-dependent cell lines seemed to encourage invasion, metastasis and progression to castration-resistant prostate cancer.

