Content: 47.   ^Ait-Khaled M, Rakik A, Griffin P, Cutrell A, Fischl MA, Clumeck N, Greenberg SB, Rubio R, Peters BS, Pulido F, Gould J, Pearce G, Spreen W, Tisdale M, Lafon S and CNA3003 International Study Team. Mutations in HIV-1 reverse transcriptase during therapy with abacavir, lamivudine and zidovudine in HIV-1-infected adults with no prior antiretroviral therapy. Antivir Ther 2002. [...] susceptibility (e.g., the 5-fold reduction observed with K70R alone) are considered to be clinically significant likely because AZT is an intrinsically less potent NRTI in vivo. [...] . It usually occurred in combination with two or more of the following four accessory mutations: A62V, V75I, F77L, and F116Y (141,155,156,157). In this setting Q151M confers high-level resistance to AZT and ABC, and intermediate resistance to 3TC, FTC and TFV (20,62). Q151L is an extremely rare highly unfit NRTI-selected mutation. Although it does not appear to reduce NRTI susceptibility, it represents a transition between wildtype and Q151M (158). [...] 17.   17.017.117.217.3Whitcomb JM, Parkin NT, Chappey C, Hellmann NS and Petropoulos CJ. Broad nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitor cross-resistance in human immunodeficiency virus type 1 clinical isolates. J Infect Dis 2003.
