URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2211124724001682
Content: Nr5a2, an orphan nuclear receptor,12 is widely studied in liver metabolism and ovarian functions13,14,15,16,17,18,19,20,21 and in maintaining pluripotency in mouse embryonic stem cells (ESCs). It is known to activate or maintain Oct4 gene expression in mouse embryos’ epiblast and undifferentiated ESCs.22,23 Previous studies report that Nr5a2, in tandem with Esrrb, parallels the importance of core factors Oct4 and Sox2 in ESCs self-renewal.24 The redundancy of the transcription factors Esrrb and [...] different cell fates (Figure 7). Our research highlights the importance of Nr5a2’s DBD and LBD in early mouse embryo development, suggesting potential ligands during early developmental phases. Notably, Nr5a2’s sumoylation sites, critical in reprogramming processes,26 showed less activity, highlighting different operational mechanisms in early mouse embryos compared to iPSCs reprogramming. [...] Nr5a2 contributes to the robust reestablishment of pluripotent gene regulatory networks post-mitosis.25 Nr5a2 is also crucial in establishing induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs).26 However, Nr5a2’s regulatory effect on Oct4 expression in embryos’ ICM is not fully understood. Earlier studies using Nr5a2 inhibition indicated developmental arrest at the 2-cell stage, highlighting Nr5a2’s role in early mouse embryonic development.27 Contrarily, subsequent studies showed that Nr5a2 knockout [...] Nr5a2, like other nuclear receptors, possesses a crucial ligand-binding domain (LBD) and DNA-binding domain (DBD).30 As an orphan nuclear receptor, its specific endogenous ligands are yet unidentified.31,32 Previous studies emphasized Nr5a2’s sumoylation site’s importance in reprogramming.26 Investigating Nr5a2’s functional significance, we introduced deletions and point mutations to create mutants: M1 (lacking amino acids 120–193 in the DBD), M2 (lacking amino acids 340–550 in the LBD), and M3 [...] and showed compromised viability (Figures 2G and 2H). Additionally, 2-cell embryos from both groups were transplanted into the fallopian tubes of pseudo-pregnant mice, followed by an analysis of embryonic implantation sites at E5.5. The results indicated a significantly lower implantation rate in the SI group compared to the control group (Figures 2I and 2J). These findings underscore Nr5a2’s crucial role in ICM formation and its impact on blastocyst developmental potential.
