Content: Release of neutrophil extracellular traps (NETosis) is a unique form of neutrophil cell death in which granule contents are extruded into the extracellular space along a mesh-like network or net. NETs consist of dsDNA, citrullinated histone peptides and azurophilic granule proteins such as MPO and neutrophil elastase . Functionally, NETs form as an anti-microbial defence mechanism, trapping bacteria and removing them from the circulation; however, a range of stimuli have been shown to induce [...] There is good evidence from _in vitro, in vivo_, and clinical studies that ANCA are pathogenic (Table 1). Clinically, treatments which remove ANCA or decrease its production are effective, and there is one reported case of maternal-foetal transfer of antibodies resulting in vasculitis in the newborn ( ( ( Many studies have shown that, _in vitro_, ANCA bind to and activate neutrophils, leading to cytokine production, degranulation, neutrophil extracellular trap formation (NETosis), and reactive [...] Once ECs and neutrophils interact, EC injury is induced by a variety of mechanisms including NETosis, degranulation, and serine protease release. There may also be a role for other mechanisms of EC injury in AAV although these remain controversial. Some studies have shown that MPO and PR3, released from neutrophils can bind directly to EC acting as planted antigens, particularly in an inflammatory environment . Circulating ANCA can then bind to these antigens resulting in EC injury. Non- MPO, [...] compared to healthy individuals, with no difference between patients with active disease and those in remission . _In vitro_, stimulation of neutrophils with ANCA IgG or serum from patients with AAV can induce NETosis [98,103,105]. Interestingly, one study has shown that NET formation in AAV can occur independently of ANCA; AAV serum depleted of IgG or IgA was still able to induce NETosis _in vitro_, suggesting other humoral factors may be present . The mechanisms by which NETosis occurs in AAV [...] NETs including PMA, bacteria and immune complexes . Classical NETosis occurs by ROS dependent pathways in which intracellular ROS leads to the release of neutrophil granule proteins, migration of proteases to the nucleus, activation of PAD4 leading to histone modification of chromatin, and expulsion of DNA through the disrupted plasma membrane ( ( ( ( Vital NETosis, in which there is release of chromatin and mitochondrial DNA following neutrophil activation without cell death, is more
