Content: The findings suggest that regular yoga practice over six months can enhance cognitive function, particularly in areas such as working memory, inhibition, and attention, potentially offsetting the adverse cognitive effects of prolonged sedentary behaviour. This has implications for improving workplace productivity and employee well-being. Further research with larger sample sizes and extended follow-up periods is recommended to understand better the long-term effects and underlying mechanisms of [...] The results of our six-month yoga intervention study demonstrate significant improvements in cognitive function among desk-based workers, particularly in areas of executive function such as working memory, inhibition, and attention. These findings not only align with previous research on the cognitive benefits of yoga practice but also extend our understanding of the potential long-term effects of regular yoga practice on cognitive performance in a sedentary workforce. [...] Moreover, the progressive improvement in cognitive function observed over the six-month period suggests that sustained, long-term yoga programs may be more beneficial than short-term interventions. This has implications for the design and implementation of workplace wellness initiatives, suggesting that ongoing, consistent programs may be more effective than sporadic or short-term offerings.

8. Conclusion [...] Additionally, the physical activity component of yoga may play a role in cognitive enhancement. Chandrasekaran et al. proposed potential physiological mechanisms by which breaking up prolonged sitting could improve cognitive functions ( These mechanisms include improved cerebral blood flow, reduced inflammation, and enhanced neurotrophic factor production, all of which could contribute to the cognitive benefits observed in our study.

