Title: Induced pluripotent stem cells-podocytes promote repair in acute ...
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0009279723002016
Content: Acute kidney injury (AKI), defined by a rapid increase in Scr and/or decrease in urine output, occurs in about 10%∼15% of patients admitted to the hospital, while its incidence is more than 50% in patients in the intensive care unit . Despite an increasing incidence of AKI and developing insights into the causes and mechanisms of the disease, there are few preventive and therapeutic options . Macrophages are innate immune cells that play an important role in all steps of kidney injury, and the [...] 2025, Translational Research  Show abstract Acute kidney injury (AKI) is increasingly being recognized as a systemic disorder associated with significant morbidity and mortality. AKI manifests with extensive cellular damage, necrosis, and an intense inflammatory response, often leading to late-stage interstitial fibrosis. Although the mechanisms underlying renal injury and repair remain poorly understood, macrophages (pivotal inflammatory cells) play central roles in AKI. They undergo [...] polarization into pro-inflammatory and anti-inflammatory phenotypes, contributing dynamically to both the injury and repair processes while maintaining homeostasis. Macrophages modulate microenvironmental inflammation by releasing extracellular vesicles (EVs) containing pro- or anti-inflammatory signaling molecules, thereby influencing the regulation of tissue injury. The injured tissue cells release EVs and activate local macrophages to initiate these responses. Our bibliometric analysis [...] ### Macrophage-driven inflammation in acute kidney injury: Therapeutic opportunities and challenges [...] Graphical abstract

