Title: Comparative analysis of HPV testing versus cytology in ...
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0090825824011375
Content: The WHO now recommends high-risk human papillomavirus (hrHPV) testing as the primary screening method for cervical cancer . Evidence indicates that HPV testing leads to earlier detection of high-grade cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN), ultimately preventing more cervical cancers . HPV tests are more sensitive than conventional cytology . However, studies have shown that the specificity of an HPV test is lower than cytology. This raises concern that HPV-based screening could lead to [...] Our pilot implementation study in Denmark unveils the impact of HPV-based primary cervical screening. Analyzing data from initial and subsequent rounds, we find HPV screening detects nearly double the high-grade cervical lesions compared to cytology. However, this is accompanied by a significant increase in colposcopy referrals. Our research also highlights the early detection capacity of HPV screening for cervical cancer. These findings inform future screening strategies for improved disease [...] •Pilot implementation study in Denmark on HPV-based primary cervical screening. 
   •HPV screening detects nearly double the high-grade cervical lesions compared to cytology. 
   •Significant increase in colposcopy referrals observed with HPV screening. 
   •HPV screening shows strong early detection capacity for cervical cancer. 
   •Findings will guide future screening strategies for better disease management and prevention. 

Abstract

### Objective [...] The findings of this extensive pilot implementation study show that HPV-based cervical cancer screening detects more precancerous lesions and cancers earlier than cytology, However, the higher detection comes at a cost of higher colposcopy referral rates. Longer follow-up is needed to confirm if HPV testing leads to a lower cumulative incidence of cervical cancer compared to cytology screening. The stronger prognostic value of a negative hrHPV test compared to a negative cytology suggests that [...] The first round of screening showed higher colposcopy referrals in the HPV group (6.4%) compared to cytology (2.3%), with HPV screening detecting more CIN3+ (_n_=306/28,677 (1.1%)) than cytology (_n_=241/43,622 (0.6%)) (RR=1.9 (95%CI 1.6–2.3). In absolute terms, 41 additional colposcopy referrals resulted in the detection of 10 more cases of CIN2+ and five extra CIN3+ cases per 1000 women screened. The PPV for CIN3+ of a colposcopy initiated by routine screening was similar in the HPV group
