Title: Two-component regulatory system
URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Two-component_regulatory_system
Content: Although two-component signaling systems are found in all domains of life, they are most common by far in bacteria, particularly in Gram-negative and cyanobacteria; both histidine kinases and response regulators are among the largest gene families in bacteria. Two-component systems accomplish signal transduction through the phosphorylation of a response regulator (RR) by a histidine kinase (HK). (By contrast, eukaryotic kinases are typically easily identified, but they are not easily paired with their substrates ""Substrate (chemistry)"").) A database of prokaryotic two-component systems called P2CS has been compiled to document and classify known examples, and in some cases to make predictions about the cognates of ""orphan"" histidine kinase or response regulator proteins that are genetically unlinked to a partner. ""Histidine kinases and response regulator proteins in two-component signaling systems"". ""Histidine kinases and response regulator proteins in two-component signaling systems"".
