Content: Innate immune responses are required for the control of West Nile virus (WNV) infection (reviewed in references 17 and 54). Type I IFN induction is essential to the host response against WNV, as mice lacking the IFN-α/β receptor (_IFN-αβR_−/−) are vulnerable to WNV infection, with expanded tissue tropism, uncontrolled viral replication, and rapidly uniform death (55). WNV triggers type I IFN production in different cell types through processes involving RIG-I, MDA5 (27), and IPS-1 (17, 65), [...] marrow-derived macrophages and dendritic cells between 24 and 72 h after infection (Fig. 4 A and B). Analogous results were observed in macrophages pretreated with a neutralizing IFN-β MAb prior to infection (data not shown); thus, _IFN-β_−/− cells do not have intrinsic antiviral defects associated with development in the absence of IFN-β in mice. Somewhat smaller (3- to 7-fold) yet still significant (_P_< 0.05) increases also were detected in _IFN-β_−/− MEF and cerebellar granule cell neurons [...] ruffling and reduced activity. Whereas wild-type mice showed an ∼60% survival rate and a mean time of death of 12.3 days after WNV infection, _IFN-β_−/− mice were more vulnerable, with an ∼5% survival rate and a mean time of death of 10.2 days (_P_< 0.0001) (Fig. 1). A similar increase in lethality was observed after WNV infection when mice were pretreated with a neutralizing anti-IFN-β monoclonal antibody (MAb) but not isotype control MAb (data not shown). Although a genetic or acquired [...] To begin to investigate the cell-specific function of IFN-β in restricting WNV infectivity, we compared multistep growth kinetics in several different wild-type and _IFN-β_−/− primary cells, including macrophages, dendritic cells, fibroblasts, and two types of neurons, after WNV infection at a low MOI (0.001 to 0.01). In myeloid cells an absence of IFN-β resulted in enhanced WNV replication. For example, 8- to 30-fold (_P_< 0.05) increased WNV titers were observed in _IFN-β_−/− bone [...] IFN-β restricts pathogenesis of several viruses in mice. Increased lethality or viral burden was observed in _IFN-β_−/− mice after infection with vaccinia virus (18), influenza A virus (39, 46), coxsackie B virus (19), Sindbis virus (10), and Friend virus (28) but not with La Crosse virus (5).
