Content: An improper balance of regulatory/effector T (T reg/T eff) cells is central to the development of autoimmune diseases, including type 1 diabetes. We previously showed that low-dose interleukin 2 (IL2) induced T reg cell expansion and activation and clinical improvement in patients with hepatitis-C-virus-induced vasculitis. We aimed to establish which low doses of IL2 would be safe and induce T reg cells in patients with type 1 diabetes, considering that: (1) type 1 diabetes might be linked to [...] Strong evidence links the IL2 pathway and T reg cells to pathogenesis of type 1 diabetes: several susceptibility genes and proteins are directly connected to the IL2 pathway,11 including IL2, IL2RA (CD25), IL2RB, and PTPN2; IL2 prevents type 1 diabetes in non-obese diabetic (NOD) mice;12 and a short course of IL2 at diabetes onset led to diabetes cure in a third of NOD mice.6 [...] The stimulation of regulatory T (T reg) cells is an appealing alternative immunotherapy for type 1 diabetes. T reg cells have a major role in the control of autoimmune diseases: people and mice with a genetically determined T reg-cell deficit develop several organ-specific autoimmune diseases, including type 1 diabetes;3, 4 a T reg-cell defect has been described in experimental and human autoimmune diseases, including type 1 diabetes;5 and addition or restoration of T reg cells induces clinical [...] 2019, Frontiers in Immunology  Show abstract Regulatory T cells (Tregs) are important for the induction and maintenance of peripheral tolerance therefore, they are key in preventing excessive immune responses and autoimmunity. In the last decades, several reports have been focussed on understanding the biology of Tregs and their mechanisms of action. Preclinical studies have demonstrated the ability of Tregs to delay/prevent graft rejection and to control autoimmune responses following adoptive [...] ### Nat Immunol (2010) 
