Content: The capacity for interleukin-2 (IL-2) to stimulate effector T cells and NK cells drove the development of high-dose IL-2 treatments as a cancer immunotherapy. However, the discovery of IL-2-dependent CD4+ regulatory T (Treg) cells has subsequently spurred efforts to use low-dose IL-2 treatment in autoimmune diseases.1,2,3,4 These apparently contrasting effects are mechanistically explained by the two main configurations of the signaling IL-2 receptor (IL-2R). A dimeric, intermediate-affinity [...] recent thymic emigrant (RTE) CD4+ Tconv cells (Figure 2B). This effect was less pronounced during the last treatment cycle of IL-2 immunotherapy (day 63 vs. day 68) (Figure 2C), which could be explained by progressive expansion of Treg cells suppressing the stimulation of these effector T cell populations at late time points or by epigenetic changes in Treg cells rendering them less responsive to IL-2 signals.28 [...] further supported by the low expression profile of activation markers on PD1 hi Treg cells, including CD25 and ICOS (Figure S4E). Cluster 4, containing CD45RA lo Treg cells, showed a marked reduction upon IL-2 treatment, which was likely due to their differentiation into the more activated Treg cell clusters 5 and 7. Highly differentiated Treg cells were found particularly in clusters 7 (HLA-DR+), 9 (CD38+), and 12 (CD38+ HLA-DR+), which vigorously expanded upon IL-2 immunotherapy. [...] and NK cells.3,7,8 Several clinical trials testing low-dose IL-2 in different autoimmune diseases have confirmed preferential expansion of Treg cells in peripheral blood; however, they did not provide an in-depth multimodal characterization of IL-2-induced phenotypical and functional changes of circulating and tissue Treg cells on a single-cell level, compared with other lymphocyte subsets.10,11,12,13,14,15,16 Furthermore, studies applied different dosing schemes, complicating comparability of [...] galectin-9), most of which are associated with suppression.44,45 Furthermore, the DP Treg cell subset was the most proliferative Treg cell subset before and during low-dose IL-2 immunotherapy, compared with CD38+ and HLA-DR+ Treg cells, while proliferation was minimal in CD45RA+ and DN Treg cells (Figure 4G). CD38+ Treg cells carried proteins associated with suppression, including CTLA-4 and LAG3 (Figure 4F).44 HLA-DR+ Treg cells expressed increased CD39 and a tendency to increased _TGFB1_,
