Content: The evolutionarily conserved histone variant H2A.Z has an important role in the regulation of gene expression and the establishment of a buffer to the spread of silent heterochromatin. _Saccharomyces cerevisiae_ Swr1, a Swi2/Snf2-related ATPase, is the catalytic core of a multisubunit chromatin remodelingenzyme, called the SWR1 complex, that efficiently replaces conventional histone H2A in nucleosomes with histone H2A.Z. Swr1 is required for the deposition of histone H2A.Z at stereotypical [...] The histone H2A.Z variant is a functionally distinct, highly conserved histone subgroup that likely represents a separate evolutionary lineage of histone H2A (Redon et al., 2002). The histone H2A.Z variant replaces the histone H2A in a small fraction of the nucleosomes in chromatin and is localized in transcriptionally active domains near telomeres and flanking the HMR mating-type locus (Hatch et al., 1983, Meneghini et al., 2003, Redon et al., 2002). The incorporation of histone Htz1, the [...] promoter locations _in vivo_, and Swr1 and H2A.Z commonly regulate a subset of yeast genes. Here, we describe an integrated nucleosome assembly–histone replacement system whereby histone exchange by chromatin remodeling activities may be analyzed _in vitro_. The system demonstrates ATP- and SWR1-complex-dependent replacement of histone H2A for histone H2A.Z on a preassembled nucleosome array. This system may also be adapted to analyze dynamic interactions between chromatin remodeling and [...] H2A.Z variant in yeast, requires _Saccharomyces cerevisiae_ Swr1, a member of the Swi2/Snf2 family of chromatin remodeling complexes (Kobor et al., 2004, Krogan et al., 2003, Mizuguchi et al., 2004). Swr1 is the catalytic subunit of the 14-component SWR1 complex, which evicts H2A–H2B dimers stepwise from a canonical nucleosome, replacing them with H2A.Z–H2B in an ATP-dependent fashion. Here, we present an integrated nucleosome assembly–histone exchange system whereby the functional consequences [...] Briefly, plasmid DNA immobilized with a paramagnetic bead at one end is first reconstituted in a nucleosomal array using bacterially expressed yeast octamers, histone chaperone Nap1, nucleosome spacing factor ISW1, and an ATP-regenerating system (Gelbart et al., 2001, Vary et al., 2004). After nucleosomal array assembly for 4 h, ATP and proteins not associated with the reconstituted nucleosome are removed by washing with a buffer containing 0.6 _M_ salt (Gelbart et al., 2001). The purified
