Title: Review The H2A.Z-nucleosome code in mammals: emerging functions
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0168952521002870
Content: It has been suggested that H2A.Z plays a critical role in RNAPII initiation and elongation; however, opposing results are also reported (Figure 3) [57,59., 60., 61., 62.]. After RNAPII enters early elongation, it pauses about 20–60 bp downstream from the TSS; this ‘pausing’ effect is increasingly recognised as an important step in regulating gene expression . The first (+1) nucleosome of the TSS is a major barrier for transcription _in vivo_ and it was suggested that H2A.Z incorporation at the [...] H2A.Z nucleosomes can increase or decrease nucleosome mobility and stability [20,22], depending on the nucleosomal context, including the presence of other histone variants, the level of replacement of H2A.Z in the entire nucleosome (Box 3), or companion histone PTMs. [...] study showed that H2A.Z-H2A nucleosomes were more stable than the homotypic H2A.Z nucleosomes, but slightly less stable than nucleosomes carrying canonical H2A . In line with this, it has been shown that H2A.Z homotypic nucleosomes are enriched downstream of TSSs in _Drosophila_, which is thought to decrease the energy barrier for RNA polymerase II (RNAPII) elongation, facilitating transcription . Thus, the exact effect of the heterotypic or homotypic H2A.Z nucleosomes on transcription remains [...] Nucleosome composition can impact nucleosome stability and, accordingly, functional properties. There is _in vitro_ and _in vivo_ evidence that H2A.Z can form heterotypic (one copy of H2A.Z and one copy of H2A) or homotypic (two copies of H2A.Z) nucleosomes [135,144]. _In vitro_ biochemical analyses show that H2A.Z homotypic nucleosomes are more stable than canonical H2A  and inhibit transcription , while H2A.Z-H2A nucleosomes are less stable and favour transcription. However, a more recent [...] robust study combining genomic techniques and live cell high-resolution microscopy supports that H2A.Z.1 slows RNAPII pause-release rate and is further supported by biophysical data that H2A.Z nucleosome widens the transcriptional barrier and lengthens the crossing of RNAPII compared with canonical nucleosomes .
