","Title: MDA5, A Nucleic Acid Sensor Critical for Immune Response
URL: https://bellbrooklabs.com/mda5-a-nucleic-acid-sensor-critical-for-immune-response/?srsltid=AfmBOoqo6KAeipTGHtuEOQixpNXCc-0DlhmgHY7K5nSU1Aq45fLwzRF4
Content: MDA5 is an RNA helicase that functions as a pattern recognition receptor (PRR) to recognize nucleic acids in the cytosol and initiate downstream inflammatory responses such as activating the type I interferon (IFN) signaling pathway and proinflammatory cytokines. Typically, MDA5 is present in low levels in resting cells, but upon activation by type 1 IFN, MDA5 expression is induced, creating a feed-forward loop in response to a virus infection1. [...] Melanoma differentiation-associated protein 5 (MDA5), also known as interferon-induced helicase-1 (IFH1), is one of the three members of the RIG-like receptors (RLRs) family of proteins. The RLR family of proteins share similar protein domains essential for their functions – namely the N-terminal caspase activation and recruit domains (CARDs), DExD/H box RNA helicase domain, and a C-terminal domain (CTD). [...] RLRs such as MDA5 form an essential component of the innate immune response. RLRs function as a PRR which detects pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs) such as foreign viral nucleic acids. This event serves as the first line of defense. Upon recognizing and binding double-stranded RNA (dsRNA), the CARD domain of MDA5 binds to signaling adaptor MAVS. This leads to the activation of key immune regulator NFkB, which initiates IFN signaling cascade2. Type I IFN signaling is essential in [...] Because interferon signaling is integral to human antiviral immunity, it is no surprise that alterations to MDA5 are associated with specific inflammation-related pathologies. In fact, severe viral respiratory infections were observed in children with loss-of-function mutations in MDA5. These mutations result in MDA5 deficiency, therefore reducing inflammatory responses towards viral infections, leading to the abnormal increase in susceptibility to common respiratory RNA viruses4. Also, others [...] ## Intracellular RNA Sensing and Interferon Signaling
