Content: Nonetheless, Cas9-mediated cleavage cannot perfectly recapitulate physiological DSBs. First, the repair of Cas9-induced breaks appears to be relatively slow and mutagenic . Next, Cas9 repeatedly cleaves the DNA until PAM or PAM-proximal sequence is disrupted, indicating, that the mainly observed repair outcomes will be mutagenic NHEJ or TMEJ. In addition, to make a DSB, Cas9 unwinds DNA and generates an RNA-DNA hybrid R-loop structure. Importantly, the R-loop made by dCas9 is shown to be [...] Cas9 generates predominantly blunt-ended DSBs and less frequently breaks with 1 nucleotide 5’-overhangs . Consistent with the notion that NHEJ is the most active repair pathway, Cas9-induced breaks are primarily repaired by NHEJ , , . However, multiple repair pathways can repair the Cas9-induced break at a single locus, albeit with different kinetics . Interestingly, mutagenic repair of Cas9-induced breaks was comparable in WT and LIG4 KO cells . Here, Polθ was responsible for the mutagenic [...] Specific histone marks associated with chromatin compaction also impact DSB repair pathway choice (Fig. 2b) . For instance, open chromatin marks, such as H3K36me3 and H3K4me2 are associated more with HR than with NHEJ  at A _sis_ I-induced breaks. Interestingly, the repair of Cas9-mediated breaks at the heterochromatin, marked by H3K27me3, H3K9me2, and lamina-associated domains (LADs), are skewed towards MMEJ, while breaks at the euchromatin, marked by H3K4me1, H3K4me2, and H3K27ac are [...] •DNA double-strand breaks are severe threats to genomic integrity. 
