Content: Neuronal degeneration in HD first becomes evident in the neostriatum, with the selective loss of medium spiny neurons (MSNs) and additional neuronal loss in the overlying cerebral cortex.1,2,3 Therapeutic strategies focused on neuronal rescue have proven challenging, whether based upon mutant huntingtin knockdown or neural tissue transplantation.4,5,6,7 Yet recent studies have also emphasized the role of glial pathology in HD and its potentially causal contributions to the disease.8,9,10,11,12,13,14,15 To better isolate the effects of glial pathology in HD, we previously established human-glia chimeras by neonatal engraftment of human fetal tissue-derived glial progenitor cells (hGPCs)—sorted on the basis of CD44 to enrich for astrocyte-biased progenitors—into the murine striatum.16 Using immunodeficient Rag1−/− x R6/2 hosts, we found that neonatal chimerization resulted in the effective replacement of the murine striatal glial progenitor pool by human donor-derived cells by 12 weeks of age.
