Content: Our umbrella review showed that a low level of vitamin D ( < 50 nmol/L) in pregnancy significantly increased the risk of low birth weight (<2500 g). However, this evidence was considered as low because the result was obtained from observational studies and had high heterogeneity (I2 > 50%). We did not find any association between low level of vitamin D and birth length. On the other hand, a high dose of vitamin D supplementation in pregnancy significantly increased birth length but not increase [...] For maternal outcomes, a low level of vitamin D ( < 50 nmol/L), significantly increased the risk of preterm birth (OR = 1.28; 95% CI = 1.08–1.52; number of studies = 21; Table 2A) , miscarriage (OR = 1.60; 95% CI = 1.11–2.30; number of studies = 6; Table 2A) , and small-for-gestational-age infants (OR = 1.43; 95% CI = 1.08–1.91; number of studies = 19; Table 2B) . Vitamin D deficiency also increased the risk of GDM (OR = 1.38; 95% CI = 1.22 – 1.57; number of studies = 31) , recurrent [...] supplementation during pregnancy did not affect the risk of preterm birth, this may be due to the fact that the participants in the RCTs did not all have vitamin D deficiency, and thus the effect of vitamin D supplementation on preterm birth was not significant. [...] During pregnancy, the body undergoes significant physiological change, including requirements and metabolism of vitamin D. Vitamin D deficiency in pregnancy is a common problem worldwide. The prevalence varies between 5% to 90% depending on the country [8,9,10]. Vitamin D deficiency has been linked with several adverse maternal and fetal outcomes [11,12,13]. For example, hypertensive disorders of pregnancy, gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), preterm birth, and low birthweight . [...] For birth length, a low level of vitamin D during pregnancy did not increase its risk. However, vitamin D supplementation during pregnancy significantly increased birth length.
