Title: Birthweight as a risk factor for breast cancer - ScienceDirect.com
URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0140673696031029
Content: [Can prenatal factors influence future breast cancer risk?](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0140673605661648 ""Can prenatal factors influence future breast cancer risk?"") The mammary gland is largely undifferentiated before birth and may be particularly susceptible to intrauterine influences that could increase the risk of cancer through acceleration of [cell proliferation](https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/biochemistry-genetics-and-molecular-biology/cell-proliferation ""Learn more about cell proliferation from ScienceDirect's AI-generated Topic Pages"") or other pregnancy-related processes. Birthweight is significantly associated with breast-cancer risk, which suggests that intrauterine factors or processes affect the risk of breast cancer in the [offspring](https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/child ""Learn more about offspring from ScienceDirect's AI-generated Topic Pages""). ### [Hypothesis: does breast cancer originate in utero?](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/014067369091000Z) ### [Prenatal factors may influence predisposition to breast cancer](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959804905800061) ### [The aetiology and pathogenesis of human breast cancer](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/002751079500128X) ### [The fetal antigen hypothesis for breast cancer, revisited](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0306987794900590) ### [Oestrogen fractions during early reproductive life in the aetiology of breast cancer](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0140673669915372)
