Content: exchange for glutamate export (6, 7). Intracellularly, cystine is reduced to its monomeric form, cysteine, which is important for several cellular processes, including the production of glutathione (GSH), a key intracellular antioxidant. Due to the oxidizing extracellular environment, cystine is more abundant in plasma (and in tissue culture medium) compared with cysteine (8, 9). Thus, tumor cells may rely on xCT to fulfill the majority of their cysteine and GSH needs by importing cystine. [...] these results indicate that murine T cells rely on the xCT-mediated uptake of cystine for proliferation, but not activation, in vitro. [...] Fig. 2. [...] Fig. 1. [...] Fig. 6.
